regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:39:6bec468	2026-08-21	panel_20260821_015232	8 fired: 7 scriptable + opus5 measuring seat; fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner directive), not a seat failure	7of7	SHIP-WITH-CHANGES, applied before this receipt. *** SEVEN MUTANTS ALL SCORED 29/29 AGAINST THE SHIPPED DETECTOR *** -- three guest-visible, one reinstating the original host kill verbatim: killw (`if (len == 2) exit(1);`), restinlatch (the restore braced INSIDE the latch, re-opening corruption on the second offence at all ten arms), nombrrestore (one restore deleted), bothvoid (both void PCICONF0 statements deleted -- LEAKS GUEST DATA), plus three void or equivalent mutants. *** AND THE ROUND HAD DISCLOSED ONE HOLE WITHOUT READING IT: its own kill table says 'the restores deleted -> 27/29, P3 and P3b, AND NOTHING ELSE', which is the statement that ONLY 2 OF THE 10 RESTORES ARE COVERED. I wrote that line as a strength. *** BOTH CAUSES WERE STRUCTURAL. The 274-access census was ONE-DIMENSIONAL -- an instrumented build reports widths seen: ['4'], so 137 offsets x 2 directions x ONE width, and len=2 was never issued anywhere in the file although a 16-bit access reaches the device. And the two void statements are MUTUALLY MASKING, so single-line mutation cannot see either while deleting both leaks the guest's word. DETECTOR 29 -> 38 ROWS and every mutant now dies: killw -> P14/P14b (len=2 census); restinlatch -> P17/P18 (second offence); nombrrestore -> P16/P17 (first offence parameterised over all 8 guarded arms); bothvoid -> P19 (-C SH7750 write-then-read); perofflatch -> P20. NO NEW ROW IS VACUOUS -- each of the nine kills something -- and restinlatch leaving P16 GREEN is the useful negative: the first offence really is still restored, so P17 is a distinct defect rather than a duplicate. Two extra controls came with it: noconf6restore proves P16 is parameterised rather than PCIMBR-specific, and killb_narrow is the DISCRIMINATING control for P15, since plain killb was already caught by the old file and so could not isolate the len=1 census. *** A REAL DEFECT IN THE SHIPPED CODE, AND MY FIRST FIX FOR IT WAS HALF A FIX. ***  The latch merged semantically DIFFERENT faults at one offset -- the exact hazard the code's own comment claimed to avoid, one level below #438's '1 of 4 kinds'. Measured twice: with -C SH7750 a write to PCICONF0 suppressed the later 'read for unimplemented CPU type' (which DOES print in a fresh process), and a byte-write at 0xfe200015 suppressed the later 'PCICONF5 unknown value', because PCIC_REG() maps both addresses to one word. I added TWO fault classes; the seat then measured that as HALF A FIX -- it closed the aliasing case and left PCICONF0 open, because BOTH of that register's complaints were BADVAL at the same word index and still shared one bit. A THIRD class fixes it, and the lesson outlives the fix: THE CLASSES ARE KINDS OF COMPLAINT, NOT KINDS OF REGISTER, and one register can raise several -- counting them from the register's point of view is what produced the half-fix. Severity throughout was diagnostic-only because the restore sits OUTSIDE the `if` and state stayed correct -- and THAT PLACEMENT IS LOAD-BEARING, which is exactly what restinlatch proves: bracing it inside is a brace slip of the kind a tidy-up produces. Gate 10's row floor 29 -> 38 in the same edit that grew the probe. It is a FLOOR, so it would have stayed green at 38 with no edit at all -- which is how a floor stops noticing. STILL OPEN AND DECLARED, filed as pcicmergeA: reproduction A (the PCICONF0 merge) has no row. It COULD NOT have had one at the time -- writing it against the two-class code would have CEMENTED THE DEFECT, asserting the merged behaviour as correct, so a later correct fix would have looked like a regression. The seat named that and declined rather than writing a row that passed. The third class now exists, so the row is three lines, and it is filed rather than written because it needs a probe run made AFTER that class landed. Also unmeasured: whether a real landisk boot ever issues a narrow-width PCIC access, so P14/P15 are rung-3 reachability rather than boot knowledge; and whether the guest driver ever commits a second offence at one register, so P17/P18 assert the contract rather than driver behaviour. Gates: gate 10 PASS (52), gate_offline PASS (263), gate_hygiene PASS (48), each run singly.
regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:41:bcdbfb8	2026-08-21	panel_20260821_164002	8 fired: 7of7 scriptable answered + opus5 measuring seat; fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner directive), not a seat failure	7of7	SHIP THE FIX, REPLACE THE DETECTOR -- and the detector was replaced before this receipt was filed. 8 seats fired, 7of7 scriptable answered (codex, agy, kimi 69KB, grok, glm, deepseek, minimax) plus the opus5 measuring seat; fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED per the owner's standing deprioritisation, recorded as a deliberate omission and never as agreement. SIX of the seven returned DEFECT-IN-DETECTOR; minimax said SOUND-with-the-detector-as-the-weaker-link, which is the same finding under a different label. *** THE MEASURING SEAT BUILT SIXTEEN MUTANTS AND ALL SIXTEEN SCORED 7/7. *** The champion is TWO CHARACTERS: `ETHERNET_STRING_MAXLEN` -> `0*ETHERNET_STRING_MAXLEN`. snprintf with size 0 writes NOTHING, not even the NUL, so the heap-buffer-overflow returns on ip12/ip28/ip30/ip35 -- ASan-measured back to the pre-fix numbers -- and it compiles with ZERO warnings while the gate row printed green. Also 7/7: size 1 (writes only the NUL -> EMPTY MAC, ASan silent), sizeof(eaddr_string) (=8 -> "08:20:3"), `#if 0`, a comment wrapper, `if (0)`, `if (subtype == 32)`, a second malloc, an arm re-pointing the pointer, and `#define snprintf(b,l,...) ((void)(b))`. *** AND THE REDUCTIO THAT SETTLES IT: A 217-BYTE FILE CONTAINING NOTHING BUT A C COMMENT SCORED 7/7. *** No function, no MACHINE_SETUP, nothing compilable. That is VACUOUS under this project's taxonomy, not weak -- and no additional regex repairs it, because the failure is the MEDIUM and not the rows. THE OLD FILE'S OWN STATED PURPOSE WAS THE THING IT DID NOT DO. Its docstring called itself "the LENGTH/FORMAT oracle" that sees what ASan cannot. IT NEVER EXAMINED THE LENGTH ARGUMENT. The size-1 mutant is precisely the `eaddr_string[0]='\0'` hole it named as its reason for existing, spelled with an snprintf, and it passed. Three tracked copies of that claim (probe docstring, CHANGELOG, gate comment) were corrected under the grep-for-siblings rule. REPLACED WITH A RUNTIME VALUE ORACLE, 10 rows: break on arcbios_init() in a real construction of the five reaching subtypes and require the ethernet STRING to be the formatting of the MAC BYTES passed beside it -- a provenance check, so garbage, empty, truncated and right-shaped-wrong-octet all fail. MEASURED kill table (PASSING rows out of 10; clean = 10/10): `0*` 5/10, size-1 5/10, sizeof 5/10, `#if 0` 5/10, comment 5/10, pre-fix 5/10, wrong-last-octet 6/10. Every mutant that beat the old file dies. *** V4 KILLED NOTHING IN THAT TABLE AND THAT WAS CORRECT, SO A DISCRIMINATING MUTANT WAS BUILT FOR IT. *** ip32 has its own snprintf in its arm and is rescued from every mutation of the pre-switch fill. An untested row is indistinguishable from a vacuous one, so both fills were removed: 4/10, V4 fires. No row now goes unexercised. GATE MOVED, AND THE MOVE IS THE POINT: the old detector lived in gate_offline because source text needs no binary. The replacement needs build/gxemul and gdb, and gate_offline RUNS NO EMULATOR AT ALL (zero references to the binary) -- adding one would have made the gate's own name untrue. It went to gate 9, which already constructs every machine and subtype: ASan is that gate's instrument for the memory-safety class, this probe is its instrument for the VALUE class ASan cannot see. check_probe_wiring.py followed the move with no edit, because it DERIVES wiring from run.sh's GATES array rather than asserting it. Nothing was left behind in gate_offline: a stub row asserting the probe merely EXISTS cannot fail for the reason it names. degrade() not gate_skip() on a missing gdb -- gate_skip EXITS and would have discarded the sweep's own eleven checks and reported the whole gate as SKIP. THREE WRONG RECORDS FOUND IN MY OWN MATERIAL, all corrected. (1) The `:633` citation had drifted to :661 -- the read-the-line-before-citing-it rule. (2) The added source comment said "machine.c forces machine->bootarg non-NULL"; machine.c:559 is a DEBUG PRINT whose local has_bootarg only selects which message is emitted, and bootarg is not on this path at all. The CONCLUSION (only defective caller) survives on the two-caller grep, which agy and grok both performed independently; the argument for it did not. (3) "callee-side hardening is an IMPOSSIBILITY" overreached: impossible AT THAT SIGNATURE, not categorically. *** THE UBSAN FOLLOW-UP UNDERCOUNTED ITSELF BY FIVE TIMES. *** It was filed as "two left-shift defects at arcbios.c:737/784". The measuring seat found ip32 never reaches those lines -- its two hits are :570 and :590 -- making it four sites. A grep for the idiom then found TEN unguarded `(buf[3]<<24)` sites and FOUR correctly cast `((uint32_t)buf[3]<<24)`. Line 580 sits BETWEEN 570 and 590 and is the only one of the three that is cast: the author knew the idiom and applied it to 4 of 14 sites. :737 is the damaging one -- the sign-extended low word is ADDED to high-word terms, so the 64-bit pointer's high word comes out off by one for every address with bit 31 set. A SEAT'S FACTUAL CHALLENGE WAS REFUTED BY EXECUTION WHILE ITS MECHANISM CLAIM WAS SIMULTANEOUSLY RIGHT. grok read the arms and reported ip27 has no abort(), so "five call abort()" overstates. MEASURED over all ten advertised subtypes: ip19/20/22/24/27 return 134 and ip12/28/30/32/35 return 1 -- the five ARE the five. But ip22 has no body and FALLS THROUGH into case 24, and ip27 dies under gdb at interrupt_handler_lookup() <- devinit_z8530() <- device_add(), on its own arm's irq=0 -- which is exactly what grok predicted. A token search of a switch cannot see fall-through, and reading for abort() misses an abort raised by a callee; my own case-splitter had the identical blind spot. kimi audited the kill-table ARITHMETIC rather than the code and challenged "four-octet MAC 6/7 (E5 alone)" as inconsistent. MEASURED: format-only mutant 6/7 with E5 the sole failure; format-AND-arguments mutant 5/7 with E5 and E6. Both numbers right, different mutants -- and the real defect it exposed is that "(E5 alone)" never says whether E5 alone FIRED or alone SURVIVED, and the two readings are opposite. A kill-table cell must name the direction. STILL OPEN AND DECLARED: the ip32 arm's own snprintf is now redundant; kept, because deleting it drops the buffer to a single write site and costs nothing to leave. Nothing here claims anything about the bytes the GUEST reads back -- that needs a rung-3 probe on a machine that gets that far, and there is no SGI rig. The probe reads arguments from SysV AMD64 registers because build/gxemul has no DWARF; that ties it to one ABI, stated in the file rather than hidden, with V6/V7 as the rows that would catch a wrong register rather than let it read as zero. Gates: gate_offline PASS (263), gate_asan_sweep PASS (13, was 11), each run singly.
regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:44:f437411	2026-08-21	panel_20260821_211546	8 fired: 7of7 scriptable answered + opus5 measuring seat; fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner directive), not a seat failure	7of7	SHIP, with two wording corrections applied before this receipt. 8 seats fired, 7of7 scriptable answered (codex 13.7KB, agy 11.3KB, kimi 62KB, grok 7.1KB, glm 5.0KB, deepseek 2.4KB, minimax 4.5KB); fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED per the owner's standing deprioritisation, recorded as a deliberate omission and never as agreement. Verdicts: 5 SOUND, 2 DEFECT. *** THE TWO DEFECT VERDICTS WERE ABOUT MY BRIEF, NOT THE SOURCE, AND THEY WERE RIGHT TO REFUSE IT. ***  The brief inlined its diff through `grep -vE '^\+\s*\*'` to save space, which strips comment CONTINUATION lines while keeping the `/*` that opens them, so the shown patch could not compile. codex and kimi INDEPENDENTLY traced C comment semantics through translation phase 3 to the unmatched brace; grok noticed the hunk header line counts did not match the displayed text, declined to treat the snippet as complete, and read the repository instead. THREE SEATS, THREE ROUTES, ONE DEFECT. codex's conclusion is the one to keep: 'the reported clean measurements must come from different source text.' That is the correct response to a packet claiming measurements against source a reader can see will not build. The shipped file compiles with zero warnings and both gates are green -- but the seats could not know that, and should not have assumed it. *** DO NOT FILTER A DIFF TO SAVE SPACE: trim by HUNK SELECTION, never by line filter. A corrupted diff makes every measurement claim in the packet unverifiable. ***  The next round's brief was written unfiltered and said so at the top. THE SUBSTANTIVE FINDING MEASURED EQUIVALENT. codex named the smallest undetected mutation -- swap the latch class SH4_VAL_RTCSRINT -> SH4_VAL_RCR1INT -- and CONDITIONED it correctly on 'an earlier RCR1 complaint with an OVERLAPPING BIT'. MEASURED: 10/10, but the bit sets are DISJOINT (RCR1 CIE|AIE = 0x18, RTCSR CMIE|OVIE = 0x42) and sh4_val_first keys on BITS, so no suppression is possible. The mutant is EQUIVALENT, not surviving -- a different diagnosis from 'the detector is weak', and its stated condition is simply not met. Filed as sh4latchcollide, where a later flagship review REJECTED the obvious disjointness assertion on three measured grounds and named a C99 enum as the real structural answer. TWO WORDINGS CORRECTED, both more specific than the code earns: 'the interrupt is NOT delivered' implied something becomes pending and is dropped, when nothing ever becomes pending (CMF/OVF are never set on any path and RTCNT has no `case`) -- now 'refresh-interrupt generation is not implemented'; and '(once per bit)' is not what the latch does, because one store setting BOTH enables yields ONE coalesced line, which is exactly what row R3 measures. THREE ROWS WERE MEASURED VACUOUS DURING THE ROUND AND REBUILT: A1 was written against RTCSR_CMF -- write-1-to-keep, and nothing in the device ever sets it, so a guest cannot put that bit in the predicate's operand and the row could never fire; L1 was FLAKY with one spin (it passed once and failed once on the SAME binary) and now has two; and the guard-only widening mutant is EQUIVALENT because the latch argument still masks to the original bits. A2 is an UNEXERCISED CONTROL and is recorded as one rather than claimed as coverage. *** A1's FAILURE BECAME A CARRIER RULE. ***  A later flagship adjudication adopted ACCEPT-ROW FAILABILITY from it: an accept-side row is discharged by a MEASURED KILL against the smallest realistic JOINT guard-growth mutant, not by existing. 'The guest can produce the value' was measured to be one level too shallow -- the guest DID write CMF=1 at the interface and the store arm filtered it before the predicate saw it. TWO DETECTORS COVER DIFFERENT HALVES. The rung-3 probe proves the HOST lives; the offline row `rtcsr_falls_through` in diff_sh4_tmu.c proves the TIMERS live with it, and is what catches an early `return` -- the obvious fix and the wrong one. MEASURED there: clean 0 failures, early-return 1 failure, exit(1) reinstated kills the differential outright. A pass-1 seat proposed that row AND caught that the file's own comment had become false. Gates: gate_sh_rounding PASS (58 checks, was 56), gate_offline PASS (263), each run singly.
regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:118:67120fd	2026-08-30	panel_20260830_105834 (pass 2) + opus5/fable5 Agent seats	#495 wdc: a host store during an ATAPI DATA-IN transfer was pushed into the ring	9of9	*** ALL NINE ANSWERED, AND THE PANEL FOUND TWO CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACHES THAT WERE REPAIRED IN-ROUND. ***  Seven scriptable seats plus opus5 and fable5 via the Agent tool.  Nothing was down.  *** THIS RECEIPT IS LATE AND SAYS SO. ***  The panel ran on 2026-08-30 and its nine seat entries were written into the ledger row `wdcaddtoinbufbound`; the RECEIPT LINE was never appended, so `precommit_check.sh` correctly blocked the next commit until it was.  The review happened; the record of it did not.  Those are different failures and only the second one occurred here -- which is exactly why the check reads the tsv rather than the ledger.  DIFF DISCLOSURE (36 files, `git show --numstat 67120fd`): `src/devices/dev_wdc.c` +73/-1 INLINED verbatim, the guard and its full enclosing switch; `regress/wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py` +577 SUMMARIZED -- the promoted rung-3 witness, its row table and arms described and its oracles quoted, not pasted whole; the 26 `killtable_20260830/` artifacts NOT SHOWN individually, but TABLE.txt's scores were inlined and the seats were pointed at the directory; `tools/propagate_all_trees.sh` +105 NOT SHOWN -- a build-tree helper with no bearing on the fix; the three `wdc_altoffset_*` probes +12/-1 each NOT SHOWN, the identical parked-check repair described in prose; CHANGELOG/OUTSTANDING_BUGS/ledger/matrix NOT SHOWN as records.  E1 `&& len == 2` -- the MIRROR of the round's own `len == 1` mutant.  Both injections were 2-byte stores, so one character swept all 22 rows: the table had measured the inert member of a family and concluded the family was covered.  Predicted from the packet ALONE by glm before anyone built it, then measured escaping at 22/22.  Closed by ONE INSTRUCTION -- the second injection is now a single-byte store, so no single width EQUALITY qualifier can refuse both.  E3/M5 `== PHASE_DATAIN` -- every row injected during DATA-IN, where the narrowed guard still refuses, so a phase-narrowing edit swept clean.  Named by deepseek, which said PLAINLY it could not check its own claim from the packet; it was checkable here and it was right.  That honesty is the useful part.  Repaired by a PHASE_COMPLETED arm where `status_byte()`'s DRQ reflects ring occupancy alone.  *** AND THE DEEPEST FINDING WAS ABOUT THE INSTRUMENTS, NOT THE FIX. ***  opus5 found that FOUR COMMITTED DETECTORS reported `parked: yes` for a guest that never parked -- each tested `if "BREAKPOINT" in tail.upper()` and matched its own echoed `breakpoint add` command, so the check could never return False.  It then found the SCORER cannot tell a kill from a non-measurement: three runs with `words 0 of 46`, pc at HALT and 0.00 s elapsed, on BOTH legs including the unmutated base, were scored `KILLED (20 red)`; re-run quietly, two ESCAPED.  **Every one carried the completion sentinel**, which proves the probe finished WRITING and says nothing about whether it MEASURED.  A false kill is worse than a false escape: it certifies coverage never taken.  Fixed by refusing to score when the BASE leg's controls fail -- the discriminator is the base leg, NOT a threshold, because M7's mutant leg legitimately kills the guest.  fable5 caught a records defect committed INSIDE this round: `shipped.diff` was rewritten IN PLACE after the table consumed it, so the artifacts dir cited a run whose input existed nowhere durable.  It named the staged blob and the timestamps; both versions now sit in the directory with PROVENANCE.txt, and the comment-only claim was CHECKED (three code lines each, identical) rather than asserted.  It also found A3: the #477 dependency ALREADY has a defender, so no row was owed where one was about to be built.  TWO ESCAPES FILED rather than fixed, and the distinction is the DEPENDS-ON bound: `wdcatapistswap` (the flag operand swapped for `atapi_st != NULL`, measured to refuse every ordinary data-port write for the life of the device) and `wdcprobeendian` (every rung-3 probe here is little-endian only).  Neither is reachable by clause (d)'s enumeration, which is why they file.  DISCLOSED: the probe is dated-exempt from gate wiring until 2026-09-20, so THE BATTERY DEFENDS NONE OF THIS TODAY -- the kill table is the evidence, not the gate.
regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:122:821eb83	2026-08-30	panel_20260830_214531 (pass 2 on the shipped diff) + opus5/fable5 Agent seats	#497 widthkillclass: six guest-reachable width kills become latched declines; detector 35/35 against the shipped binary; floor (a)-(f) discharged by measurement	8of9 AT TIME OF WRITING -- SNAPSHOT, NOT A TALLY (opus5 still in flight; this project has recorded three seat-counts stated as fact while seats were still returning, all wrong)	*** THE PANEL FOUND THREE DEFECTS IN THE BRIEF AND TWO WRONG RECORDS, ONE STAGED AND ABOUT TO SHIP. ***  Packet-fed seats, from the packet alone: (1) premise 1 gave the PRE-FIX census 21/13 as CURRENT state when the shipped file reads 15/7 -- a stale number in a SETTLED PREMISES block, the one field seats are told not to re-derive; (2) C0 said the round had NOT shipped while the header called the diff shipped; (3) C0's third command was a NULL PATTERN, grep -i 'sgi|width' without -E being a literal -- right answer by luck.  fable5: F4, the gate comment claimed 'Filed, not done' about a row that DID NOT EXIST, now filed as gatedevicesplit with a forcing criterion; F5, sgirekills' witness still said no rung-3 probe existed, false since the six ld kill rows shipped -- and those rows mean fixing it will turn a GREEN GATE RED by succeeding, so it inherits n6's oracle-correction ordering.  The ask-again was SPENT, returning F1 (the one-character |= -> =, passing all 35 because every multi-site session uses one traffic pattern) and F2 (announce-and-perform, re-opening the LINEAR OOB with the host alive), both FILED as coverage debt with the reading stated.  My scope warrant was OVERCLAIMED and corrected by the seat that made the ruling, including a circularity it owed plainly: the shared-bitfield hazard exists BECAUSE the one-round fix chose six bits in one word.  And the generalisation worth more than any single defect: A CENSUS INLINED INTO A SETTLED-PREMISES BLOCK IS A FROZEN MEASUREMENT WEARING THE CLOTHES OF A GIVEN.
