regress\artifacts\pcc2c040kill\grok_witness_20260907.txt:1:I'll attack the proposed one-byte witness rather than ratify it: first the site, GENCTL write path, existing probe, and every other way the process can die.Premises look plausible; next I’ll read the helper, every call site, the GENCTL arm, and the existing probe’s survival/oracle mechanics.S3’s tick claim may be sequencing-wrong; I’ll read how the probe classifies death, whether `fatal()` is flushed, and whether C040 can be set without calling `reassert`.**VERDICT: NOT RATIFY as proposed.** A guest `st.b` of `0x04` at GENCTL is a sound *core* of a rung-3 witness of this kill. Survival-only, in `pcc2_width_probe.py`, with no same-decode preset and no site-attribution, is not. That is the same class as the design you asked me to assume: the observation is either destroyed by the defect, or it reports success while measuring nothing.
regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\grok_witness_20260908.txt:1:I'll read the Alpha PAL, dyntrans, machine, and existing probe sources first so the witness design is grounded in how this tree actually runs, not in the previous pass's claims.The defect site and the two Alpha probes are the next reads; I’ll also pull dyntrans invalidation and how alphabook1 maps memory so the witness can actually cache a VPN.PAL dispatch is confirmed; next I’ll read how VADDR vs ALL invalidation actually matches entries, and how Alpha load/store fills and consumes the dyntrans TLB.A rung-3 guest witness **is constructible** on unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1`. The observable is a **stale dyntrans hit after a PTE remap**, not a dispatch spy. Section 1 is right on the defect; two extra constraints decide whether the row is real or vacuous.
