regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxreg\pass2_panel_20260908\grok.txt:1:I'll inspect the `#514` product, probe, and artifacts in `src/` first, then check the premises those six seats treated as given.Pass-1 panel notes and the actual palcode handler are next, so I can test the floor claim against the tree rather than the write-up.I'll read the pass-1 briefs, witness artifacts, and Alpha PAL docs next, then check whether the wrapper and census claims actually match the tree.Pass-1 seats and the architecture sources are next; I need to see which census and wrapper claims actually got corrected.I'll check the vacuity-floor rule, the `#507` probe, `alphatbinegsel` coupling, and whether `print` can accidentally yield `C`.**VERDICT: The one-identifier product is correct and sufficient. The witness is a real rung-3 red-then-green. The round is not complete as argued: clause (d) was misnamed, `a0 land` pins a value the architecture does not require, and the probe is blind to a hardcoded `v0 = 0x01000000`. None of that is an L2 floor breach. Do not owe a built `v0 = a0` mutant. Do not treat the `alphatbinegsel` re-baseline as a `#514` flush debt — this hunk did not touch the invalidate.**
regress\artifacts\pass2_7a41b3f\seats\ollama_glm.txt:48:Now, the key internal-consistency check on #505: the round is named "roundtrip" and section 1 says "an RGB pixel did not survive a round trip." The comment itself concedes the fixed GET does NOT return the source word (0xaabbccdd → 0xaabbcc00). So the round-trip claim in section 1 is, strictly, still false after the fix unless the pixel's low byte is zero or unless "RGB pixel" means a 24-bit quantity. The comment addresses this ("The low byte stays ZERO on purpose"). So the section-1 one-liner overstates: it says the defect was "an RGB pixel did not survive a round trip" and the fix repaired that; but the fix makes the round trip preserve only 24 bits. Is that a "partial repair presented as complete"? The comment discloses it, so it's not hidden — but the witness: "Each shipped with a rung-3 witness measured RED pre-fix and GREEN post-fix with the same script." If the witness script PUTs a color like 0x11223344 and GETs and compares to... 0x11223300? Then GREEN post-fix is consistent. If it compares to 0x11223344, it would still be RED. So the witness presumably compares against the 24-bit-truncated value, or uses a color with zero low byte. Either way, the packet can't tell me. But there's a real question: what SHOULD the GET return — 0xaabbcc00 (comment's claim) or 0xaabbccdd (full word)? That depends on whether the register's low byte is meaningful for RGB mode, which is a hardware-semantics question not settleable from the packet. The stated warrant (scanout ignores byte 3; RGBA/ABGR PUT arms lay R,G,B,x) supports the memory layout, not the register semantics. Actually — hmm, wait. If PUT lays [R,G,B,x] where x is... what? If PUT writes the register's low byte at buf[3], then GET reading buf[3] into the low byte would round-trip exactly and would match the RGBA arm's form. The comment explicitly says the pad is at buf[3] "because the consumer ignores buf[3], not because PUT writes a zero there" — this strongly suggests PUT writes the register's byte 3 (low byte) at buf[3], i.e., PUT does `buf[3] = color & 0xff` (or the word's fourth byte). If so, then a GET of `(buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8)+buf[3]` would return the EXACT source word (assuming channels map to top three bytes), and the round trip would be perfect. The chosen fix instead DROPS the low byte. So the fix is a partial round trip by choice, justified by "RGB carries three channels."
regress\artifacts\pass2_7a41b3f\seats\ollama_glm.txt:619:Now — the TODO question (Q4). The new code keeps `// TODO.` and `return 2`. The comment says "The TODO below STAYS: `flags' is still ignored, the protection bits untested, and `return 2' still reports every walked page writable." So the disclosure IS in the shipped comment. Question: was keeping the TODO right, or does the round owe a disclosure it did not make? From the packet: the round DID make a disclosure — in the code comment (which is part of the shipped diff). Does the WITNESS/witness row disclose it? "Each shipped with a rung-3 witness measured RED pre-fix and GREEN post-fix" — the witness for #508 presumably tests offset preservation; nothing in section 1 about protection bits. The round's scope: it fixed the offset drop; it did NOT claim to fix protection. Keeping TODO right? I'd say: keeping the TODO is right (the protection behavior is unchanged; removing it would falsely signal completion), and the comment explicitly discloses what return 2 still means. What the round arguably owes beyond the comment: nothing in the packet shows the ledger/witness notes disclosing that walked pages remain reported writable — but the brief says the pre-commit gate handles records; I can only judge the packet. The comment's disclosure is real and prominent. So answer: keeping it was right; the disclosure WAS made (in the shipped comment); if the witness row claims "protection verified" that would overclaim, but no such claim appears in the packet — so no undisclosed debt visible in-packet. However — subtle: "return 2" — what does return 2 mean to memory_rw's caller? The comment says it "still reports every walked page writable." If return code 2 means "page writable, no protection check" — that's a pre-existing lie to the guest (a read-only page would be treated writable). That's a real defect but explicitly out of scope and disclosed. Fine.
regress\artifacts\pass2_7a41b3f\seats\ollama_glm.txt:680:- #508 (pfn offset): the fix form is standard and self-consistent; measured witness; low risk IF the walk is only used in the emulated Alpha OS path... behavior change: every walked access now lands at correct offset — this CHANGES behavior for all guests using the software walk. Risk: if memory_rw's offset_mask premise is wrong (e.g., memory_rw does NOT derive offset from paddr), the fix could produce DOUBLE-offset (paddr already includes offset, and memory_rw adds offset again?). Wait — the comment says memory_rw takes offset = paddr & offset_mask. If instead memory_rw ADDS a separately-computed offset to paddr... The comment's premise is specific. If wrong, the fix yields base+offset and memory_rw adds offset again → base+2*offset — worse. The settling fact: memory_rw's offset computation. Given the author claims a measured witness (ldq vaddr+8 returns vaddr+0 value pre-fix; presumably vaddr+8 value post-fix), the premise is evidenced by measurement — which I can't re-verify but which is a rung-3 claim. I'd ship it, noting the dependency.
regress\artifacts\pass2_7a41b3f\seats\ollama_glm.txt:684:Hmm, but wait — the brief says each shipped with a rung-3 witness RED/GREEN. For #507, a witness that checks flags propagation would be GREEN post-fix. If the fix had REMOVED ptbr loading (premise false), would the witness catch it? Only if the witness also checks ptbr. "Three of the four gained an extra row because a fix review refused the round — the witness could not distinguish the fix from a lesser edit" — if #507's witness originally only checked flags, a "lesser edit" like `apcb_flags = 0;` or assigning flags from something else could also go GREEN... The extra row presumably strengthens. Can't know. So my objection to #507 stands at the packet level: the packet cannot establish that an earlier `apcb_ptbr = new_ptbr` exists, and the comment's locator for it is arithmetically inconsistent with the inlined context. Name the settling fact.
regress\artifacts\pass2_7a41b3f\seats\ollama_glm.txt:698:Also the brief's own framing: "5 hunks, 63 changed lines, checked before this brief was sent" — enumerated 62 (57+/5−), with every hunk header self-consistent. Off by one. And "Each shipped with a rung-3 witness measured RED pre-fix and GREEN post-fix with the same script" — packet asserts; unverifiable; consistent with claims.
regress\artifacts\pass2_7a41b3f\seats\ollama_glm.txt:907:Also PRIx64 requires <inttypes.h> — is it included in dev_lca.c? The packet doesn't show includes. If PRIx64 isn't available, compilation fails (which would be caught by build, presumably — the witnesses ran, so it compiled). Since the witness ran GREEN post-fix, compilation evidently succeeded (REASONED: the brief says witnesses measured GREEN post-fix with the same script; a non-compiling tree can't go green — assuming the witness builds the tree). Actually, careful: does the witness build the whole tree or just exercise the device? A rung-3 witness "measured RED pre-fix and GREEN post-fix with the same script" implies the tree was built both times. So PRIx64 is available. OK, I can note this is settled indirectly.
regress\artifacts\pass2_7a41b3f\seats\ollama_glm.txt:1473:Yes — "// TODO." and "return 2;" are trailing context. The comment block explicitly discloses: "The TODO below STAYS: `flags' is still ignored, the protection bits untested, and `return 2' still reports every walked page writable." So the round DID make the disclosure — in the code comment. Q4 asks "does the round owe a disclosure it did not make?" — the comment in the diff IS a disclosure. Whether the round's ledger/witness notes disclosed it is outside the packet (the witnesses aren't inlined! The brief says each round "shipped with a rung-3 witness measured RED pre-fix and GREEN post-fix" but does NOT inline the witnesses). Hmm — important: the witnesses are NOT in the packet. Section 3 mentions "five negate=True rows across three of this commit's own witnesses" — so witnesses have rows/notes, none inlined. So I cannot check witness claims at all. Flag that.
regress\artifacts\pass2_7a41b3f\seats\ollama_glm.txt:1525:Q4 (TODO): The comment in the shipped code IS the disclosure — it states flags ignored, protection untested, return 2 = always-writable. So the code-level disclosure was made. Whether the ROUND (witness/ledger) owed more: the witness for #508 — a rung-3 witness RED/GREEN on offset behavior — would pass regardless of protection semantics, so the round's scope (offset only) is disclosed. Keeping the TODO is right (the protection work is a different round; removing the TODO would falsely signal completion). One caveat: the comment says "the protection bits untested" — with `return 2` meaning writable-unconditionally, any guest relying on read-only pages (e.g., write-protecting a shared page) is broken; that's a live defect the round correctly left flagged. Does the round owe MORE disclosure? From the packet: the disclosure exists and is unusually explicit. I'd say keeping it was right, and the disclosure was made — in the comment. If the panel's convention is that disclosures belong in the witness notes (not inlined), I can't check. Also note: `return 2` semantics (what callers do with 2) is outside the packet — "still reports every walked page writable" is the comment's claim about the CALLER's interpretation of 2; uncheckable. So Q4: right call, disclosure present in-packet at code level; the one uncheckable premise is what return-code 2 means to the caller (name it).
