regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:3816:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-3936-     "note": "FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION, queue item 7, batched. Badge `s` disclosed: same-model review of the main loop's records, not a two-family check. Re-verified every code claim by grep/read; boots and mutant builds attributed to the measure seat rather than re-run.\n* *** THE FINDING IS A FLAW IN THE LADDER IT WROTE THIS MORNING: THE RUNG ORDER AND THE PRIORITY ORDER DIVERGE. *** b120r's witness is rung 4 (an instrumented boot) while armbdt's is rung 3 (a probe), so the ladder's clause 'order items by the strongest witness that exists TODAY' puts b120r FIRST. That is wrong for scheduling: b120r's measured guest-visible symptom is NIL (told-98 and told-35 give byte-identical transcripts) while armbdt CORRUPTS GUEST DATA. RUNG MEASURES REACHABILITY-PROOF STRENGTH, NOT HARM. The carrier clause is corrected.\nCONFIRM: TRUE as code, DEAD as defect. Drop as a round, rider on the next diskimage round. READ-verified exactly TWO call sites, emul_parse.c:558 and main.c:707, both branching on < 0 and both aborting. Nothing consumes a half-built entry, so no execution witness exists OR IS NEEDED -- rung-none by construction. Re-promote only if a third, non-aborting caller appears."
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:3834:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-4292-     "note": "FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION, queue item 7, batched. Badge `s` disclosed: same-model review of the main loop's records, not a two-family check. Re-verified every code claim by grep/read; boots and mutant builds attributed to the measure seat rather than re-run.\n* *** THE FINDING IS A FLAW IN THE LADDER IT WROTE THIS MORNING: THE RUNG ORDER AND THE PRIORITY ORDER DIVERGE. *** b120r's witness is rung 4 (an instrumented boot) while armbdt's is rung 3 (a probe), so the ladder's clause 'order items by the strongest witness that exists TODAY' puts b120r FIRST. That is wrong for scheduling: b120r's measured guest-visible symptom is NIL (told-98 and told-35 give byte-identical transcripts) while armbdt CORRUPTS GUEST DATA. RUNG MEASURES REACHABILITY-PROOF STRENGTH, NOT HARM. The carrier clause is corrected.\nFLAGSHIP ASSESS, retroactive: REAL and worth the round it got. READ-verified diskimage_scsicmd.c:915-933 -- the pre-fix inline fsync(fileno(d->f)) with no fflush synced only the base handle, and under R: (which both rigs use) fwrite_helper writes ONLY to the overlay, so the command synced the one file with no new data while skipping stdio entirely. The host-death probe (512->0, 4096->0, 8192->4096) and the luna88k boot issuing 0x35 exactly at MARKING FILE SYSTEM CLEAN make this rung 4, correctly promoted. Shipped as #437."
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:3899:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:5295:   "note": "  *** THE DESIGN IS DONE, THE TITLE'S COUNT IS STALE, AND AN ACCEPT ROUND WOULD RED THE GATE UNLESS ONE PROBE ROW MOVES FIRST.  codex seat, 2026-08-30, farmed read-only while agy was quota-walled; every load-bearing claim re-checked here. ***  **THE COUNT: THREE NUMBERS ARE IN CIRCULATION AND ALL THREE ARE RIGHT ABOUT DIFFERENT THINGS.**  A comment-aware census of `dev_footbridge.c` finds **10** live `exit(` calls.  **TWO of them are in `DEVINIT(footbridge)`** -- `footbridge unimpl machine type` and `footbridge: unimplemented machine type.` -- which are CONSTRUCTION-TIME and not guest-reachable, because no guest chooses its own machine type.  That leaves **EIGHT guest-access kills**, and this row's title says NINE.  The ninth was the divide-by-zero-on-a-guest-written-timer-encoding fixed by `#432` (`35302f2`), verified by `git log -S`.  **L4: the title is stale and is left unedited on purpose -- titles are identity strings other records grep -- but no future round may quote the nine.**  **TWO DISPOSITIONS, THREE ROUND-GROUPS.**  Not one round, and the split is by semantic class rather than by convenience:  (1) **ACCEPT, one site**: `read from ENABLE SET?` at `+0x188`.  Table 7-10 (`dc21285.txt:6821`) and the IRQEnable prose (`:6911`) make it a documented READ whose value can only be changed via EnableSet/EnableClear -- and **the model already computes `d->irq_enable` and then discards it by dying**.  So: delete the kill, KEEP the value, no latch and no message, because there is no event to announce.  This is the FOURTH option-set member, and a bounded-announce here would ship a wrong record in product code.  (2) **DECLINE + BOUNDED ANNOUNCE, five sites, one class**: the invalid-DIRECTION interrupt accesses -- WRITE to IRQStatus `+0x180` and FIQStatus `+0x280` (read-only, `:6886`), WRITE to IRQRawStatus `+0x184` and FIQRawStatus `+0x284` (read-only, `:6901`), and READ from ENABLE CLEAR `+0x18c` (write-only).  **`+0x18c` carries an extra obligation the others do not: the decline must NOT expose the currently assigned `d->irq_enable`**, which is the value a naive 'just return the field' fix would leak.  (3) **DECLINE + an ACCURATE announce, two sites, a DIFFERENT rationale**: the SDRAM Base Address Mask `+0x100` (`:5844`) and Offset `+0x104` (`:5897`) reads.  These registers are R/W and legal -- **the model simply does not implement them** -- so the message must say `unimplemented read`, NOT `invalid access`.  Getting that wording wrong is the same wrong-record defect as (1), one step milder.  *** THE OPERATIONAL TRAP, AND IT IS THE SHARPEST THING IN THIS FINDING: `gate_arm.sh` REQUIRES THE `+0x188` KILL TO KEEP HAPPENING. ***  Its row is `check \"footbridge: the one-instruction kill arm still dies\" ... '^K1 .*ok$' ... 1`, and `K1` is precisely the ENABLE-SET read the ACCEPT round would fix.  **So the ACCEPT round turns a GREEN GATE RED by succeeding**, and the fix is to REPOINT K1 at `+0x18c` in the same commit.  A round that does not know this reads the red as a regression in its own work.  Verified by opening the gate.  **AND THE PROBE'S FAILABILITY LEG RESTS ON A PRODUCT DEFECT**, which is fine until it is the LAST one: `footbridge_sites_probe.py` reaches exactly one fatal site (K1) and uses its death as the proof the guest code got that far.  Once the final live kill is repaired there is no product defect left to point at, and the leg must become a MUTATION/failability leg instead.  That is a real cost of finishing this cluster and it should be planned, not discovered.  DETECTOR DEBT the design already owes: seed `irq_enable` with a NONZERO write to `+0x188` before reading it, so the ACCEPT row cannot pass on the reset zero; a row per remaining live site checking survival, accurate bounded reporting AND no state mutation; and for `+0x18c`, that the invalid read neither clears `irq_enable` nor returns it.  **A PER-SITE LATCH BITMASK, not one shared latch** -- otherwise the first access kind to fire suppresses the first useful report for every other, which is `#490`'s measured `M3shared` shape.  *Provenance and its limit: the datasheet citations are `dc21285.txt` line anchors from a seat that read the file.  I verified the count reconciliation, the `#432` attribution and the gate coupling myself; I did NOT re-read all six datasheet sections, and this row says so rather than implying a completeness it does not have.*  *** TWO FARMED RUNS OF THE SAME SEAT ON THE SAME BRIEF AGREED ON EVERYTHING CHECKABLE AND SPLIT ON ONE DESIGN CALL.  Both are recorded; neither is picked, because picking is what the round is for. ***  (Both ran because a `${j}` in the launch command did not expand, so each job read all three briefs -- an accident that bought an unplanned replication.)  **AGREED, and these are the facts:** eight guest-access kills not nine, the ninth removed by `#432`; `+0x188` is ACCEPT; the four writes to read-only status registers are decline+announce; and WIDTH vs DIRECTION at the sgi_re sites are TWO rounds, not one, because they share an address and a probe but not a semantic class.  **SPLIT 1 -- how many round-groups.**  Run A said THREE (accept / five invalid-direction / two SDRAM).  Run B said FOUR, splitting `+0x18c` (IRQEnableClear, a READ of a write-only register) into its own group because it carries an obligation the other four do not: **the present code must also STOP EXPOSING `d->irq_enable`**, which a plain decline does not accomplish.  Run A recorded that obligation but kept the site in the same group.  **Run B's split is the better one on the round-scope rule's own text** -- a site needing a state-suppression the others do not is not covered by the same fix design -- but it is a judgement call and it is recorded as one.  *** SPLIT 2, AND IT PROPOSES A FIFTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: `IMPLEMENT`. ***  For the two SDRAM Base Address registers (`+0x100` mask, `+0x104` offset) Run A said decline + an ACCURATE announce (*'unimplemented read'*, not *'invalid access'*).  Run B said flatly **'none of the four offered options fits'** and proposed implementing them: reset-zero state with masked write/readback.  **The disagreement is real because these registers are LEGAL AND READ/WRITE.**  The existing four members all presume the access is either unserviceable (delete / decline / decline+announce) or already correctly computed (ACCEPT).  A legal R/W register the model simply never implemented is neither: declining it announces truthfully and still returns a wrong answer to a guest that wrote then read back.  **THE ADDITION LICENCE PUTS THE BURDEN ON `IMPLEMENT`, and that is the right way round:** additions to product code here are licensed by a MEASURED DELTA or a TRANSFERRED WARRANT, never by 'it cannot hurt'.  Implementing two registers is an addition, so it owes one of those -- a guest that actually reads them back, or an in-tree precedent for modelling an SDRAM sizing register.  **Until one exists, decline+accurate-announce is the licensed choice and IMPLEMENT is the filed alternative.**  A round taking these two sites must answer this explicitly rather than inheriting whichever note it reads first.  *Recorded now because the four-member option set is carrier doctrine, and a proposed FIFTH member is a rule question -- which this project's own splitting rule says goes to the adjudicating seat at the next round boundary rather than being settled in passing.*  *** THE K1 GATE DEPENDENCY IS VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY, NOT TAKEN ON THE DESIGN'S WORD.  Main loop, 2026-08-30. ***  `gate_arm.sh` carries `check \"footbridge: the one-instruction kill arm still dies\"` and `footbridge_sites_probe.py` emits `K1  kill arm dies at 0x188, exit 1`.  **So the gate genuinely asserts that the kill KEEPS HAPPENING, and the ACCEPT round reddens it BY SUCCEEDING.**  A round that does not know this reads the red as a regression in its own work -- which is why it is written here rather than discovered at gate time.  **AND THE DEEPER PROBLEM IS THE ONE WORTH CARRYING FORWARD: the probe's failability leg RESTS ON A PRODUCT DEFECT.**  It reaches exactly one fatal site and uses that death as proof the guest code got that far.  That is sound while other kills remain and self-defeating when the round's whole purpose is to remove the last one.  *A test that proves its own liveness by reaching a crash is spending a defect as apparatus, and the bill comes due on the round that fixes it.*  **HOLD QUESTION SET 2026-08-30.**  This row was held with `hold_question: None` -- the exact defect `check_stage_panels.py` exists to catch, and it passed only because the hold predates the checker's cutoff.  *A stage held for a seat must carry the question it is held for; a seat fired against a disposition note has to GUESS what it is being asked, and a guessed question yields an answer to the wrong question that still looks like a discharged stage.*  *** THE HOLD QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER MOVED THE ROUND'S SHAPE IN FOUR PLACES.  Adjudicated 2026-08-30; every load-bearing claim re-verified here by grep before recording. ***  **(1) THE REPOINT IS LEGITIMATE -- BUT NOT FOR THE REASON I ASKED ABOUT.**  I framed it as an oracle-correction: a shipped row pinning the opposite of the architecture.  **K1 pins nothing about `+0x188`'s correct behaviour.**  Its own header says so -- `+0x188` is *an UNFIXED site, used here rather than repaired, because a probe that reports SURVIVED for every row is vacuous unless something in it can still die*.  It is a **FAILABILITY CONTROL whose CONSUMABLE the fix consumes**, and retargeting a control to another live consumable is not re-pinning an oracle.  **THE ORDER DISCIPLINE TRANSFERS ANYWAY, and this is the laundering hazard that IS real:** a round that repointed the arms and shipped nothing else would change behaviour with **zero rows changing colour** -- no red ever measured, observationally identical to fitting the harness around the fix.  So on the committed PRE-FIX build, before any `exit(1)` is touched: the repointed arms must still DIE at the new site, and the new ACCEPT row must be RED **for the reason under test** (its fatals carrying the ENABLE SET text -- mechanical rule 4).  A dead replacement control is the check-that-cannot-fail shape.  *** (2) THE BLAST RADIUS IS UNDERCOUNTED BY A FACTOR OF THREE, AND IT CARRIES A THREE-WEEK FUSE.  VERIFIED BY GREP. ***  The design says *repoint K1*.  **THREE committed probes plant `0xE5907188`** -- `footbridge_sites_probe.py:103`, `fbpending_drain_probe.py:226`, `fbpending_witness.py:195` -- and the latter two score their K rows on `HOST-DIED` (`fbpending_drain_probe.py:659`).  **`fbpending_drain_probe.py` is dated-exempt from wiring only until 2026-09-20**, with `gate_arm.sh` named as its home -- so a round that repoints one arm leaves two probes broken on disk and a fuse that wires one into a gate where its K row reads as a regression.  **All three move in the same commit**, each with its own pre-fix still-dies measurement.  **(3) THE THIRD ORACLE OBLIGATION BITES, AND THIS ROW'S OWN PRESCRIPTION IS INSUFFICIENT.**  The repointed arm observes the SAME CHANNEL RELOCATED -- guest read, fatal text, exit -- so without more the FIXED site would carry no row at all, which is worse than a row that observes the same value either way.  The other-channel row is the ACCEPT row, and this row previously prescribed *seed with a nonzero write, then read it back*.  **That is RAM-INDISTINGUISHABLE and the probe's own header already rejects it** -- *RAM, or any decode that merely absorbed the store, would return the value unchanged*.  **THE ROW MUST CARRY THE HANDLER'S OWN TRANSFORMATION AS ITS SIGNATURE**, and the set/clear machinery supplies one free: write V1 then V2 to `+0x188` and read back **V1|V2** (the `|=` accumulation), or write V1 to `+0x188` and V2 to `+0x18c` and read back **V1 & ~V2**.  *Any absorbing decode returns the last write; only this device returns the accumulation.*  **(4) A STRUCTURAL CAUTION THE DESIGN MISSED: the accept sequence needs its OWN probe arm, not the shared `BODY` list.**  `BODY` is executed by arms T, L and K alike, so a `+0x188` read placed there makes PRE-FIX arm L die mid-run, zeroing the liveness rows and mis-attributing the red -- *failed for some other reason*, which is the guard mechanical rule 4 exists to enforce.  A fourth launch costs about 0.6 s against a measured 1.9 s wall.  **(5) REPOINT TO `+0x100`, NOT `+0x18c` -- one hop instead of two.**  `+0x18c` is consumed by the DECLINE round under this row's own group order, so targeting it means three arms moved now and three moved AGAIN.  `+0x100` (`ldr r7,[r0,#256]`, fatal `read from sdram_ba_mask`) is a last-group site: same one-word edit, and the pre-fix measurement doubles as a rung-3 witness the SDRAM round inherits.  *** (6) THE TITLE IS DE-COUNTED, AND THE PREMISE FOR LEAVING IT WAS MEASURED FALSE. ***  The design left `nine` standing because *titles are identity strings other records grep*.  **Swept: the title text appears NOWHERE outside `ledger.json` itself**, the grep identity is the `id` field, and `gen_dashboard.py` uses the title for display only while `check_stage_panels`, `check_seats_read`, `gen_codex_wall` and `check_fable_queue` never read titles at all.  Editing it breaks zero cross-references; leaving it kept a wrong count in the row's most-read line -- **the verdict-line-position problem, where the half that survives truncation is the wrong half.**  **De-counted rather than re-counted to EIGHT**, following this repo's own adjudicated form: *removed rather than re-counted -- a prose list that duplicates a generated table has no way to stay true*.  The `n6` id is untouched, and the note keeps the NINE verbatim with its `#432` warrant, so provenance survives.  **THREE STALE CITATIONS TO FIX IN THE SAME ROUND**, all naming the kill site: the probe header and `gate_arm.sh` both say `dev_footbridge.c:495-497`, and `fbpending_witness.py` says `:486-499`; the real arm is `:577-591`.  **And the anchor itself is DELETED by this fix**, so the rewritten comments must anchor on the new site's text.  *** THE 2026-09-20 FUSE IS BOUNDED TO ONE PROBE.  Swept 2026-08-30 after this row surfaced the entanglement, because the obvious next question is whether OTHER exemptions carry the same trap. ***  **All six dated exemptions expire on the same day, 2026-09-20**, and expiry means the probe gets wired into a gate.  A probe whose row depends on a site DYING then reads as a regression if the round that fixes that site has shipped -- which is exactly what this row found for `fbpending_drain_probe.py`.  **Swept all six for any dependence on a site dying** (`HOST-DIED`, a `died` flag, an `exit == 1` expectation, `s.dead`), with denominators and a positive control so a pattern-matched-nothing result could not masquerade as a clean one:      fbpending_drain_probe    14 of 727 lines   <- the known entanglement      hpcmips_ctor_probe        2 of 758         <- BOTH ARE COMMENTS, not code      wdc_altoffset_probe       0 of 698      wdc_altoffset_sig_probe   0 of 263      wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe  0 of 240      wdc_addtoinbuf_probe      0 of 863      CONTROL footbridge_sites  3 of 316         <- the pattern fires where it must  `hpcmips_ctor_probe`'s two hits were opened and are prose -- *the process died and it died HERE are different claims*, and a note about a core dump.  **No functional dependence.**  **SO THE FUSE IS EXACTLY ONE PROBE WIDE**, and it is already named in this row's blast radius.  Nothing else in the exemption table needs to move when these expire.  *The positive control is the part that makes this worth recording: a sweep of six files returning five zeros is precisely the shape this project has twice mistaken for a result, and the control is what separates 'nothing there' from 'pattern matched nothing'.*  *** AND THE BLAST RADIUS HAS THREE TIERS, NOT ONE NUMBER.  Swept 2026-08-30, generalising the K1 finding into the question that actually matters. ***  **THE FIRST FORMULATION WAS WRONG AND ITS ZERO WAS WEAK.**  Sweeping the GATES for rows whose label demands a death found exactly one of **423 check rows** -- the K1 row -- and a second pattern looking for `HOST-DIED` in a check's want returned **0 of 423**.  That zero proves nothing: gates consume probe OUTPUT TOKENS (`^K1 .*ok$`), so the death-dependence lives INSIDE the probe, not in the gate row.  *A denominator validates the artifact, never the pattern's fitness for its format.*  **THE RIGHT QUESTION IS: WHICH PROBES SCORE A ROW ON A DEATH, AND WHICH OF THOSE DOES A GATE ACTUALLY RUN?**  Three probes score on a death -- `fbpending_drain_probe.py` (2 sites), `footbridge_sites_probe.py` (1), `fbpending_witness.py` (1) -- and **all three are REFERENCED by gates**, which looked at first like the radius tripling again.  **IT DOES NOT: SEPARATING INVOCATIONS FROM MENTIONS LEAVES EXACTLY ONE.**  `gate_arm.sh:987` genuinely runs `footbridge_sites_probe.py`; **every other reference in `gate_arm.sh`, `gate_hygiene.sh` and `gate_offline.sh` is a COMMENT.**  That is the mentions-are-not-wiring distinction `check_probe_wiring.py` exists to enforce, applied by hand here and giving the same answer.  **SO THE THREE TIERS ARE:**  **(1) TODAY** -- one gate row reddens, `gate_arm.sh`'s K1, via the only real invocation.  **(2) ON 2026-09-20** -- `fbpending_drain_probe.py`'s exemption expires, it is wired into `gate_arm.sh`, and its K row joins the first.  **(3) SILENTLY, AT ANY TIME** -- `fbpending_witness.py`'s K row breaks on disk and **NO GATE RUNS IT**, so nothing reports the breakage at all.  *That is the worst tier despite being the least urgent: a broken detector nobody runs is indistinguishable from a working one, and it is exactly the state the wiring checker was built to make visible.*  All three arms still move in the same commit -- the tiers change WHEN each would be noticed, not whether each is broken.  *** THE DATASHEET SPLITS WHAT THIS ROW TREATS AS ONE CLASS.  Derived 2026-08-30 from `_scratchpad/dc21285.txt`, cross-confirmed in THREE independent places so no extraction stagger is possible: the register summary (`IRQEnable/IRQEnableSet 188h`, `IRQEnableClear 18Ch`), the offset table (`188h  IRQEnable  IRQEnableSet` / `18Ch  --  IRQEnableClear`), and the section heading `7.3.32 IRQEnableSet/FIQEnableSet Register--Offset 188h/288h`. ***  Constants confirmed in `src/include/thirdparty/dc21285reg.h`.  **(a) READ of 0x188 -> ACCEPT.**  *\"IRQEnable/FIQEnable Register--Offset 188h/288h.  This read-only register is used to mask the interrupt input sources... The value of this register can only be changed by writing to the EnableSet and EnableClear registers.  Reset value: 0.\"*  The emulator ALREADY does `odata = d->irq_enable;` -- the documented value -- and then calls `exit(1)` on it.  Delete the kill, KEEP THE VALUE, **no latch**: there is no event to announce, because the read is implemented and is not ignored.  A bounded announce here would ship a wrong record in product code.  **(b) READ of 0x18c -> DECLINE.**  The offset table gives `--` for its read function: there is NO defined read.  The emulator returns `d->irq_enable`, which is a GUESS, then exits.  **Different disposition from its immediate neighbour, and the code treats them identically.**  **(c) WRITE to FIQ_STATUS (0x280) -> DECLINE.**  *\"The Status and RawStatus are read-only.\"*  *** WHY THIS MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TRIVIA: a single splice across all three would ACCEPT an undefined read at 0x18c and DECLINE a documented one at 0x188 -- getting both neighbours wrong, in OPPOSITE directions. ***  This is the option-set rule paying out again, with the ACCEPT member in play -- and that member is visible only from the datasheet, never from the source.  *This row's title treats 'the guest-reachable kills' as ONE THING.  It is three things.*",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:3971:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:7266:   "hold_question": "A straddling store whose tail faults makes an MMIO head see the write twice. The row was REPRODUCED at rung 3 and then RE-RANKED DOWN -- three of its factual claims were measured false, it is PPC-only, no in-tree guest reaches it, and the fix may itself DIVERGE from real hardware. QUESTION: given all that, is this documented-only? Be specific about the last point, because it is the one that decides: what does real PPC hardware do on a straddling store whose second half faults, and is our current behaviour actually wrong? If we cannot establish the hardware behaviour, then 'fix' is unwarranted and the honest disposition is to document the divergence and close.",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:3972:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-7267-   "witness": "none yet -- guest-reachable on PowerPC and witnessable at rung 3/4 (the observable is a side-effect COUNT, not a value), but a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids.",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:3989:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:7885:     "note": "8253/8254 PIT CLUSTER: five MEASURED defects at one site, brought to the panel with the scope question promoted to Q0 -- the brief did not ask whether they are real (a rung-3 reproduction had settled that) but WHICH IS THE ROUND and how many may travel together.\n\n*** Q0 ANSWERED UNANIMOUSLY: NO MERGES. FIVE SEPARATE ROUNDS. ***  agy: 'split pitflip, pitlatch, pitlsb and isaorder into separate candidate rounds'. glm: 'none merge under the one-fix-design test'. deepseek: 'no merges'. minimax sequenced them rather than combining. **This OVERRULES the measure seat's own suggestion that pitflip was 'the natural companion' to i8253zero** -- it argued same-device and same-semantic-class (counter-load byte routing), and the panel applied the remaining prongs and said no. The measuring seat found the defects; the panel scoped them, and the two jobs came apart cleanly.\n\nWHAT IS FIRST -- pitlatch, 3 to 1:\n  pitlatch    deepseek, glm, minimax. glm's clause is the decisive one: 'a real guest reaches it in microtime()'. That is this project's ordering rule applied exactly -- severity and reachability, never rung, never vote -- and pitlatch is the only one of the five demonstrated to be on a path a shipped guest actually takes (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209).\n  i8253zero   agy alone, as a standalone fix round.\nMINIMAX GAVE THE MOST REASONED SEQUENCE and it is worth keeping: pitlatch, then pitflip, then **i8253zero THIRD 'once pitflip closes its door'** -- because pitflip is what MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so fixing pitflip removes i8253zero's main reachability route and lowers its rank. That is a dependency between rounds, not a merge.\n\nDROP isaorder AS A ROUND -- deepseek and glm ('latent, unmeasured, different file'); minimax puts it 'filed-only pending a reproduction', which is the same disposition in gentler words. It is the one item recorded at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 17.7 KB, grok 11.2 KB, kimi 89.0 KB, glm 10.1 KB, deepseek 4.7 KB, minimax 12.7 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, tenth consecutive wall, arithmetic again: '19649b = brief 18961b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: THIRD by the panel's most reasoned sequence, not first -- and the reason is a dependency worth recording: pitflip MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so closing pitflip removes this row's main reachability route. agy alone would do it first, as a standalone round. It keeps its rung-3 witness either way.",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4276:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:11014:     "note": "*** THE ENTRY I WROTE EARLIER TODAY CARRIED A REFUTED CLAIM AND HAS BEEN REPLACED BY THIS\nONE (1 entry removed, its content quoted below).  Caught by an adjudicating seat BEFORE\nANYTHING WAS STAGED, so this is L1 construction, not a shipped wrong record. ***\n\n**IT SAID: \"no FLUSH constant exists in this tree's ATA headers\".  FALSE.**\n`src/include/thirdparty/wdcreg.h:130` is `#define WDCC_FLUSHCACHE 0xe7`.\n\n**AND THE RECORDED EVIDENCE COULD NOT HAVE PRODUCED THE RECORDED CONCLUSION.**  I ran a\n`grep -rn FLUSH src/include/` piped through `head -6` -- there are **46** hits -- and read a\nTRUNCATION as an ABSENCE, under a label reading 'blank = the constant does not exist' while\nthe output was not blank.  *The apparatus-failure class, committed inside a record whose own\nclosing note warns about it.*  **Three siblings already held the right answer and none was\nconsulted**: this row's own CONFIRMED research entry of 2026-08-19, `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` at\nthe same fact, and the header itself.\n\nFIVE REPAIRS, each re-verified by me against the tree before writing:\n\n**(1) The correct fact is STRONGER than the false one.**  The constant is DEFINED and\n**referenced ZERO times in `dev_wdc.c`**, so 0xE7 falls to `default:` BY CONSTRUCTION.  0xEA\n(FLUSH CACHE EXT) has no constant under `src/` at all and belongs in scope too -- the\n2026-08-19 research covered both; my scope line covered only 0xE7.\n\n**(2) '18 of 256 words' dropped a qualifier: they are 18 SITES, not 18 words.**  Three are\nmemcpys spanning ranges -- word 10 a 20-byte serial (10-19), word 23 an 8-byte firmware\n(23-26), word 27 a 40-byte model (27-46) -- so ~49 words are written.  **The load-bearing\nfact is better stated as a CEILING: the highest word touched is 68**, so words 82-87 keep the\nmemset's zero.  A stronger form of the same conclusion: word 83 = 0x0000 also fails the bits\n15:14 = 01b validity signature, so a conforming host discards the whole word -- the drive\nadvertises NO VALID COMMAND-SET INFORMATION AT ALL, not merely a cleared flush bit.\n\n**(3) The arm census missed `case SET_FEATURES:` (0xEF).**  It is defined in `wdcreg.h`\nWITHOUT the `WDCC_` prefix, so my prefix-keyed grep could not see it.  Twenty case labels, not\nnineteen; none a flush.  *A count claiming completeness from an instrument blind to a member.*\n\n**(4) THE WARRANT HIERARCHY WAS INVERTED, and this is the substantive one.**  I presented\n'the drive never advertises it' as *the whole content* of the disposition.  The row's research\nentry measured something stronger AND partly contrary: OpenBSD 7.6 through a full filesystem\nlifecycle issued `0xEC 0xF5 0x10 0x91 0xC6 0x20 0xC4 0x30 0xC5 0xE0` -- no 0xE7, no 0xEA --\n**and with word 83 forced to 0x5000/0x7000 the guest demonstrably READ the new block (it began\nissuing 0xEF) and STILL never flushed.**  opus5 therefore demoted the advertise-reason: why\nOpenBSD declines *remains an honest UNKNOWN*.  **The MEASUREMENT is the warrant; the IDENTIFY\nfact is what a CONFORMING guest is entitled to conclude.**  I had re-derived a compressed\none-clause version of a multi-factor review -- unanimity with a stated premise, where I\nverified the arithmetic of a claim whose causal story the row's own evidence had already\ndemoted to hypothesis.\n\n**(5) Point 3 under-cited: `#485` belongs beside `#464`.**  The `default:` arm carries BOTH\noutside the latch guard -- `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` (#464) and `d->int_assert = 1` (#485).\n**Without #485 the honest abort was POLLING-ONLY**; an interrupt-driven guest saw nothing at\nall.  Citing only #464 claimed an abort that three days earlier would not have been delivered.\n\n*** THE POLARITY CONTRAST SURVIVES BUT MUST NOT TRAVEL UNQUALIFIED. ***  IDE's `default:`\naborts honestly; SCSI's reports success (`b120u`).  **But `dev_wdc.c:1515` routes ATAPI packet\ncommands into `diskimage_scsicommand()`, and dev_wdc treats `res == 1` as success** -- so an\nunknown PACKET command on an IDE ATAPI drive takes the SUCCESS polarity THROUGH the wdc\ndevice.  One device, both polarities, selected by path.  **Deliberately NOT minted as a\ncarrier line**: it is an instance-pair, not a rule, and the qualifier it needs makes it a\nparagraph -- an unqualified line would be the dropped-qualifier class itself.  It lands in the\nrecord and as a cross-reference on `b120u`, whose fixer gains a second reachability route.\n\nDISPOSITION UNCHANGED AND RATIFIED: **ASSESSED, NOT CHANGED.**  Witness `none`, in the honest\nform: *a non-event admits no rung-3/4 witness (a ceiling four seat entries already record);\nthe non-issuance claim itself is carried by the measured lifecycle census of 2026-08-19.*  The\n2026-08-27 ask-one-seat ruling is satisfied in substance -- the attempt question was answered\nin-row by measurement, not skipped.\n\nArtifact: `regress/artifacts/idesync/assessed_not_changed_20260829.md`, rewritten with all\nfive repairs and the correction kept IN PLACE rather than silently swapped.\n\n*NOT CLOSED -- closure owes the stage coverage the full-panel directive mandates.*"
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4338:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:11744:   "state_note": "*** STATUS FIRST, BECAUSE THIS NOTE OPENED WITH PRE-FIX GUIDANCE THAT THE SHIPPED FIX HAS ALREADY CONSUMED.  Added 2026-08-29. ***  **FIXED at HEAD** -- #464 (six unlatched guest-drivable `fatal()` sites, re-scoped from one arm to six), #485 (the completion interrupt, without which the abort was polling-only), #486 (repaired the round's own non-discriminating clause-(b) gate row).  A rung-3 witness with both admissibility controls is on the row.  **It stays `in work` for exactly two reasons: a minimax pass-2 seat failure owed a re-fire with a repaired brief, and closure adjudication.**  The title is the FOUNDING claim, which is this ledger's convention and not a wrong record -- but a reader meeting `in work` plus a '100 commands, 100 host log lines' title plus work-it-this-way guidance concludes the flood is live, which is the es438 / [[ledgerstatelies]] shape.\n\n  *** THE THREE-MEMBERED OPTION SET APPLIES DIRECTLY TO THIS ROW (carrier rule, minted 2026-08-25 from #463). *** #463 de-fatalised a device by framing the choice as KEEP the unlatched `fatal()` versus DELETE it, chose delete, and its own research stage then found the third member had been shipping in-tree the whole time: **`memory_rw.c:495-523` bounds a guest-drivable diagnostic to 8 emissions via `n_trunc_msgs`, because 'probing at the end of a device's mapped range is common and benign'.** The carrier already said 'a guest-drivable fatal() needs a latch and not a verbosity flag' -- standing rule, never consulted by that round's two-membered framing. **So before this row is worked: the option set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+BOUNDED-ANNOUNCE, and the first step is to grep the fork for the existing idiom rather than reason from first principles.** #463 also established that a bounded announce is not merely acceptable but RECOVERS the self-announcing property that de-fatalising destroys -- which for an unlatched flood is the whole point, since the flood and the announcement are the same channel.  *** MEASURED 2026-08-25, AND THE ROW IS AIMED AT THE WRONG SITE. *** The opus5 seat built a `cp -a` copy at /tmp/gxwdc, verified freshness independently of MAKE_RC, and left GXEMUL-SEC untouched (verified by `git status --porcelain`: no src file modified). *** THE FLOOD IS CONFIRMED AND IS WORSE THAN THE TITLE SAYS: *** 20 stores produce 20 `fatal()` lines WITH and WITHOUT `-q` (0 `debug()` lines either way), and the OVER-READ site at 500 iterations produced 2000 lines / 158,449 bytes of host log (`regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/n3.out`, preserved off the volatile session scratchpad this row used to cite). *** CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-26: this sentence said \"the read path\", which points a reader at the non-8-bit READ guard. `n3.out` is 2000x \"reading too much\" -- the RING OVER-READ. Same numbers, wrong site named. The correction had already landed in the round block, the source comment and the gate comment while this field kept the false wording -- which is the corrections-land-in-CHANGELOG-only failure this ledger records having made twice before, now three times. It was found by a mechanical sweep for stale claims across all five records, not by reading. *** *** BUT `:583`, THE SITE THIS ROW NAMES, IS THE MORE GATED OF TWO FLOODS. *** It requires an attached IDE image (`:461-468` early-returns with a silenceable `debug()` otherwise) and yields ONE line per store. The unnamed sibling `wdc_get_inbuf` over-read at `:160` requires NOTHING -- landisk adds wdc unconditionally at machine_landisk.c:90 -- is on the READ path, and yields FOUR lines per single guest instruction (measured: mov.b/mov.w/mov.l x20 give 20/40/80). SIBLING CENSUS: ten `fatal()` in dev_wdc.c, FOUR latched by #337, SIX UNLATCHED -- :145, :160, :583, :607, :704, :1025. **Fixing :583 alone leaves the strictly worse :160 floodable at 4x with no precondition**, which is the project's recorded 'grep for its siblings' failure. SCOPE RULING SOUGHT: the round is the FILE, not the line -- six sites, one design, one detector. *** WHY #337 MISSED IT, AND IT IS EXPLICABLE RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT: *** that round's scope was \"each replaced `exit(1)`\", and `:583` was always a `fatal()`, never an `exit(1)`, so it fell outside the enumeration. *** THE OPTION-SET PREMISE WAS REFUTED, AND IT WAS MINE. *** The three-membered option set rule describes member 3 as \"announces the first N occurrences and then goes quiet\". That describes `memory_rw.c:495-523` -- which is the tree's ONLY counter-style bound, is DOUBLE-GATED so the counter never increments at default verbosity (`&&` short-circuits), is GLOBAL not per-instance, and prints no give-up notice. The house idiom is different and mature: **N=1 PER-INSTANCE LATCH plus an explicit \"(further occurrences suppressed)\" in the message text**, at roughly 34 sites across 11 device files. `dev_wdc.c` itself holds FOUR (`warned_badlen`, `warned_atapi_err`, `warned_atapi_dataout`, struct at :75-77) and is the tree's REFERENCE implementation -- dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:147 says its own latch is \"Modelled on dev_wdc.c's warned_badlen (#337)\". *** AND `fatal()` SHOULD BE KEPT, ON A RECORDED HOUSE WARRANT: *** dev_asc.c:250-257 states that under `-q` verbosity settles at VERBOSITY_ERROR, so a WARNING-level debugmsg would be INVISIBLE to the boot-log hygiene grep and an ERROR-level one would trip #261's break-on-ERROR. The chronology runs one way -- #245 chose verbosity-gating; #265/#269, #337, #460, #461 all chose latch + fatal(). TWO HAZARDS FOR THE DIFF, both from the seat: (1) SKETCH RULE -- DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc) has a common tail at :1019-1030 (`ret:` -> memory_writemax64 -> return 1), so any decline at the ACCESS level must `goto ret`; `wdc_command()` itself is void and called at :1003, so returning inside IT is safe. (2) CLAUSE (d) -- the guard would read the new `warned_*` field while the body reads `d->cur_command`, so the detector owes a state where a SECOND, DIFFERENT unimpl -- nothing else in the file covers them. `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1` are the two rows that read a guest-visible SIDE EFFECT rather than a line count, and they are the only rows that can see a repair swallowed by a guard. *** M2 IS THE MEASURED VINDICATION OF AN ORACLE CHOICE. *** It sets the flag before the test so the site emits ZERO lines. **A `<= 1 line` oracle would have passed it**; `exactly 1` catches it. That choice was argued from a seat's reasoning and is now measured. *** AND M6 IS THE ESCAPE, PREDICTED BY A SEAT ON PASS 2 AND CONFIRMED HERE. *** It swaps the latch operand for the GUEST-RESETTABLE `d->seccnt`: `if (len != 1 && !d->seccnt) { d->seccnt = 1; fatal(...); }`. Every row in table 1 fires its site TWICE IN SUCCESSION, so nothing between the fires ever puts the operand back and the mutant looks perfectly latched. A guest that writes `seccnt = 0` between wide writes floods without bound. **This is a STRUCTURAL blindness of the row design, not a missing mutant** -- the same shape as `#463`'s distinctness invariance, where rows that assert a property back-to-back cannot see an edit that changes only what happens BETWEEN the assertions. *** DETECTOR NOW 22 ROWS, MEASURED 22/22 ON THE FIXED BUILD (`table2_22rows/base_22rows.raw`). *** Seven rows added, each closing something table 1 or pass 2 exposed: `overrun x2` (the :145 row -- **this is what closes the L2 floor breach**, and it needed a hardware loop, `dt r3` / `bf` with a computed displacement, because 513 undrained IDENTIFYs unrolled would be 513 debugger commands); `interleave` (fire, VALID command, fire); `w16 reset` (fire, guest CLEARS the operand, fire -- the M6 killer); `fire3` (kills period-N latches, not just period-2); `w32 x2` and `r32 x2` (the width rows probed 16-bit only, so a guard narrowed to `len == 2` escaped); `r16 value` and `w16 value` (value oracles at the two width sites, for a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- which a count-only row cannot see). Row hygiene checked: no name over 13 chars, no duplicates, no name that is a prefix of another followed by a space. TABLE 2 is running against the 22-row detector with three mutants table 1 could not have had: `Mb2` removes the :145 guard verbatim (the clause-(b) reinstatement at the site that had no row), `M7` is the reset-on-normal-operation escape, `M8` is the period-2 latch. All three anchors were verified to occur EXACTLY ONCE in the source first -- **a mutant that fails to apply scores a false clean**, which is the worst possible failure for a kill table. *** A CAPTION DEFECT IN THE APPARATUS, FOUND AND FIXED AT SOURCE. *** `killwdc.sh` wrote its header at start with the row count and the pre-fix score as LITERALS (`14 rows`, `8/14`). The moment pass 2 forced seven more rows, table 2's own header became wrong about its own size. The script now DERIVES the count (`grep -c '^row(' ...`) and stamps a UTC start time instead of quoting scores it cannot know. Table 2's already-written header is stale and is corrected in place; the script fix stops it recurring. *A table that misreports its own size cannot be cited safely at all.*  *** KILL TABLE 2 IS CLEAN: 22-ROW DETECTOR, TEN MUTANTS, **ZERO ESCAPES**, EVERY LOG INTERNALLY CONSISTENT. *** Preserved at `regress/artifacts/wdcflood/table2_22rows/` with a header that DERIVES its row count rather than quoting one. Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. base 22/22; Mb1 18/22; M1 18/22; M2 17/22; M3 21/22; M4 21/22; M5 21/22; M6 21/22; M7 21/22; M8 21/22; Mb2 21/22. **Consistency check on all eleven logs: score + anchored-red count == 22 in every one**, which is the check that would catch a contaminated extractor. *** SIX ESCAPES, SIX SINGLE-ROW KILLS, EACH BY THE ROW BUILT FOR IT -- and that one-to-one correspondence is the result, not the scores: *** M3 last-warned-opcode -> `unimpl 2op` ALONE; M4 ABRT inside the guard -> `abrt 2nd` ALONE; M5 `return -1` inside the guard -> `overread -1` ALONE; M6 guest-resettable operand -> `w16 reset` ALONE; M7 reset-on-normal-operation -> `interleave` ALONE; Mb2 the :145 verbatim reinstatement -> `overrun x2` ALONE. **Nothing else in the file sees any of them.** *** THE L2 FLOOR BREACH IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT. *** `Mb2` is the exact edit that swept CLEAN against the 14-row detector -- removing :145's guard verbatim -- and it now reddens `overrun x2`, the row that did not exist when the breach was found. Clause (b) holds at all six fixed sites. *** M8 VINDICATES A CAUTION, AND THE CAUTION IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. *** deepseek proposed `d->warned_X = !d->warned_X;` as a period-2 escape. I read its SPECIFIC edit as unworkable -- the assignment sits INSIDE the guard, so fire 2 never reaches it -- and recorded it as **\"a prediction to be MEASURED, not refuted\"**. M8 constructs the same class differently (toggle BEFORE the guard) and escapes a two-fire oracle exactly as deepseek said the class would. **Wrong about the placement, RIGHT ABOUT THE CLASS.** Declaring it refuted on the strength of a correct reading of one construction would have lost a real escape. *A seat can be wrong about the mechanism and right about the family; the family is the part worth building a row for.* *** CLAUSE (c), THE NULL MEDIUM, IS NOW MEASURED AND IN ITS STRONGEST FORM: 0/22 ON BOTH. *** `nullmedium_absent.raw` (a binary that does not exist) and `nullmedium_notanemu.raw` (a real executable that is not an emulator) each score **0/22 -- every row fails, not merely some**. It had NOT been verified before; the question was raised against the round's own readiness and answered in seconds. **AND THE MEASUREMENT EXPOSED A DESIGN PROPERTY THAT WAS ACCIDENTAL RATHER THAN INTENDED:** the three accept rows expect ZERO diagnostics, so a naive implementation would PASS against silence -- zero is exactly what silence produces. They fail correctly only because `n()` returns `\"died\"` rather than `\"0\"` when the run produced nothing (probe :227-228). **That single line is what stops every accept row in this file from being vacuous under clause (c)**, and it was not written for that reason. Worth carrying to any future detector with absence-shaped oracles: an \"expect 0\" row MUST distinguish 'ran and saw nothing' from 'did not run'.  *** THE READINESS ADJUDICATION RETURNED **HOLD-FOR** AND FOUND TWO SHIPPED ROWS VACUOUS. ASKING BEFORE THE TABLE FINISHED IS WHAT MADE THAT POSSIBLE -- a clean sweep invites ratification. *** **`r16 value` WAS VACUOUS AS BUILT.** Its oracle was `printed(t,\"r2\") != \"absent\"` against a wanted `\"read\"`, so it passed whenever the debugger echoed ANY hex value -- structurally incapable of failing while the emulator lived. **Confirmed: green under every one of table 2's ten mutants -- it did not exist for table 1, which measured a 14-row detector, and an earlier note said 'tables 1 and 2', which is false.** The `live` control already covered session death, so the row measured nothing at all. **AND BOTH WIDTH VALUE ROWS CHECKED THE WRONG FIRE.** The escape they cite -- a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- corrupts the FIRST access (flag unset, guard entered, return skips the repair); the SECOND finds the flag set, skips the guard and repairs normally. Both rows read the value ONCE, after both fires, and so saw a perfectly good value. The contract line claiming they carried value oracles \"for exactly that edit\" was FALSE AS BUILT. Repaired to check BOTH fires, with a low-byte mask because the repair sets only `data[0]` and asserting the upper byte would make the row about the wrong thing. *** TABLE 3 EARNS THE REPAIRED ROWS THEIR MEASURED KILL, WHICH IS WHAT A REPAIRED ROW OWES: *** `table3_repaired/`, base 22/22. `Mv1` (delete `idata = data[0]`, the write-side repair) 18/22 -> r16 value, w16 value, overrun x2, accept ec. `Mv2` (delete `data[0] = odata`, the read-side repair inside the `ret:` tail) 18/22 -> devsig, abrt 2nd, r16 value, w16 value. **`Mv3` (codex's EXACT edit -- insert `return 1;` after the latched READ `fatal()`) 21/22 -> `r16 value` ALONE.** *** Mv3 IS THE ONE THAT PROVES THE REPAIR RATHER THAN THE ROW. *** It corrupts only the first accessd guest actually takes (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209).\n  i8253zero   agy alone, as a standalone fix round.\nMINIMAX GAVE THE MOST REASONED SEQUENCE and it is worth keeping: pitlatch, then pitflip, then **i8253zero THIRD 'once pitflip closes its door'** -- because pitflip is what MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so fixing pitflip removes i8253zero's main reachability route and lowers its rank. That is a dependency between rounds, not a merge.\n\nDROP isaorder AS A ROUND -- deepseek and glm ('latent, unmeasured, different file'); minimax puts it 'filed-only pending a reproduction', which is the same disposition in gentler words. It is the one item recorded at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held.\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 (agy 17.7 KB, grok 11.2 KB, kimi 89.0 KB, glm 10.1 KB, deepseek 4.7 KB, minimax 12.7 KB). Codex ECHO-ONLY, tenth consecutive wall, arithmetic again: '19649b = brief 18961b + 688b'.\n\nFOR THIS ROW: DROP AS A ROUND -- two seats explicitly, a third as 'filed-only pending a reproduction'. It is the one item filed at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason: latent, unmeasured, different file. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held. If it survives, it needs its own reproduction first -- an ordering bug in what construction itself does is what a rung-2 witness is for.",
