regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4454:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-15299-   "witness": "detector:regress/diff_footbridge.c -- OWNER-ADOPTED TERM, 2026-08-27.  The fix shipped with a MEASURED detector and a kill table, and no guest-path witness.  `detector:` IS NOT A RUNG: it ranks below every access witness and does NOT discharge the rung-3 ATTEMPT the lifecycle ruling requires.  Before this term the row had to be written `none`, which asserts DOCUMENTED-ONLY and was false about a round that shipped a fix.  *** THIS FIELD CITED `regress/diff_fb_clear.c`, WHICH DOES NOT EXIST AND NEVER DID. Corrected 2026-08-27 by a sibling grep. ***  The real detector is `regress/diff_footbridge.c` (its rows are tagged fbclearread in the file).  Found while correcting `gdromexitsix`, whose `probe:` field cited two scratchpad scripts that are also gone -- the adjudicating seat pointed out that the grep-for-siblings rule had been skipped, and doing it turned up these two.  **The `detector:` term was owner-adopted on 2026-08-27, used on three rows that day, and TWO of the three cited a file that does not exist** -- a plausible-looking filename written from intent rather than from the tree.",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4456:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-15301-   "hold_question": "Does a fix shipped on rung-1 (offline differential) evidence alone need an OWNER EXEMPTION, or must it wait for a rung-3 probe? THREE rounds now sit here -- b115 (#472), this one, and its sibling -- so it is a standing policy question, not a one-off. The ladder admits `harness:` only when the round's SUBJECT is the test apparatus, which is not the case for any of the three."
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4663:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17986-   "witness": "probe:regress/gdrom_data_probe.py (rung 3, 23 rows, SHIPPED). MEASURED 5/23 pre-fix and 23/23 post-fix, SAME SCRIPT, with the pre-fix build rebuilt from `git show HEAD:` into a private clone and grep-confirmed to still contain the old fatal text -- NOT from build/, which a seat had measured moving underneath it mid-review the previous round. All 17 pre-fix FAILs read `died`; none failed by a wrong value. Controls: LIVENESS (guest store then guest load), SH4 DEVICE SIGNATURE (IPRD reset 0xda74), and a GDROM-SPECIFIC mapping proof (a served GDROM_BUSY read rather than a paddr fault) -- all three green on both sides and each in its OWN session, so no control touches GDROM_DATA.",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4675:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17998-     "note": "CENSUS taken after #459 closed the last guest-reachable exit( in dev_sh4.c, counting CODE only -- `grep -c 'exit(1)'` is measurably wrong in this tree because the hardened comments DISCUSS exit(1) constantly, so dev_sh4.c scores 21 by that method and 0 by a real one. Live (non-#ifdef, non-construction) exits remaining on the SH surface: dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c 14, dev_dreamcast_g2.c 6, dev_dreamcast_maple.c 3, dev_dreamcast_asic.c 1, cpu_sh_instr.c 2, cpu_sh.c 1 (sh_exception). THE GDROM ONES ARE THE MOST GUEST-REACHABLE: handle_command() switches on d->cmd[0], a byte the guest writes, and its arms exit(1) on an unimplemented command, on a Read-TOC length that is not 408, and on a data format that is not 0x20 -- each an ordinary guest-supplied value. WITNESS SHAPE IS ALREADY PROVEN: a rung-3 cold-debugger probe on `-E dreamcast -x` (the -x is required; two console inputs make construction fatal without it, console.c:994-1007). NOT YET WITNESSED -- no dreamcast image exists in _images/, so the probe must drive the GDROM registers by planted guest stores rather than by booting."
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4696:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18444-     "note": "*** WITNESS CONSTRUCTIBLE AT RUNG 3, contrary to `witness: none`. Verified by READING the arm; flagged as a reading, since a probe run would have contended with a live gate. *** The carrier's rule is that 'no witness of either kind' is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked rather than asserted. Checked: `len` appears exactly THREE times in DEVICE_ACCESS(dreamcast_gdrom_dma), and all three are byte marshalling -- memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len) on the way in and two memory_writemax64(..., len, ...) on the way out. The store itself, at dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:889 (re-derived 2026-08-23), is `d->dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata;` -- a full 32-bit assignment with no width discrimination anywhere. THE WITNESS IS `dma seed` PLUS ONE STORE, exactly as the filing predicted: 32-bit store 0x11223344 at DMA+0x04, then a BYTE store of 0xff at DMA+0x04 (ALIGNED, so it matches `case 0x04` and is NOT the #461 alias), then read +0x04 back. Real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits; this model yields 0x000000ff. Ordinary guest instructions through real address decode on a committed machine -- rung 3, no new device and no machine edit, so no laundering. NO BUILD NEEDED EITHER: the defect is pre-existing upstream and untouched by #461, so it is in the current binary."
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4703:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18451:     "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] *** FOUND THE DECISIVE PRECEDENT, AND IT IS IN THIS VERY FILE FROM THE PREVIOUS ROUND. *** dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515-522, written by #460: 'DO NOT \"EMULATE\" NARROW ACCESS INSTEAD. A pass-1 panel split on that, and it was MEASURED ACTIVELY HARMFUL: letting cmd_count step by 1 makes eleven byte writes plus one word write go 11 -> 13, SKIPPING the == 12 equality, so busy&0x08 never clears and the next write reaches the \"Too much GDROM_DATA\" exit -- otherwise unreachable. It also writes cmd[12], one past the array.' VERIFIED BY READING. Option A is exactly that shape, ruled out by the file's own measured history one round ago. Its reachability line is the right distinction too: 'Reachable as a guest instruction; unobserved as a driver path. That is not Option C.' -- rung-3 reachability is established, driver reachability is not, and the ladder says a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE while severity orders it."
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4860:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-19924-   "witness": "probe:GXEMUL-SEC/regress/asic_badaddr_probe.py -- rung 3, 13 rows. RED on committed HEAD 3710d41 (5 of 9 at the then 9-row form, regress/artifacts/asic463/witness_prefix_HEAD.raw), GREEN with the fix. Final table 13/13 base, 12 mutants, no escapes (regress/artifacts/asic463/table5_13rows/).",
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4865:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:19929:    "note": "CLOSE. Fix shipped at f1fa6a0 with a 13-row rung-3 detector wired into gate_sh_rounding.sh in the same commit. Witness RED on committed HEAD 3710d41, GREEN with the fix; final table 13/13 base, 12 mutants, NO ESCAPES (regress/artifacts/asic463/table5_13rows/). Vacuity floor (a)-(e) measured, and the round MINTED clause (f), the ANCHOR RULE, after its own detector was escaped by a translation three distinctness rows were blind to BY CONSTRUCTION. **A-badge: author self-review disclosed** -- the fix, the detector and this closure are all mine; the escapes that forced four of the thirteen rows came from seats (glm/deepseek/agy on `& 2`, kimi/agy on `& 7`, grok on `& 1`, codex on `/ 2`, opus5 on the `+1` translation), and the disposition was adjudicated rather than self-ruled. TWELVE residuals filed, not fixed: asicpermute, asicindexcase, asicoffsetkill, asickillsurvive, asicarray4, asicarraygeom, asiceventaddr (corrected, still latent), plus panelseatlabel, seatthinkburn, scoreclaimhex and ledgerstatelies from the round's own tooling and records panels."
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4959:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-20773-    "note": "FIXED: `witness` and `state_note` now render into the row TOOLTIP -- not inline, per the owner's 2026-08-15 layout directive that the header stays one line and everything else is mouse-over. matrix.html grew 1,876,287 -> 1,920,514 bytes, +44,227, against the 34,712 bytes of field text measured as invisible plus HTML escaping: **the delta is the confirmation.** Spot-checked two corrections that were previously unreachable from the published artifact and are now present -- `asicbadaddr`'s severity correction (not a memory-safety defect) and `probetimebased`'s corrected nine-of-57 census. *** THE COST WAS NEVER COSMETIC: `witness` is the field a RANKER READS, and this week two facts were corrected in a seat entry and left stale in it -- `gdrompionull`'s rung-3 cost, and `asicbadaddr`'s `none yet -- one constant away` while its witness existed and had been reviewed, under-rating the row a ranking then placed FIRST. A field nobody can see is a field nobody re-reads. *** Badge A: found, filed and fixed by the main loop, by noticing that a substantial ledger edit changed the rendered artifact by ZERO BYTES -- twice.  COMMITS AND closure.by SET 2026-08-25 from a SUBSTANCE READ OF GIT, not from prose. `e6c7e28` (\"harness: ... and two invisible ledger fields\") adds 21 lines to `tools/pipeline/gen_dashboard.py`, TEN of them mentioning `witness` or `state_note`, and regenerates matrix.html -- which is exactly this row's subject. **closure.by previously read `gen_dashboard.py`: a FILE, not a party that can adjudicate.** That is the wrong-KIND class filed as `ledgerclosureby`; the row has no adjudicating entry, so the value is set to the seat that authored the fix and the substitution is disclosed here rather than made silently."
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:4963:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:20777:   "state_note": "MAIN-LOOP, 2026-08-24, found while correcting a wrong witness field. `gdrompionull`'s witness said rung 3 `costs four stores and a load`. The opus5 seat had MEASURED that wrong (18 guest accesses, or 9 by the cheap route) and its correction was recorded in its own entry the same day -- but the FIELD kept the old number for a further round, which made the item look four times cheaper to witness than it is, in the one piece of text that orders the queue. *** THE REASON IT SURVIVED IS MECHANICAL, NOT INATTENTION: `gen_dashboard.py` contains no reference to `witness` at all, so the field is invisible on the published artifact. *** Editing it changed matrix.html by ZERO BYTES, which is how this was noticed -- the byte count was expected to move and did not. So every check anyone performs by LOOKING at the dashboard is structurally blind to this field. Corrected in place with its provenance; a sibling sweep across all 197 rows found no other witness field carrying a superseded cost. FIX (small): render `witness` in the row detail, or add a checker that refuses a witness field whose claim a later entry corrects. The second is the stronger one and generalises past this field.  *** THE SAME GAP EXISTS FOR `state_note`, AND IT IS THE LARGER HALF. Measured 2026-08-24, again by a byte count that did not move when it should have. *** Three queue dispositions were written and `matrix.html` came back BYTE-IDENTICAL; `gen_dashboard.py` contains ZERO references to `state_note`, exactly as it does for `witness`. **QUANTIFIED, because the first framing of this was going to be wrong:** 9 rows carry `state_note` totalling 16,746 bytes, plus 17,966 bytes of `witness` -- **34,712 invisible against 1,125,256 bytes of rendered entry notes, so 3.0% of ledger prose**. *I was about to write `a large fraction of today's analysis is invisible`; the measurement says 3%, and the honest statement is that it is SMALL IN TOTAL and CONCENTRATED IN TODAY'S ROWS* -- the six largest `state_note` fields (`probetimebased` 4779, `killartifacts` 3108, `lethalonly` 2573, `killdriverunTracked` 1851, `sectionEnarrow` 1635, this row 1186) were all written this session. So the proportion is rising precisely because the field is where a round's own reasoning now goes. FIX unchanged and now cheaper to justify: render both fields in the row detail, or add a checker that refuses a field whose claim a later entry corrects. *The second is still the stronger one -- it is what caught the `asicbadaddr` witness field under-rating the row a ranking put FIRST.*  *** SHIPS ON SINGLE-SEAT REVIEW, AND THAT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN LEFT IMPLICIT. *** A completeness check named the sharpest form of a gap I had only half-seen: THREE things enter the permanent record of #462 having been reviewed by the adjudicating seat ALONE -- (1) `check_cited_artifacts.py` and its precommit section X, (2) the clause-(e) contract going into the probe's docstring, and (3) the POLICY that kill-table logs are tracked in-repo forever (228 KB and growing every round). All three appeared AFTER pass 2, so the nine-seat panel that reviewed the fix and the diff never saw any of them, and the owner's standing FULL-PANEL-ON-EVERY-STAGE directive was quietly not applied to the parts that arrived late. **The seven held seats are currently pointed at `probetimebased` and `asicbadaddr` research, not at these.** OWED WHEN THEY FIRE, one added question: *is tracking every round's kill logs in-repo the right durability choice, and does the section-X gate over-reach?* They are firing anyway, so the marginal cost is a paragraph. *Recording this is not a substitute for asking -- it is what stops a policy and two mechanisms reading, later, as though a panel had weighed them.*"
