ch-7 Fable adjudication, which also notes that ANY device interrupt after the arming store kills the host on a running guest).  OPTION SET -- batch-7 Fable adjudication RULING (2026-09-05 ~22:40): (a) ALONE -- bound the reassert loop to `d->ncpus' captured at DEVINIT (house idiom dev_mp.c:134/:191 and this file's own RESET_CPU_ALL arm `i < cpu->machine->ncpus'); (b) declining stores to absent slots is REJECTED for this round (an unsourced board-model claim, changes guest-visible readback, reddens two shipped es438 rows D4/E2) and FILED as the question row lunaabsentmaskdecl; (c) a no-op connection fabricates plumbing with no in-tree idiom; (e) a NULL check guards the symptom.  Detector: W1 kill row + the connected-slot control + a `-n 2' ACCEPT row whose channel is cpu 1 ASSERTING (irq_asserted / vector entry), not survival; PRETABLE mutants `cpu <= d->ncpus', ncpus never assigned (slot 0 never asserted: the boot gate reds), `ncpus = 1' hardcoded.  Wire luna_absentcpu_probe.py into gate_m88k_rounding.sh.  Also owed in that round: es438cells (MASK2/MASK3 readback rows).  Ranked HIGH: guest-reachable process death by an ordinary instruction on the machine the project boots (precedent: pcc2defaultkill, m88kexcdeliver ranked on reachability, not on a known driver doing it).  Provenance: retrospective batch 7 -- GPT-6 Astra (codex, max effort, read-only) predicted it from the source ('interrupt delivery can still reach an unconnected CPU callback'); the main loop built and ran the witness the same evening; Fable 5.1 adjudication of batch 7 in flight.  The fix round runs the full nine-seat panel.",
