regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass1\panel_20260905_143211\ollama_glm.txt:1:{"model": "glm-5.3", "created_at": "2026-09-05T19:38:13.501508752Z", "response": "", "thinking": "Let me work through this brief carefully. I'm a packet-fed seat \u2014 I cannot read files. I must audit the premises as stated, from the inlined text alone, and identify where derivations don't follow from the given facts.\n\nThe verdict line first. Let me figure out what the verdict should be.\n\nThe core claims:\n\n1. **The fallback**: `m88k_exception()` marks only RESET/INTERRUPT/INSTRUCTION_ACCESS/DATA_ACCESS as `implemented = true`. Nine other switch cases print VERBOSITY_ERROR and break. Then `if (vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented)` prints unimplemented, sets `running = false` on every CPU, returns.\n\nLet me count the cases in the inlined function:\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_RESET \u2192 implemented = true\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_INTERRUPT \u2192 implemented = true\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS \u2192 implemented = true\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS \u2192 implemented = true\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_MISALIGNED_ACCESS \u2192 break (not implemented)\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE \u2192 break\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION \u2192 break\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION \u2192 break\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE \u2192 break\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_INTEGER_OVERFLOW \u2192 break\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_ERROR \u2192 break\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_SFU1_PRECISE \u2192 break\n- M88K_EXCEPTION_SFU1_IMPRECISE \u2192 break\n- case 0x80 / case 0x1c2 (syscall) \u2192 break (not implemented, but vector \u2265 M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START presumably \u2014 trap vectors start at 128 = 0x80, so these are above the threshold)\n- MVMEPROM_VECTOR \u2192 break\n- default \u2192 prints \"unknown vector\"\n\nSo the count of \"nine other switch cases print VERBOSITY_ERROR and break\": MISALIGNED, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE, PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION, BOUNDS_CHECK, ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE, INTEGER_OVERFLOW, ERROR, SFU1_PRECISE, SFU1_IMPRECISE = nine. That checks out: 9 cases print VERBOSITY_ERROR and break. Good.\n\nWait, but are these \"nine other\" in addition to the four implemented? Yes: 4 implemented + 9 VERBOSITY_ERROR + syscall (VERBOSITY_INFO) + MVMEPROM (VERBOSITY_INFO) + default. The brief says \"Nine other switch cases print a VERBOSITY_ERROR debugmsg and break\" \u2014 count is 9. READ: verified by enumerating the inlined function.\n\n2. **The claim about the tail `default:` arm**: The brief's key premise is that setting `implemented = true` alone (D2) reaches the tail switch's `default:` which prints \"internal error\" and sets `cpu->running = false`. Let me check: the tail switch is `if (vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START) { switch (vector) { case RESET...; case INTERRUPT...; case INSTRUCTION_ACCESS...; case DATA_ACCESS...; #if 0 blocks...; default: internal error; running = false } }`.\n\nSo yes \u2014 if the five vectors pass the fallback (implemented = true), they take the common path, then enter the vector-specific switch, and land in `default:` \u2192 \"internal error\", `cpu->running = false`. That stops THIS cpu only (not all CPUs \u2014 note the difference: the fallback loop sets `running = false` on every CPU; the tail default sets only `cpu->running = false`). On single-CPU testm88k it's the same observable halt. The brief acknowledges this distinction implicitly (\"sets THIS cpu's running = false\"). Good \u2014 that's consistent.\n\nWait, one subtlety: the `#if 0` blocks. In the inlined code, the `#if 0` case for ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE is inside the tail switch \u2014 but it's compiled out. And the SFU1_PRECISE `#if 0` block too. So compiled code has only RESET, INTERRUPT, INSTRUCTION_ACCESS, DATA_ACCESS cases plus default. Yes.\n\n3. **D1**: set `implemented = true` for the five in the first switch AND add the five as case labels with bare `break` in the tail switch. Does that deliver? Let's trace: vector = e.g. 8 (ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE). First switch: case \u2192 implemented = true (after fix). Falls through the fallback check. Common path: SFRZ not set (first exception), so update_shadow_regs = true, EPSR = PSR, set PSR with SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE. Shadow regs: SSBR = 0, SXIP = pc | V; not delay slot \u2192 SNIP = (pc+4) | V, SFIP = SNIP+4. Then `cpu->pc = VBR + 8*vector`. Then delay_slot = NOT_DELAYED. DMT/DMD/DMA zeroed. Then tail switch: with the new case labels, bare break \u2014 no adjustment. Then `m88k_pc_to_pointers(cpu)`. Done. The exception is delivered: pc = 8*vector (VBR=0), SXIP = faulting pc | V, SNIP = pc+4 | V. That matches the manual contract as stated in \u00a74: SXIP = faulting instruction, rte resumes at SNIP = next instruction.\n\nSo the reading seems right: D1 delivers, D2 halts (with \"internal error\" instead of \"unimplemented\").\n\n4. **\"rte resumes at SNIP\" contract for all five**: The manual quotes say for misaligned, unimplemented opcode, privilege violation, integer-divide: SXIP points to faulting instruction, handler can emulate/discard, resume... The 8983-8985 quote: \"an rte instruction resumes execution at the instruction pointed to by the SNIP.\" And 9030-9032: \"The instruction pointed to by the SNIP is the first instruction to execute after the return.\"\n\nHmm, wait. There's a subtle issue. For precise exceptions where the handler should RE-EXECUTE the faulting instruction (e.g., a page fault emulation \u2014 but these aren't page faults). For the five trap-like classes:\n\n- Integer divide: the destination is not changed, scoreboard bit set. The manual's question in the `#if 0`: \"Is it correct to continue on the instruction after the division by zero? Or should the PC be backed up one step?\" The manual quote at 10300-10305 says SXIP points to the instruction that caused the exception. Does it say what SNIP is? Not quoted. Hmm. For precise exceptions in general on 88100, when an exception is taken, the pipelined state: SXIP = faulting, SNIP = faulting+4? Actually on MC88100, for a precise exception, the hardware sets SXIP to the faulting instruction, SNIP and SFIP to subsequent. The rte resumes at SNIP. So the handler that wants to retry would modify SNIP (set SNIP = SXIP) before rte. The handler that wants to skip (e.g., emulate and continue) leaves SNIP alone. So the contract \"SNIP = pc+4, handler adjusts if it wants retry\" is correct architecture: the hardware's job is to set SNIP to the next instruction; the handler decides. Actually wait \u2014 is that right? Let me think about MC88100 precise exceptions more carefully.\n\nOn MC88100, exceptions: when a precise exception occurs, the shadow registers capture: SXIP = the excepting instruction's address (with V bit), SNIP = the address of the next instruction to be executed (typically excepting+4, but in delay-slot situations more complex), SFIP = the next prefetch. The manual says \"an rte instruction resumes execution at the instruction pointed to by the SNIP.\" So for a divide-by-zero where the handler emulates the divide (e.g., to support divide-by-zero semantics or fcr-based emulation), the handler would do the operation and then rte with SNIP = SXIP+4 (skip the faulting instruction) \u2014 which is what hardware already set. Or if the handler wants to retry the instruction, it sets SNIP = SXIP. So hardware setting SNIP = faulting+4 is correct; the `#if 0` doubt (\"should the PC be backed up one step?\") is resolved: no, hardware points SNIP at the next instruction; the handler does the backing up if it wants. Hmm, but wait \u2014 actually let me double-check: for a precise exception where the instruction did NOT complete, does the hardware set SNIP to the faulting instruction or the next one?\n\nThe manual quote 9030-9032: \"4. Instruction execution resumes after the instruction corresponding to NIP is prefetched. The instruction pointed to by the SNIP is the first instruction to execute after the return.\" That's about rte mechanics. And 8979: \"If an instruction causes a precise exception, the SXIP points to that instruction.\"\n\nHmm, actually there's an important subtlety: for precise exceptions, on the real MC88100, the SNIP is set to the address of the instruction FOLLOWING the excepting instruction... I believe that's right for most exceptions, but for INSTRUCTION_ACCESS and DATA_ACCESS the handler typically retries by copying SXIP to SNIP. The code in the tail switch for INSTRUCTION_ACCESS zeroes SNIP/SFIP unless in delay slot \u2014 interesting, that suggests the fork's rte implementation uses \"First try the NIP, if it is Valid\" \u2014 i.e., rte resumes at SNIP if V set; if SNIP is zeroed (invalid), it falls to... hmm, actually the code comment says \"we want to re-execute the instruction in SXIP, not SNIP/SFIP\" \u2014 so zeroing SNIP/SFIP makes rte fall back to SXIP? That implies the fork's rte: try NIP; if not valid, try XIP. OK.\n\nFor the five trap-like classes, the manual says handlers typically emulate or discard. The brief's premise \"rte resumes at SNIP, SNIP = pc+4\" is the hardware behavior. Let me sanity check with the integer-divide: \"The destination register is not changed, but the scoreboard bit is set for the destination register.\" If the handler is OpenBSD's, on divide by zero it delivers SIGFPE to the user process and the process either dies or longjmps \u2014 the kernel doesn't rte back into the faulting context in the same way... Actually for a user process that catches SIGFPE and returns, the return goes back via sigreturn to the instruction after? No \u2014 the kernel sets the context to resume... hmm, this is guest-behavior detail beyond the packet. The key point: hardware sets SNIP = next instruction; handler may adjust. So D1's \"no SNIP/SFIP adjustment: the manual's rte-at-SNIP semantics are the common path's defaults\" is architecturally right. But wait \u2014 there's a catch: the manual says for the divide handler emulating the divide: fcr2/fcr4 contain source operands, fcr5 the destination register number. If the handler wants to emulate (e.g., produce a result and skip), it writes the destination and rtes with SNIP as-is. Fine.\n\nHmm, but actually let me reconsider: is SNIP really faulting+4 for precise exceptions on MC88100? For data access exceptions... The 88100 pipeline: XIP, NIP, FIP. On an exception, the hardware captures SXIP = the excepting instruction, and SNIP = the next sequential instruction... For most precise exceptions, yes. Actually there's nuance: for exceptions detected at different pipeline stages, SNIP may be set differently, and the manual says the exception handler should use SXIP. But the brief's claim is conditioned on manual quotes; the quotes given don't explicitly say \"SNIP = faulting+4 for these five.\" They say SXIP = faulting, rte resumes at SNIP. The inference \"SNIP = pc+4 | V is what hardware does\" is a REASONED premise that is standard 88100 behavior (the shadow pipeline: when exception taken, SNIP points to the next instruction to be executed after the excepting one). I recall from the 88100 manual: \"the SNIP contains the address of the next instruction to be executed after the instruction that caused the exception\" \u2014 something like that. I'll accept it as architecturally standard but note it's the one link not directly quoted; the brief itself flags INTEGER_OVERFLOW as \"not re-extracted... treat as premise to CONDITION on.\" Actually the brief flags only 6.6 Integer Overflow as not re-extracted. The SNIP=+4 for the five is claimed as \"the common path's defaults\" matching the manual. I'll note the gap: the quotes establish SXIP=faulting and rte-at-SNIP, but none of the quoted lines literally states SNIP = SXIP+4 for these classes; the derivation relies on the general exception model. It's very likely right (that's how the 88100 works), but from the packet alone it's conditioned.\n\nActually hold on \u2014 there IS a potential counter-consideration: for UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE and PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION, if the handler DISCARDS the instruction (manual: \"or can discard the instruction\"), it rtes with SNIP unchanged = +4. If it EMULATES, also +4 (it did the work). If it wants to RETRY after fixing state, it sets SNIP=SXIP. All handler choices; hardware's +4 is right. For MISALIGNED: \"The exception handler can emulate the memory access in software or can discard the instruction\" \u2014 same. OK so D1's contract is right.\n\n5. **The encodings.** Let me check these carefully \u2014 this is an arithmetic/enumeration claim and the brief asks me to check.\n\nTriadic form: `0xf4000000 | D<<21 | S1<<16 | sub<<8 | S2`. Calibrated on exemplar `or r0,r0,r0` = f4005800. Let's verify: or is sub-op 0x58 in the 0x3d major opcode? MC88100: major opcode 0x3d (111101) is the \"arithmetic/logical triadic\" form: bits 31-26 = 0x3d \u2192 0xf4000000 base. D at bits 25-21, S1 at 20-16, sub-opcode at bits 15-8? Wait, let me check: the standard 88100 triadic encoding: bits 31-26 = 0x3d, bits 25-21 = D, bits 20-16 = S1, bits 15-10 = 6-bit sub-opcode?? Hmm. Actually MC88100: for the X-form/triadic instructions, the opcode field is bits 31-26, D bits 25-21, S1 bits 20-16, and then a 10-bit...? Let me recall `or r0,r0,r0` = 0xf4005800. Bits: 0xf4005800 = 1111 0100 0000 0000 0101 1000 0000 0000. Bits 31-26: 111101 = 0x3d. D (25-21) = 00000. S1 (20-16) = 00000. Bits 15-8 = 0x58 = 01011000. S2 (4-0) = 00000. So sub-op at bits 15-8 = 0x58 for `or`. And the decoder cited: `switch ((iword >> 8) & 0xff)` under `case 0x3d` \u2014 consistent. Good, so sub-op is an 8-bit field at bits 15-8. Hmm wait, actually in the real 88100 manual the triadic sub-opcode is 8 bits at bits 15-8? The real encoding: bits 15-8 for extended opcode... I believe the 88100 uses a 6-bit? Let me just check with known encodings: `addu r9,r0,r10`... addu sub-op 0x64? Hmm. Known: `or` = 0x58? In the real MC88100, `or` extended opcode is 0x58? The 88100 instruction set: add.co (0x58 is or?). Actually let me not fight this \u2014 the brief calibrates on the exemplar, which is self-consistent: if `or r0,r0,r0` = f4005800 with sub at bits 15-8, then 0x58 = or. Fine. The calibration is internal to the packet. The risk flagged: \"a wrong field yields a different instruction.\"\n\nNow check `divu r2,r3,r0` = f4436800 (sub 0x68). Compute: 0xf4000000 | (2<<21) | (3<<16) | (0x68<<8) | 0. 2<<21 = 0x00400000. 3<<16 = 0x00030000. 0x68<<8 = 0x00006800. Sum: 0xf4000000 + 0x00400000 + 0x00030000 + 0x00006800 = 0xf4436800. \u2713. D=2 (r2 destination), S1=3 (r3 dividend), S2=0 (r0 divisor = 0 \u2192 divide by zero). Wait \u2014 the div0 row: `divu r2,r3,r0` with divisor r0 = 0. The probe seeds registers with `rN=` \u2014 presumably r0 is hardwired zero on 88100 (r0 is hardwired to zero in MC88100 \u2014 yes, r0 always reads zero). So divisor = 0. \u2713. divu sub-op 0x68: in the real 88100, divu = 0x68? I recall divu is 0x68... plausible (div = 0x60? divu = 0x68? hmm, actually I think div=0x60, divu=0x68? or div=0x64...). Can't verify from packet beyond calibration consistency; the `live` row uses the same encoding with nonzero divisor and gets 6, which validates the encoding end-to-end (the instruction decoded as divu). Good \u2014 the control covers it.\n\n`add r2,r3,r4` = f4437004 (sub 0x70). Compute: 0xf4000000 | (2<<21) | (3<<16) | (0x70<<8) | 4 = 0xf4000000 + 0x00400000 + 0x00030000 + 0x00007000 + 0x4 = 0xf4437004. \u2713. r3=0x7fffffff, r4=1 \u2192 signed overflow \u2192 `d != dx` in X(add) \u2192 INTEGER_OVERFLOW raise. The claim \"r3=0x7fffffff, r4=1 \u2192 d != dx\" \u2014 the probe seeds r3/r4 via `rN=`. Hmm, but the ovf row's HALT output shows vector=0x009 (INTEGER_OVERFLOW) \u2014 consistent with vector 9. \u2713.\n\n`ld r2,r3,0` = 14430000. The brief says \"opcode 0x15 form\". MC88100 ld: bits 31-26 = 0x15? 0x14430000: 0001 0100 0100 0011 ... bits 31-26 = 000101 = 0x05?? Wait: 0x14430000 = binary 0001 0100 0100 0011 0000 0000 0000 0000. Bits 31-26 = 000101 = 0x05. Hmm, but the brief says \"opcode 0x15 form\". 0x15 in hex = 21 decimal; bits 31-26 = 000101b = 5. So \"opcode 0x15\" doesn't match bits 31-26 = 5 unless they mean the top 6 bits as 0x05... or they mean bits 31-27? Hmm. Actually MC88100 memory access instructions: ld is major opcode 0x05 (000101) \u2014 hmm wait. Let me recall: 88100 ld rD,rS1,rS2 (address = S1+S2): opcode 000101 (0x05), D at 25-21, S1 at 20-16, then bits 15-10 = 0x14?? Hmm, the ld has a \"memory opcode extension\" in bits 15-10 or 15-9...\n\n0x14430000: D = bits 25-21: 0x14430000 = 0001 0100 0100 0011 ... Let me be careful. 0x14430000 in binary: 0x1=0001, 0x4=0100, 0x4=0100, 0x3=0011, 0x0,0x0,0x0,0x0. So bits 31-28 = 0001, bits 27-24 = 0100, bits 23-20 = 0100, bits 19-16 = 0011. Full 32 bits: 0001 0100 0100 0011 0000 0000 0000 0000.\n\nBits 31-26 = 000101 = 5. Bits 25-21 = 00 010 = 2 (D = r2 \u2713). Bits 20-16 = 0 0011 = 3 (S1 = r3 \u2713). Bits 15-10 = 000000, bits 9-5 = 0, bits 4-0 = 0 (S2 = r0 \u2192 offset 0 \u2713). The \"0x15\" the brief mentions: \"opcode 0x15 form\" \u2014 0x14430000 top byte is 0x14, and the top 6 bits are 0x05; maybe they mean the top 6 bits = 5 and the next... hmm, \"opcode 0x15\" would be 6-bit value 21 = 010101, which is NOT 000101. But maybe they mean bits 31-27 (5 bits) = 00010 = 2? No. Hmm, \"0x15\" as a hexadecimal reading of the high nibble+? The high byte is 0x14, high 6 bits = 0b000101 = 0x05. So the brief's \"opcode 0x15\" is... the top byte 0x14 plus bit 25-21? Actually wait: 0x14430000 \u2014 maybe they meant the opcode field is bits 31-26 = 0x05 and there's a sub-extension field. Actually in MC88100, load/store instructions have opcode bits 31-26 = 000101 (5) for ld? Hmm, no: 88100 memory references: ld = 000101? Let me recall actual 88100 encodings from OpenBSD/gas: `ld r2,r3,r4` assembles to 0x14430400? The pattern 0x14xxxxxx for ld with register indirect: I believe ld rD,rS1,rS2 = 0x05000000-ish... Hmm, actually from GXemul's m88k decoder (cpu_m88k.c): case 0x14: ld? Hmm. Wait \u2014 actually I recall the 88100 ld encoding is 0x84?? No. Let me think about known 88100 words: `br` = 0xc3ffffff for branch-always to self: br encoding: opcode 0x0c?? Hmm: 0xc3ffffff: top 6 bits = 110000 = 0x30. Hmm, br is major opcode 0x30 with condition in bits... 0xc3ffffff: bits 31-26 = 110000 (0x30), bits 25-21 = 00011 (3), bits 20-16 = 11111 (31), bits 15-5... br has a 16-bit displacement (bits 15-0) = 0xffff and a 5-bit debug trap field (bits 20-16). br = opcode 110000, condition 00011 (always), D field? Hmm, actually the br format: bits 31-26 = 110000, bits 25-21 = 5-bit condition? no wait.\n\n88100 br: \"br.x\" mnemonic; encoding: 31-26 opcode (110000), 25-21 = 5-bit debug trap vector? Hmm I don't remember exactly. 0xc3ffffff = 1100 0011 1111 1111 1111 1111 1111 1111. If bits 31-26 = 110000, bits 25-21 = 00111 = 7?? Wait: bits 31-26: take the top 6 bits: 110000 (0x30). Then bits 25-21 = next 5: 0 0011 = 3. Hmm, 0xc3 = 1100 0011. bits 31-26 = 110000, bits 25-21 = 00011 = 3. Hmm br's condition field... br (unconditional) condition = 3 (\"always\")? In 88100 bcnd/br conditional codes: 2 = equal, 3 = always? Yes! bcnd with condition 00011 is \"always\" \u2014 br is encoded as bcnd-like with cond=3? Hmm no. Actually in 88100: `br` = \"br always\" and its condition code in bits 25-21 is 00011 (3)? Hmm \u2014 I recall the conditional branch bcnd has a 5-bit condition field where 00010 = eq0, 00011 = always... and br is encoded with the same \"always\" code. The 16-bit displacement 0xffff = -1 \u2192 branch to self (pc + 4*(-1)? or pc-4?). `br .` branches to itself: displacement -1 from... The 88100 br displacement is relative to the delay-slot/next instruction? br .: if displacement counts from pc+4, then -1 \u2192 pc. 0xffff = -1 \u2192 target = (pc+4) + (-1)*4 = pc. \u2713 self-loop. Fine \u2014 internal consistency holds; I can't fully verify against the real ISA from the packet, but the calibration control (`live` row executes divu correctly and gets 6) validates the divu encoding; the add encoding is same-form; the ld encoding is validated by the misalign row halting with vector 4 (MISALIGNED) \u2014 which confirms it decoded as ld with r3=0x20001 misaligned (0x20001 & 3 = 1 \u2260 0). Hmm wait \u2014 0x20001 & 3 = 1. \u2713 misaligned. And pc=0x10004 matches the test instruction at 0x10004. The output shows vector=0x004 for misalign \u2713, 0x008 for div0 \u2713, 0x009 for ovf \u2713.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 one thing to check: the div0 row. If PSR at reset has FPU ENABLED (SFD1 clear), then `divu` executes in the integer unit... wait, on MC88100, integer divide is done by the... hmm. Actually the MC88100's divide instructions are implemented in the integer unit? No wait \u2014 MC88100: `div` and `divu` are implemented via the floating-point unit?! Hmm. Actually yes! On the MC88100, integer division (div, divu) is executed by the FPU (SFU1)! The 88100 integer unit does not have a divider; div/divu are dispatched to SFU1. Hmm, but then a divide-by-zero raises... the manual says integer-divide error exception (vector 8) occurs when divisor is zero \u2014 and this exception is reported... if the FPU is disabled (SFD1 set), div/divu raise SFU1 precise exception (vector... SFU1 unimplemented? no \u2014 SFU1_PRECISE = vector 0x0? hmm).\n\nHold on. Let me think. MC88100: div/divu are SFU1 (FPU) instructions? Actually no \u2014 I recall that on MC88100, `div`/`divu` are executed by the floating-point unit as \"integer divide\" ops, yes. And when SFU1 is disabled (SFD1 set in PSR), executing div/divu causes an \"SFU1 precise exception\" (vector 0x04?? no...). Hmm. Actually MC88100 exception vectors: 0x00 reset, 0x08 interrupt, 0x10 instruction access, 0x18 data access, 0x20 misaligned, 0x28 unimplemented opcode, 0x30 privilege violation, 0x38 bounds check, 0x40 integer divide, 0x48 integer overflow, 0x50 error, 0x58?? SFU1 precise = 0x58? Hmm, or SFU1 precise = vector 0x04?? Hmm, I don't think that's right either.\n\nGXemul's M88K_EXCEPTION enum values (from the brief's vector claims): MISALIGNED=4 (0x20 offset \u2192 vector number 4 since offset = 8*vector \u2192 0x20/8 = 4 \u2713), UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE=5 (0x28/8=5 \u2713), PRIVILEGE=6 (0x30/8=6 \u2713), BOUNDS=7 (0x38/8=7 \u2713), ILLEGAL_DIVIDE=8 (0x40/8=8 \u2713), OVERFLOW=9 (0x48/8=9 \u2713), ERROR=10 (0x50/8=10 \u2713). And SFU1 precise would be 11 (0x58) or so, USER_TRAPS_START = 128 (0x80). Consistent.\n\nNow the SFD1 question: the brief says PSR at reset = MODE | IND, so SFD1 clear \u2192 FPU enabled \u2192 divu executes \u2192 divide by zero \u2192 ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE (vector 8). The probe output confirms: div0 row shows vector=0x008, not an SFU1 vector. And the \"attrib\" row: same divu with non-zero divisor \u2192 DONE, r2=1... wait, the attrib row shows r2=0x00000001 with \"the SAME divu as div0 with a non-zero divisor\". Hmm, but the div0 row's encoding is `divu r2,r3,r0` (S2 = r0). If attrib uses \"the same divu... with a non-zero divisor\", it must seed r0?? But r0 is hardwired zero on 88100! You can't seed r0 to nonzero \u2014 writes to r0 are discarded. Hmm. Wait: \"the SAME divu as div0 with a non-zero divisor\" \u2014 if the encoding is divu r2,r3,r0, the divisor is r0 which is always 0. So \"a non-zero divisor\" is impossible with that encoding unless they changed the encoding. Contradiction? Let me re-read.\n\n\"M88KEXC_ROW=attrib RESULT=DONE pc=0x00010008 r2=0x00000001 (the SAME divu as div0 with a non-zero divisor: ATTRIBUTION control)\"\n\nHmm. r2=1. If divu r2,r3,r0 with r3 = ? and r0 = 0 \u2192 divide by zero \u2192 should HALT. But it's DONE with r2=1. So either: (a) the encoding differs (e.g., divu r2,r3,r1 with r1 seeded nonzero), or (b) ... The `live` row is `divu r2,r3,r4` with r3=0x60, r4=0x10 \u2192 6. The div0 row is `divu r2,r3,r0` (f4436800, S2 field = 0). For attrib to be \"the SAME divu as div0 with a non-zero divisor\" \u2014 same encoding f4436800 means S2 = r0 = 0 always. Unless the probe seeds r0 (which can't work \u2014 r0 hardwired). OR \"the same divu\" means same instruction *template* (divu r2,r3,rX) with X\u22600 \u2014 i.e., a different S2 field. Then r2 = r3 / rX = 1 implies e.g. r3 = 0x10, divisor 0x10, or r3=1, divisor=1. But then it's not \"the same divu as div0\" literally.\n\nHmm wait, maybe \"the SAME divu as div0\" refers to same opcode/mnemonic but different register operands \u2014 i.e., the attribution control is meant to show that a divu that doesn't fault completes normally, so the div0 HALT is attributable to the zero divisor, not to divu generally. But the phrase \"the SAME divu as div0\" is loose. There's a real tension: if the encoding is byte-identical (f4436800), the divisor is r0 = 0 and the row could not DONE. Unless... the fork does NOT hardwire r0 to zero! That's a possibility: if the fork treats r0 as an ordinary register (a bug or a deliberate simplification), then seeding r0=0x10 via `r0=0x10` would make divu r2,r3,r0 \u2192 r3/r0 = quotient 1 if r3 = 0x10, divisor 0x10 \u2192 1. r2=1 \u2713. Hmm! But then the div0 row: r0 seeded to 0 (default?) \u2192 divisor 0 \u2192 HALT \u2713. And the liveness marker `addu r9,r0,r10` \u2014 uses r0 as source; if r0 were seeded nonzero it would still be fine.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the 88100 DOES hardwire r0. If the fork's `rN=` seeding wrote r0, a correct fork would discard it. The brief says \"registers seeded with `rN=`\". If attrib seeds r0 and the fork models r0 as hardwired, then divisor stays 0 \u2192 HALT, not DONE. But the output shows DONE. So either the fork doesn't hardwire r0 (a separate fork bug/premise!), or attrib uses a different S2 register and the brief's \"the SAME divu\" is imprecise wording.\n\nHmm, this is exactly the kind of premise-audit catch the panel wants. Let me note it: the attrib row as described is internally suspect. Either (a) it's not literally the same encoding (S2 \u2260 0), in which case \"the SAME divu as div0\" is wrong/misleading and the control is fine, or (b) it IS the same encoding and the fork must not hardwire r0 \u2014 which would itself be a notable premise (and would make the div0 row's r0-divisor depend on r0 reading as 0, i.e., the fork DOES hardwire r0 \u2014 contradiction!). Wait: if the fork hardwires r0=0, div0 row works (divisor 0 \u2192 HALT \u2713) but attrib cannot produce r2=1 with the same encoding (divisor still 0 \u2192 HALT, contradiction with DONE). If the fork does NOT hardwire r0, then attrib works only if r0 seeded nonzero, but then div0 requires r0 seeded zero \u2014 possible (seed r0=0 explicitly or default 0). Both are consistent-ish, but (b) implies the fork lacks r0-hardwiring, which contradicts 88100 architecture and would be a separate finding. Actually hmm \u2014 does GXemul hardwire r0? In GXemul's m88k, I believe writes to r0 are... I recall `if (reg != 0) cpu->cd.m88k.r[reg] = value`-style guards in many places. Can't check. The packet doesn't say.\n\nActually wait, re-read: \"(the SAME divu as div0 with a non-zero divisor: ATTRIBUTION control)\". Maybe \"the same divu\" = same instruction word except S2 field changed to a seeded register. That's the charitable read: the control isolates the divisor as the fault-attribution variable. The row's purpose: show the halt is caused by the zero divisor, not by the divu encoding being an unimplemented opcode. Fine. But \"SAME\" is imprecise; the DETECTOR section says \"attrib DONE r2=1\" \u2014 if the encoding differs from div0's, the probe script presumably plants a different word. Minor wording defect, worth flagging as an ambiguity that changes what the control proves: if literally the same word (f4436800), the row contradicts itself (r0 can't be nonzero on a hardwired-r0 core). The single fact to settle it: the actual instruction word planted in the attrib row (is S2 field zero?). Name it.\n\nHmm wait, actually, hold on. Let me reconsider: maybe the fork seeds r0 and the fork does NOT enforce hardwired r0. Let me look at the probe outputs for evidence: `live` row: `divu r2,r3,r4` with r3=0x60, r4=0x10 \u2192 6 \u2713. That uses r4, not r0, so it doesn't tell us about r0 hardwiring. The div0 row uses r0 as divisor \u2014 on ANY correct m88k, divisor reads 0 regardless of seeding. So div0's HALT is attributable regardless. The attrib row is the only one that needs r0\u22600 \u2014 and only if the encoding is literally the same. So the ambiguity is contained. But also: is `divu r2,r3,r0` even a sensible way to write div-by-zero? Yes \u2014 canonical: divide by r0 (the zero register). Good design. And attrib then should be `divu r2,r3,rN` for some seeded N \u2014 \"the same divu\" meaning the same mnemonics class. OK. Flag as a wording/premise ambiguity with the settle-fact: the S2 field of the attrib row's planted word, and whether the fork hardwires r0 (grep anchor: writes to r[] guarded by reg!=0?).\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually there's another reading! \"the SAME divu as div0\" \u2014 maybe div0's encoding is NOT divu r2,r3,r0. Let me recompute f4436800: S2 field = bits 4-0 = 0x00 & 0x1f = 0. 0x6800: bits 15-8 = 0x68, bits 7-0 = 0x00 \u2192 S2 = 0. Yes, r0. OK.\n\nAnd r2=0xdeadbeef in the fault rows: \"r2=0xdeadbeef halted=True\" \u2014 r2 seeded to 0xdeadbeef, and after HALT r2 unchanged \u2014 consistent with \"destination register is not changed\" (manual) and with the exception path never executing the divu's writeback. Also consistent with the fallback path (which returns before touching r2). Note: r2 unchanged does NOT distinguish \"exception delivered, operation not performed\" from \"halted before execute\" \u2014 in both cases r2 keeps 0xdeadbeef. For the DELIVER rows of the detector, \"r2 unchanged\" + \"machine still RUNNING\" + pc == 8*vector is the delivered signature. OK.\n\nNow \u2014 the detector's delivered-row assertions: \"pc == 8*vector, SXIP == 0x10004 | V, PSR has SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE, r2 unchanged, and the machine still RUNNING\". Check the common path: after delivery, cpu->pc = VBR + 8*vector = 0 + 8*8 = 0x40 \u2713 (div0 @0x40). SXIP = cpu->pc | V where cpu->pc at raise time = 0x10004 \u2713 (the faulting instruction address \u2014 the raise happens at the test instruction; note the liveness marker at 0x10000 executed first). SNIP = 0x10008 | V. PSR: stcr with SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE \u2713 (matches the D1 delivered PSR). SSBR = 0. DMT0.. zeroed \u2713 (manual: misaligned detected before dispatch \u2014 DMT0=0 correct for precise-before-dispatch; also correct for the other four).\n\nWait \u2014 one wrinkle: SFD1. The common path sets SFD1 (disable FPU) on exception entry \u2014 that's architecturally correct (88100 sets SFD1 on exception entry? Hmm \u2014 actually the 88100 PSR on exception entry: the hardware disables the FPU (SFD1 set) on exception entry? I believe yes: on exceptions, SFD1 is set? Hmm, actually I'm not sure the hardware sets SFD1 on exception entry. The 88100 sets IND (disable interrupts) and MODE (supervisor), and... SFD1? Hmm. I don't remember. The fork does set it. The detector asserts it. Whatever the architecture, the fork's behavior is asserted; the manual quotes don't cover SFD1-on-entry. It's fine as a detector assertion of the fork's own behavior; but if a hygiene row compares to hardware, SFD1-on-entry might diverge. Not decidable from packet. Actually \u2014 hmm, I recall the 88100 exception entry does NOT change SFD1; the EPSR/PSR: \"the PSR is set to: IND=1, MODE=1, SFRZ=1(? no...)\". Hmm. Actually SFRZ \u2014 the shadow freeze bit \u2014 hmm, on exception entry the hardware... I genuinely don't remember whether real hardware sets SFRZ on entry. In the fork, SFRZ is set (via m88k_stcr with SFRZ). Hmm, in real 88100, exception entry sets: EPSR = PSR; PSR.SFRZ? No wait \u2014 SFRZ freezes the shadow registers; on exception entry the shadow regs are UPDATED (captured), and... the SFRZ bit freezes shadow updates for subsequent exceptions. Real hardware sets SFRZ=1 on exception entry so a second exception doesn't clobber the shadow state. Yes, I believe that's right (hence the ERROR re-route for double fault with SFRZ set). OK, plausible; not packet-decidable beyond the fork's own code, which is inlined. Fine.\n\n6. **The raise-site census**: The grep-derivation claims counts: SFU1_PRECISE 58, PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION 10, ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE 4, INTEGER_OVERFLOW 3, MISALIGNED 1, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE 1. And BOUNDS/ERROR/SFU1_IMPRECISE zero. I can't check the tree. But internal consistency: the scope says \"five trap-like classes\" = PRIV, DIVIDE, OVERFLOW, MISALIGNED, UNIMPLEMENTED \u2014 all with \u22651 raise site \u2713. SFU1 separate \u2713. ERROR \"only the internal re-route\" \u2014 the inlined function shows `vector = M88K_EXCEPTION_ERROR;` re-route inside m88k_exception itself \u2713 (so \"no raise site\" is consistent with the re-route existing). BOUNDS: \"tbnd is disassembler-only\" \u2014 claimed, not checkable from packet; also Q-D1b. The census counts themselves: not checkable from packet \u2014 name that premise. The grep anchor given is plausible. One thing I CAN check: the enumeration domain. The deriver greps `src/cpus/cpu_m88k*.c` \u2014 covers cpu_m88k.c and cpu_m88k_diagnostics? etc. But m88k_exception is also (presumably) called from... device code? The grep restricts to cpu_m88k*.c \u2014 if a device or the dyntrans/other core file raises m88k_exception, the census would miss it. GXemul devices might call cpu->... exception? Unlikely for m88k. Not decidable; minor.\n\nAlso: the census claims PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION 10 raise sites \"(ldcr/stcr/xcr/rte/ld.usr/st.usr/tb0/tb1/tcnd in user mode)\" \u2014 that lists ~9 instruction kinds: ldcr, stcr, xcr, rte, ld.usr, st.usr, tb0, tb1, tcnd = 9 kinds, 10 sites (one kind has 2 sites presumably). Plausible. Not checkable.\n\nBut here's a real premise-check on PRIVILEGE: \"trap vectors 0-127 in user mode\" \u2192 privilege violation (manual 9257-9269: three conditions). \u2713 consistent with the proposed probe route (\"user-mode tb1 0,r0,0x10 \u2192 trap vector < 128 \u2192 vector 6 per #380's restriction\"). And UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE raise site: \"prom_call in user mode\" \u2014 1 site. The detector proposes UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE row via prom_call in user mode. Hmm: prom_call is a GXemul-specific pseudo-instruction? On luna88k/OpenBSD prom calls... `prom_call` \u2014 the fork models an OpenBSD/luna88k PROM-call convention? If prom_call raises UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE when in user mode, then in the testm88k probe (which boots no OS), executing prom_call in user mode \u2192 vector 5. But wait: is prom_call \"privileged\" \u2014 in supervisor mode it's a hypercall to firmware; in user mode the fork raises UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE? That's what the brief says (\"the fork's only raise site\"). Not checkable from packet beyond the claim; the detector row depends on it. Note: the manual says UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE is raised for opcodes the hardware doesn't decode \u2014 using it for \"prom_call in user mode\" is an emulator liberty (real hardware would raise it only for undefined major opcodes). Hmm \u2014 actually in GXemul, `prom_call` is implemented as an undefined/special opcode the emulator traps on; on real hardware, executing that word would raise unimplemented-opcode too (since it's not a real 88100 instruction) \u2014 so the fork maps it to UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE, which is arguably faithful for the fabricated word. OK.\n\n7. **\"rte resumes at SNIP, never at SXIP\"** \u2014 manual quote 8983-8985 \u2713 given; fork X(rte) \"First try the NIP, if it is Valid\" \u2713 given as premise. But note the fork's tail switch zeroes SNIP/SFIP for INSTRUCTION_ACCESS so rte falls back to SXIP \u2014 consistent with \"try NIP first, if valid; else XIP\". Fine.\n\n8. **\"PSR at reset is MODE|IND \u2014 supervisor, interrupts off, FPU ENABLED\"**: premise from anchor; FPU enabled because SFD1 clear (SFD1 is the disable bit; MODE|IND doesn't include SFD1) \u2713 arithmetic-consistent. The probe's div0 row producing vector 8 (not SFU1) empirically supports it (the witness ran) \u2014 READ from probe output. Actually wait \u2014 careful: if SFD1 were SET at reset, divu would raise SFU1_PRECISE (vector 11-ish, 0x58) and the probe would print vector=0x00b or whatever \u2014 it printed 0x008. So the probe output itself confirms SFD1 clear at reset. \u2713 So Q1's \"Is SFD1 really clear at reset on testm88k (else div0 is an SFU1 row)?\" \u2014 the packet answers it via the witness: vector=0x008. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 is divu an SFU1 instruction at all in this fork? If the fork implements divu in the integer unit (no SFU1 dispatch), then SFD1 state is irrelevant to divu and the whole SFD1-clear-at-reset question is moot for the div0 row. The premise \"divu by zero reaches ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE, not SFU1\" \u2014 the brief explains it via SFD1-clear. On real MC88100, div/divu ARE SFU1 instructions (the FPU performs integer division), and SFD1 set \u2192 SFU1 precise exception; SFD1 clear + zero divisor \u2192 integer-divide error exception. So the brief's reasoning matches real architecture \u2713 (REASONED, consistent with manual section 6.8.3 being an \"SFU1\" family exception \u2014 integer-divide error is grouped with FPU exceptions in the manual, which is why fcr2/fcr4/fcr5 hold operands \u2014 wait, fcr = \"floating-point control registers\" holding the source operands for the divide handler \u2014 yes, confirms div goes through the FP register file on real hardware!). And the probe confirms the fork behaves the same (vector 8). \u2713.\n\nHmm, and INTEGER_OVERFLOW: add/sub overflow \u2014 integer unit, raises vector 9. Probe: vector=0x009 \u2713.\n\n9. **\"Nine other switch cases\"** \u2014 I counted 9 \u2713 (MISALIGNED, UNIMPL, PRIV, BOUNDS, DIVIDE, OVERFLOW, ERROR, SFU1_PRECISE, SFU1_IMPRECISE).\n\n10. **The claim \"sets running = false on EVERY guest CPU\"**: fallback loop `for (int j = 0; j < cpu->machine->ncpus; ++j) cpu->machine->cpus[j]->running = false;` \u2713 READ. \"emul.c then breaks its main loop\" \u2014 premise about emul.c, not inlined; plausible (GXemul main loop exits when all CPUs stopped). Not directly checkable but low-risk. Actually the probe output shows \"emul: All machines stopped.\" \u2713 consistent.\n\n11. **\"a graceful guest-drivable machine halt, not an abort\"** \u2713 consistent with `running=false` + return, and probe output shows HALT with debugmsgs, no crash. \u2713.\n\n12. **D2 as the (d)-shaped mutant**: \"guard changed, repair nulled by the tail\" \u2014 D2 = implemented=true only \u2192 tail default \u2192 \"internal error\", running=false (this CPU only). The brief says the measurable is: fault rows print `internal error` and `All machines stopped`, pc == 8*vector. Hmm wait \u2014 pc == 8*vector: under D2, the common path DID run (pc = VBR + 8*vector), then the tail default stops the CPU. So pc = 8*vector \u2713 but SXIP/SNIP also set, PSR set \u2014 the row would look delivered except \"internal error\" + stopped. The detector's DELIVER rows assert \"machine still RUNNING\" and no \"All machines stopped\" \u2192 D2 caught \u2713. Good \u2014 the detector does catch M_NAIVE.\n\nWait, one more check on D2 \u2192 \"All machines stopped\": tail default sets `cpu->running = false` for THIS cpu. On single-CPU testm88k, that stops the machine \u2192 \"All machines stopped\" \u2713. On multi-CPU it wouldn't stop all \u2014 but testm88k is 1 CPU. The brief says \"On the single-CPU testm88k that is the same halt with a different message\" \u2713 \u2014 and importantly it acknowledges the semantic difference (fallback = all CPUs; tail = this CPU). Good premise hygiene.\n\n13. **The D1 tail-arm question \u2014 is a bare `break` right for all five?** Let me check each against the common path:\n\n- MISALIGNED: SXIP=pc|V, SNIP=pc+4|V. Manual: handler may emulate or discard. Hardware sets SNIP to next inst? The manual quote doesn't say SNIP explicitly, but \"the exception handler can emulate the memory access in software or can discard the instruction\" \u2014 both resume at next instruction \u2192 SNIP=+4 \u2713. Also \"For a ld or xmem instruction, the appropriate scoreboard bit is set\" \u2014 fork writes SSBR=0 (no scoreboard) \u2014 disclosed gap (D1d, M rows can't catch). Fine.\n\n- UNIMPLEMENTED: handler emulates or discards \u2192 +4 \u2713.\n\n- PRIVILEGE: \"the privileged operation is not performed\", SXIP = faulting \u2192 handler decides; typical: deliver SIGILL/signal, resume or terminate \u2192 +4 \u2713.\n\n- DIVIDE: destination unchanged, handler emulates via fcr or delivers SIGFPE \u2192 +4 \u2713; the `#if 0` doubt (\"back up one step?\") \u2014 resolved: NO backup; SNIP=+4 is hardware behavior; a handler that wants retry sets SNIP=SXIP itself. But wait \u2014 is that actually right on real 88100 for divide? Hmm. Let me think harder, because this is the one place the author flagged upstream doubt. MC88100 integer divide error: the exception is precise; SXIP = the divide instruction. What do SNIP/SFIP contain? On the 88100, for precise exceptions, the hardware shadow: SXIP = faulting inst; SNIP = the next instruction to execute IF the handler does nothing? Hmm, actually here's the thing \u2014 I recall for the 88100, when a precise exception is taken, SNIP contains the address of the instruction AFTER the excepting instruction... but for some exceptions (like data access?), the pipelining means multiple instructions were in flight. For a single-issue straightforward case, SNIP = SXIP+4. The general rule from the manual: \"the SNIP is the address of the next instruction to be executed after the exception\" \u2014 hmm, that phrasing would make SNIP = faulting+4 for non-branch cases. I'm fairly confident SNIP=faulting+4 (V bit set) for these precise exceptions, and the handler adjusts as needed. The OpenBSD kernel's divide handler (T_ZERODIV? m88k T_FPU... ) \u2014 OpenBSD m88k trap code for integer divide: delivers SIGFPE with the faulting PC... then the sigreturn would resume at... hmm, whatever the kernel sets. Not packet-decidable; but the architectural premise stands as the common-path default. The brief itself conditions the overflow case (6.6 not re-extracted). So: the \"SNIP = pc+4 | V\" contract for all five is REASONED and plausible but only literally quoted for the general rte mechanics \u2014 the per-class manual quotes give SXIP but not SNIP. I should say: the packet supports SXIP=pc|V for four of five (overflow conditioned); SNIP=+4 follows from the 88100's shadow-pipeline model (next-instruction semantics), and the `#if 0` doubt is resolved in favor of no-backup because the handler, not hardware, chooses retry vs skip \u2014 the manual's \"emulate or discard\" phrasing presupposes resuming at +4 with the handler doing the work. That's a solid derivation from the given quotes: \"can discard the instruction\" \u2014 discarding implies execution continues AFTER it, i.e., SNIP = +4. \u2713 Actually yes \u2014 that's the key: \"discard the instruction\" only makes sense if the return goes to the NEXT instruction. Good.\n\nWait, careful: \"The exception handler can emulate the memory access in software or can discard the instruction\" \u2014 discard = skip it, resume after \u2192 SNIP must be +4. \u2713 For divide: \"fcr2/fcr4 contain the source operands, fcr5 the destination register number\" \u2014 the handler EMULATES the divide (writes the destination? no \u2014 divide-by-zero can't be emulated to a value; the handler might emulate to implement a defined result?) \u2014 hmm, for divide the handler typically delivers an error. Whatever: +4 stands.\n\n14. **Q2 \u2014 smallest edit that breaks D1 and still passes the detector.** The kill table lists M_LEVEL (disclosed: message level INFO\u2194ERROR not observable by rows). What else could break D1 but pass the detector? Think about what the detector asserts: RESULT (delivered rows: pc==8*vector, SXIP==0x10004|V, PSR SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE, r2 unchanged, still running), live/attrib DONE with r2 values.\n\nCandidate escapes:\n- (a) EPSR not saved? The common path: EPSR = PSR only in the else branch (SFRZ not set). If a mutant removed the EPSR save, the delivered rows would still pass (no row reads EPSR) \u2014 an rte-return row would catch it, but there's no rte row! The detector never returns from the handler (no handler exists in testm88k RAM \u2014 the RAM at 8*vector is... what's at 0x40? Zeroed RAM \u2192 word 0x00000000 \u2192 opcode 0? Executing word 0... hmm wait. After delivery, pc = 0x40; the machine keeps RUNNING; the probe then reads registers. What's executing at 0x40? If testm88k RAM is zeroed, executing 0x00000000 \u2014 major opcode 0 \u2014 is that UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE? Hmm, word 0 on 88100: opcode 000000... I think 0x00000000 decodes as... major opcode 0 = \"0x00: ... hmm. In the 88100, 0x00000000 is... I don't remember. If it raises UNIMPLEMENTED (vector 5) \u2192 which after D1 delivers \u2192 pc = 0x28 \u2192 executes zeros there too \u2192 infinite loop of exceptions? Each exception: SFRZ now set \u2192 second exception is non-trap \u2192 re-route to ERROR (vector 10) \u2192 ERROR is unimplemented \u2192 fallback \u2192 HALT! Wait: word at 0x40 is 0 \u2192 if unimplemented-opcode \u2192 m88k_exception(cpu, 5, ...) \u2192 SFRZ already set \u2192 !is_trap \u2192 vector = ERROR \u2192 tail: implemented stays false?? wait the re-route: after the first switch (vector 5, UNIMPL \u2192 implemented = true after D1), the SFRZ check re-routes vector = ERROR \u2014 but `implemented` was already computed in the FIRST switch as true! So the fallback check `vector < USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented` \u2014 vector is now ERROR(10) < 128, implemented = true (computed before re-route!) \u2192 passes the fallback with vector=ERROR! Then tail switch: switch(vector=ERROR) \u2192 default \u2192 \"internal error\" \u2192 running=false \u2192 HALT. Interesting \u2014 the double-fault path under D1 leads to tail-default halt, NOT the unimplemented fallback, because `implemented` was latched before the re-route. Hmm! That's a real edge: after D1, a double fault (any non-trap exception with SFRZ set) re-routes to ERROR, but `implemented` is true from the original class, so it bypasses the intended \"genuinely unimplemented\" fallback and lands in tail-default \"internal error\" \u2192 halt. Either way it halts (ERROR is unimplemented anyway), so behavior is the same halt with a different message. But it shows the ERROR re-route + implemented-latch interaction: if later someone implements ERROR, the latch would matter. For this round: Q-D1c says ERROR out of scope, filed. But note the latch subtlety as a finding: the `implemented` flag is computed from the ORIGINAL vector, before the SFRZ re-route \u2014 so a double fault of a DELIVERED class prints \"internal error\" and halts via tail-default rather than printing \"unimplemented vector=0x00a\". The brief didn't mention this latch interaction. It's observable only via a double-fault probe row, which doesn't exist (no row raises two exceptions). Worth naming in Q2 as an escape: a mutant (or the design itself) that mishandles the SFRZ re-route for delivered classes passes the detector because no row double-faults. Actually more precisely: under D1 as specified, a second exception while SFRZ set: for the five, is_trap \u2014 what is is_trap for these? The raise sites call m88k_exception(cpu, VECTOR, is_trap?) \u2014 the census doesn't say what is_trap values are used! Hmm \u2014 the function signature has is_trap; the brief never says what the five raise sites pass. If the five are raised with is_trap=true (they're \"trap-like\"), then with SFRZ set they'd NOT re-route to ERROR, just update_shadow_regs=false and deliver to the same vector with stale shadows. If is_trap=false, they re-route to ERROR. The manual: these five ARE trap-type exceptions (the manual distinguishes trap vs non-trap: with SFRZ set, trap exceptions are still taken; non-trap \u2192 error). So the raise sites presumably pass is_trap=1 for the five? The brief calls them \"trap-like classes\" and the detector asserts delivery works. The packet doesn't state the is_trap values at the raise sites \u2014 that's a premise I can't settle; name it: the is_trap argument at the five raise sites (grep anchor `m88k_exception(cpu, M88K_EXCEPTION_ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE`). It matters for double-fault semantics and for the ERROR question (Q-D1c): if the five are is_trap=true, then a divide-by-zero inside an exception handler (SFRZ set) delivers ANOTHER ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE with stale shadow regs \u2014 which would CLOBBER... no wait, update_shadow_regs=false so shadows not clobbered, EPSR not saved, PSR still has SFRZ \u2014 the second vector's handler runs with the FIRST exception's shadow state. That's the architected trap-with-SFRZ behavior (I think hardware allows trap exceptions with SFRZ set, keeping shadow state). OK.\n\n- (b) SSBR handling: fork writes SSBR=0 always; a mutant that skips SSBR=0 \u2014 no row reads SSBR \u2192 passes. But that's already-disclosed scoreboard gap.\n\n- (c) The M_LEVEL escape is disclosed. What else? The contract line says \"Does not catch: message level; fcr2/fcr4/fc5; scoreboard bits; delay-slot arms\". So the brief already names delay-slot as an uncovered domain. Q at the end: \"should one?\" \u2014 a row executing a faulting instruction in a delay slot: the common path's delay-slot arms: for the five (not DATA/INSTRUCTION access), the else arm: SNIP = (pc+4)|V, SFIP = delay_target|V. Hmm wait \u2014 for a faulting instruction in a delay slot, pc = the branch instruction's address + 4? (the delay slot address); delay_target = branch target. SNIP = pc+4 \u2014 but the correct architectural behavior for an exception in a delay slot: SXIP = delay-slot instruction; SNIP = delay target? On 88100: if the excepting instruction is in a delay slot, SNIP = branch target address. The fork sets SNIP = pc+4 (NOT the target!) and SFIP = delay_target. That looks WRONG vs hardware (hardware: SNIP = target). Hmm wait, the fork comment says \"Perhaps something like this could work\" \u2014 author uncertainty! `cpu->cd.m88k.cr[M88K_CR_SNIP] = (cpu->pc + 4) | V; cpu->cd.m88k.cr[M88K_CR_SFIP] = cpu->delay_target | V;` \u2014 author's own \"perhaps\". For DATA_ACCESS in delay slot, SNIP = delay_target (correct). For the five in a delay slot, SNIP = pc+4 \u2014 likely wrong (should be delay_target) but NO ROW tests it and the contract line discloses it. So: a mutant (or D1 itself!) that gets the delay-slot arm wrong for the five passes the detector. That's the biggest un-disclosed-as-mutant escape... actually it IS disclosed (\"does not catch ... the delay-slot arms (no row executes a faulting instruction in a delay slot \u2014 QUESTION: should one?)\"). So my Q2 answer: besides M_LEVEL, the delay-slot arm (already disclosed as uncovered \u2014 answer the question: YES, add a row: e.g., `bcnd` ... a branch with delay slot where the delay-slot instruction is `divu r2,r3,r0` \u2192 delivered, assert SNIP == delay_target | V). Also EPSR: no row reads EPSR \u2014 an EPSR-clobbering mutant passes; add an EPSR assertion (cheap: assert EPSR == MODE|IND as captured). Also the `m88k_pc_to_pointers` / DMT zeroing: mutant skipping DMT0=0 \u2014 no row reads DMT0 \u2192 passes; disclosed under D1d? The contract line doesn't name DMT. Hmm, delivered rows assert r2 unchanged, pc, SXIP, PSR, running. Not DMT0 (should be 0 for misalign per manual 9236-9237 \"detected before dispatched\" \u2014 the brief asserts DMT0 zeroed is correct). A mutant that copies dmt[] for MISALIGNED (like DATA_ACCESS does) would pass all rows (no row reads DMT0). So name it: \"DMT0 for misalign \u2014 no row reads it\". That's a genuine Q2 answer beyond the disclosed list? The contract line lists \"Does not catch: message level; fcr2/fcr4/fcr5; scoreboard bits; the delay-slot arms\". DMT0 not listed \u2192 my addition. Also EPSR not listed \u2192 addition. Also the SFRZ re-route latch (double fault) \u2014 no row double-faults \u2192 an edit breaking the re-route (e.g., is_trap inverted) passes. Also: `cpu->delay_slot = NOT_DELAYED` reset \u2014 hmm.\n\nSmallest edit that breaks D1 and still passes the detector: I'd say: removing the EPSR save (`cr[EPSR] = cr[PSR]`) \u2014 no delivered row reads EPSR; all five rows stay green. Or: for MISALIGNED only, setting SNIP = pc (re-execute) \u2014 wait that IS M_SNIPX-ish, caught by breakpoint+SNIP assertion? The rows assert SXIP and pc but SNIP? \"for delivered rows, pc == 8*vector, SXIP == 0x10004 | V, PSR ..., r2 unchanged, machine still RUNNING\" \u2014 SNIP is NOT asserted! M_SNIPX is claimed to be caught (\"the div0 row loops \u2014 observable as the breakpoint hit with SNIP == SXIP\") \u2014 hmm, if the rows don't assert SNIP, how is M_SNIPX caught? The kill table says \"observable as the breakpoint hit with SNIP == SXIP\" \u2014 meaning the probe would... if SNIP = pc (faulting), then after delivery pc = 8*vector; the handler at 0x40 is zeros... hmm, actually how does M_SNIPX manifest? SNIP is where rte returns \u2014 there's no rte in the probe! There's no handler code at 8*vector (testm88k RAM presumably zeroed) \u2014 so how would SNIP=pc \"loop\"? The row asserts pc == 8*vector after delivery... SNIP never gets used because no rte executes. Hmm, unless the probe plants an rte at 8*vector? The probe description says: plant program, seed regs, `breakpoint add <8*vector>`, continue, `reg` \u2014 and asserts. If the probe plants a handler at 8*vector containing rte, then rte \u2192 resumes at SNIP \u2192 if SNIP = pc (0x10004, the divu) \u2192 divide by zero again \u2192 exception \u2192 SFRZ now set \u2192 is_trap? \u2192 re-route or stale-shadow delivery \u2192 infinite loop \u2192 the \"loop\" observable. But the probe section doesn't say a handler with rte is planted! It says \"each asserts RESULT and, for delivered rows, pc == 8*vector, SXIP..., PSR..., r2 unchanged, and the machine still RUNNING\". If the handler RAM is zeros, execution at 0x40 continues into zeros \u2014 what does word 0 do? If word 0 is an unimplemented opcode \u2192 exception \u2192 double fault \u2192 (under D1 with latch) \u2192 tail default \u2192 HALT \u2192 \"machine still RUNNING\" assertion FAILS! Hmm \u2014 wait, that would make the DELIVER rows impossible to satisfy unless the probe stops at the breakpoint BEFORE the zeros execute. The probe uses `breakpoint add 0x40; continue; reg` \u2014 the debugger stops at pc=0x40 before executing, reads registers, asserts \"still RUNNING\" (the machine hasn't halted at the moment of the breakpoint). OK so the rows only observe state AT the breakpoint, no rte, no handler execution. Then M_SNIPX (SNIP = pc, re-execute): observable \"as the breakpoint hit with SNIP == SXIP\" \u2014 i.e., the probe DOES read SNIP at the breakpoint and can compare SNIP == 0x10008|V (expected) vs SNIP == 0x10004|V (mutant). But the delivered-row assertion list doesn't include SNIP! The kill table says M_SNIPX is caught (\"the div0 row loops\" \u2014 hmm, \"loops\"?). If no rte executes, SNIP=pc doesn't \"loop\" anything \u2014 the assertion must be a direct SNIP read. There's an internal inconsistency-ish: the row assertions (\u00a76) omit SNIP, but the kill table claims M_SNIPX is caught \"observable as the breakpoint hit with SNIP == SXIP\". So the probe must additionally assert SNIP == 0x10008 | V on delivered rows. That's a gap between \u00a76's assertion list and the kill table's catch list \u2014 worth naming: EITHER add SNIP (and SFIP) to the delivered-row assertions, or M_SNIPX escapes. Good catch \u2014 that's a Q2-class answer: \"M_SNIPX escapes unless the probe asserts SNIP, which \u00a76's list omits.\"\n\nHmm wait, re-read the kill table: \"M_SNIPX (SNIP = pc, re-execute: the div0 row loops -- observable as the breakpoint hit with SNIP == SXIP)\". \"The div0 row loops\" \u2014 maybe: with SNIP = pc... the breakpoint is at 0x40 = 8*vector; delivery sets pc = 0x40; breakpoint hit; probe reads regs. No loop. Unless the probe CONTINUES after the reg read and the handler... zeros... hmm. If the probe continues to the NEXT breakpoint or to halt: with handler = zeros at 0x40... Actually wait \u2014 maybe testm88k RAM at 0x40 contains something else? testm88k is a bare machine; the probe plants the program at 0x10000 via `put w` \u2014 the vector area (0x0-0x400) is presumably zeros. Executing zeros: major opcode 0 = ? On 88100, 0x00000000... I don't know. If it's unimplemented \u2192 vector 5 \u2192 double fault \u2192 under D1-latch \u2192 tail default \u2192 halt. So a CONTINUED deliver row would halt with \"internal error\" unless the probe stops asserting at the breakpoint. The phrase \"the machine still RUNNING -- no `All machines stopped`\" suggests the probe checks machine state at/after the breakpoint hit \u2014 fine.\n\nSo: SNIP assertion missing from the stated row contract \u2192 M_SNIPX catch claim unsupported as stated. NAME IT.\n\nSimilarly, SXIP is asserted \u2713, PSR \u2713, pc \u2713, r2 \u2713, running \u2713. SFIP not asserted (minor, SFIP = SNIP+4 \u2014 a mutant on SFIP alone escapes; disclosed? not named; minor).\n\nAlso: does any row assert EPSR? No. M_EPSR escape.\n\nAlso the PRIV and UNIMPL rows: they need USER MODE. The proposed route: supervisor `stcr rS,cr1` clearing PSR.MODE, then `ldcr r2,cr1` \u2192 vector 6. Wait \u2014 after clearing MODE, the NEXT instruction executes in user mode. The probe asks: \"say whether clearing MODE by `stcr` while the next instruction is already translated behaves as on hardware in this fork (translation caches are mode-tagged?)\" \u2014 the packet CANNOT answer this (no decoder/translation info inlined). I should say: not checkable from packet; the settle-fact: whether the fork's instruction fetch path re-checks PSR.MODE per instruction or caches decoded pages by mode (grep anchor for the fetch/`to_be_translated` path). Also the exact stcr/ldcr encodings: the packet explicitly says it doesn't carry them \u2014 I can attempt from knowledge? The brief asks \"give the exact encodings for `stcr`/`ldcr` from the decoder (`case 0x20` group) -- the packet does not carry them\". As a packet-fed seat I cannot produce them from the packet; I can offer from general 88100 knowledge with the caveat: ldcr/stcr are major opcode 0x20 (100000): `ldcr rD,crN` = 0x84000000 | D<<21 | crN<<5? Hmm: 88100 ldcr: opcode 100000, D, 10-bit zero?, crN in bits 4-0... stcr: 100001...? I recall ldcr = 0x84xxxxxx with cr field in bits 4-0? e.g., `ldcr r1,cr1` = 0x84200021?? I'm honestly not certain. stcr rS,crN = 0x86000000-ish? Hmm: ldcr opcode = 100000 (0x20), stcr = 100001 (0x21)? bits: ldcr: 84 = 1000 0100 \u2192 bits 31-26 = 100000 \u2713 (0x20), bit 25-21 = 00010 (D=2)... The brief says \"case 0x20 group\" \u2014 so ldcr/stcr/xcr live under decoded case 0x20? Hmm, if ldcr = bits31-26 = 0x20 = 100000, then 0x84 top byte... wait 100000 \u2192 top 6 bits 100000 \u2192 the word starts 1000 00.. = 0x80-0x83 range. Hmm: 100000 in bits 31-26 means byte pattern 10 00 00xx \u2192 0x80..0x83. ldcr rD,crN: 0x80000000 | D<<21 | ... I genuinely can't reconstruct reliably. I should NOT fabricate. Say: not derivable from the packet; the brief itself says the packet doesn't carry them; the settle-fact = the decoder's `case 0x20` block (grep anchor). Note: the risk \u2014 a wrong stcr encoding could itself raise UNIMPLEMENTED (or PRIVILEGE) and fake the row; the probe needs a control: after `stcr` clearing MODE, a subsequent NON-privileged instruction should still execute (user mode liveness) before the faulting ldcr \u2014 actually the design has the faulting instruction as ldcr itself; better control: verify PSR.MODE cleared via `reg` read? `reg` in the debugger can dump cr1/PSR directly \u2014 assert MODE clear before the ldcr. Good suggestion.\n\nAlso, subtle: `ld.usr/st.usr` \u2014 user-mode access; the PRIV row via ldcr is fine.\n\nAlso: does clearing MODE via stcr even work in the fork? The brief asserts \"the fork's `m88k_stcr` handles the mode switch\" \u2014 premise, not checkable from packet (m88k_stcr body not inlined). Name it.\n\nAnother subtle issue with the PRIV row: after clearing PSR.MODE from supervisor via stcr, the CPU is in user mode. Then `ldcr r2,cr1` raises PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION (vector 6) per manual 9257 (\"control register access other than FPCR/FPSR in user mode\"). \u2713 manual-supported. But: exception DELIVERY sets PSR MODE=1 (supervisor) via common path \u2713 \u2014 good, the handler runs supervisor.\n\nAnd the UNIMPL row via prom_call in user mode: prom_call is a fork construct; raise site claimed 1. Fine, premise.\n\n15. **Q-D1b BOUNDS_CHECK**: no raise site (tbnd disassembler-only) \u2014 premise not checkable from packet; \"zero cost, no row can observe it\" \u2014 TRUE if no raise site exists (delivered-but-never-raised is unobservable). But WAIT: the SFRZ re-route! ERROR re-route: `vector = M88K_EXCEPTION_ERROR` \u2014 that's a re-route TO ERROR, not through BOUNDS. BOUNDS never receives a vector. So including BOUNDS in D1 (implemented=true + tail case) is dead code unless a raise site appears. Consistency argument (Q-D1b): including it costs nothing and makes the \"genuinely unimplemented\" set honest = {ERROR, SFU1\u00d72} only. Precedent question (Q3): how do other cores treat recognized-but-unmodeled classes \u2014 deliver or stop? Can't check from packet (no other cores inlined) \u2014 name that; the settle-fact: grep anchors for mips_cpu_exception etc. But REASONED: for MIPS, GXemul delivers all standard exceptions (TLB refs, etc.) \u2014 hmm, I know GXemul's MIPS handles quite complete exception delivery. I can reason: the emulator's purpose (boot OpenBSD) demands delivering exceptions the guest OS has handlers for; any exception class the manual defines with a guest-visible contract should be delivered when the emulator can compute the raise condition; BOUNDS cannot be raised (tbnd never executes the check in this fork \u2014 disassembler-only, premise) so the choice is cosmetic. I'd lean: include BOUNDS as implemented with a tail case ONLY IF the emulator can raise it (it can't \u2014 no raise site) \u2014 actually including it changes nothing observable; the honest option is to leave it unimplemented with a comment, OR implement tbnd's raise too. The \"consistency\" argument is weak: implemented=true for a class with no raise site is misleading dead code; the fallback's \"unimplemented\" message is honest documentation. Hmm, but the counter: IF someone later implements tbnd, the halt-on-first-tbnd is a landmine. Zero cost either way. I'll answer: leave BOUNDS out of the five but note it's unobservable either way; the precedent grep decides. Actually \u2014 hmm, wait: is BOUNDS really trap-like with a guest handler? OpenBSD m88k has T_BNDFLT? m88k trap types: T_PRIVINFLT, T_BPTFLT, T_RESOFLT (reserved/opcodes?), T_UNIMPFLT?... OpenBSD m88k/m88100_trap: T_ZERODIV, T_OVFFLT, T_PRIVINFLT, T_MISALGNFLT, T_UNIMPFLT (unimplemented opcode), T_BNDFLT (bounds check, tbnd) \u2014 yes, m88k has T_BNDFLT. So a guest handler exists for bounds too. Fine.\n\n16. **Q-D1a message level**: INFO vs ERROR. The hygiene gate 6 and #261 treat ERROR specially. A userland div-by-zero under OpenBSD \u2192 SIGFPE \u2192 routine. ERROR-level log line per routine event = log spam. REASONED: change to INFO. Would a hygiene row red either way? Can't know gate 6's rows from packet \u2014 name it: the gate 6 row set. The brief asks \"Is there a hygiene row that would red either way?\" \u2014 not checkable from packet; the settle-fact: gate 6's row list (which log lines it requires/forbids). Note the detector's M_LEVEL mutant is disclosed as unobservable \"unless a hygiene row exists\" \u2014 consistent.\n\nAlso note: debugmsg VERBOSITY_ERROR for \"unimplemented\" fallback stays \u2014 correct (genuinely unimplemented = emulator defect, ERROR appropriate). \u2713 design keeps fallback text.\n\n17. **Q-D1c ERROR double fault**: out of scope, file. The latch interaction I found above (implemented latched before re-route) means under D1 a double fault from a delivered class reaches tail-default \"internal error\" halt \u2014 NOT the \"unimplemented\" fallback. So the observable message for a guest double fault changes from \"unimplemented vector=0x00a\" (hmm \u2014 actually today: first exception = e.g. div0 \u2192 unimplemented fallback halt immediately; a double fault can't even occur today for these classes since the first fault halts). After D1, double faults become REACHABLE (first fault delivers; handler code could divide by zero again; SFRZ set). So D1 makes reachable a state (double fault) that today is unreachable \u2014 and in that state the behavior is tail-default \"internal error\" halt. Is that acceptable for this round? The manual/hardware: trap exceptions with SFRZ set are taken; non-trap with SFRZ \u2192 error exception \u2192 error handler. What are the five \u2014 trap or non-trap? The manual distinguishes: \"trap\" exceptions (misaligned? no...). MC88100: exceptions are categorized: traps (e.g., privilege violation, unimplemented opcode, bounds check, integer divide, integer overflow, misaligned access = trap-type?) vs non-trap (reset, interrupt, instruction access, data access, error). Hmm \u2014 I believe on 88100 the \"trap\" exceptions include: unimplemented opcode, privilege violation, bounds check, integer divide, integer overflow, misaligned access (those are the \"trap exceptions\" \u2014 vector 1-9?) and non-trap: reset(0)? interrupt(2), instruction access(3), data access(4)?, error. The manual's SFRZ rule quoted in code: \"Non-trap exceptions when the shadow freeze bit is already set result in an Error exception\". So the five ARE traps (is_trap=true presumably at raise sites). With SFRZ set, trap exceptions ARE taken (hardware allows nested trap exceptions keeping shadow state). So under D1 with is_trap=true at raise sites, a double fault of the five delivers the SAME vector again with stale (frozen) shadows \u2014 matches hardware. Then the ERROR question only concerns non-trap double faults (interrupt-in-handler etc.). The packet doesn't state the is_trap values at raise sites \u2014 SETTLE-FACT needed: grep anchor for the raise sites' third argument. If the five are raised with is_trap=0, D1's delivery of a handler-nested fault would re-route to ERROR \u2192 tail-default halt \u2260 hardware. This is a REAL premise to name in Q1/Q2: \"the is_trap argument at each of the five's raise sites is not in the packet.\"\n\nHmm, let me double check the manual's trap/non-trap classification for these five. MC88100 exceptions: Reset, Interrupt, Instruction Access, Data Access = non-trap (I think data access is non-trap), and Misaligned access, Unimplemented opcode, Privilege violation, Bounds check, Integer divide, Integer overflow, Error = ... hmm, Error is non-trap (it's the result of non-trap-with-SFRZ). Actually the manual says the exception types: \"trap exceptions\" (which do not cause the Error exception when SFRZ set) vs \"non-trap\". I believe: traps = unimplemented opcode, privilege violation, bounds check, integer divide, integer overflow, misaligned access? Hmm, not 100% sure about misaligned. The manual quote 9231-9240 for misaligned says \"precise exception\" \u2014 precise \u2260 trap classification. The code comment says \"Non-trap exceptions when the shadow freeze bit is already set result in an Error exception\" \u2014 consistent with manual. The brief calls the five \"trap-like classes\" \u2014 asserting they're traps. I can't verify the classification from the packet (no manual quote on trap vs non-trap for the five). NAME IT: whether each of the five is a trap-type exception per the manual (affects double-fault behavior; manual section on SFRZ/trap classification \u2014 a file-reading seat greps \"trap exception\" in mc88100.txt). This interacts directly with the is_trap raise-site values.\n\n18. **Q4 the guest**: OpenBSD/luna88k boots today without the fix \u2192 no boot-path raise of the five (given). What in a booted system would raise: (a) userland divide by zero (divu r,r,0 or div with negative operands \u2192 SIGFPE); (b) userland `ldcr` \u2014 e.g., a program reading PSR? userland wouldn't; but a KERNEL probe might deliberately execute privileged instructions to test something; (c) misaligned access with MXM clear \u2014 the kernel sets MXM? OpenBSD m88k: does it set MXM to mask misaligned? OpenBSD/luna88k I believe sets MXM=0 (unmasked) and handles T_MISALGNFLT by fixing up unaligned accesses in the kernel (m88100_trap has a misaligned fixup path). Hmm \u2014 actually OpenBSD m88k has `m88100_misaligned` handling? I recall OpenBSD m88k handles misaligned loads/stores in trap handling (T_MISALGNFLT) with fixup. If so, userland unaligned access is routine \u2192 currently every such access halts the machine \u2014 meaning a booted OpenBSD with any unaligned access would have halted... consistent with \"boots without the fix\" if boot path has none. But then \"what would raise\": plenty of userland. Also integer overflow: OpenBSD kernel \u2014 does it unmask OVE (integer overflow enable in PSR)? PSR.OVE bit: overflow exception only taken if PSR.OVE set. Hmm! INTEGER_OVERFLOW raise condition: the fork raises on `d != dx` in X(add) \u2014 does the fork check PSR.OVE?? The manual: integer overflow exception occurs only if OVE (overflow enable) is set in PSR; if OVE clear, the result wraps. The brief's raise census says INTEGER_OVERFLOW 3 sites \"(add/sub, any mode)\" \u2014 \"ANY mode\" but says nothing about OVE! If the fork raises overflow regardless of PSR.OVE, that's a FORK DIVERGENCE from hardware: with OVE clear at reset (is OVE in MODE|IND? No \u2014 OVE is a separate PSR bit, presumably clear at reset), a userland `add` overflow would WRAP on hardware but raise vector 9 in the fork \u2192 after D1, delivered \u2192 OpenBSD's T_OVFFLT handler \u2192 SIGFPE for a program that expected wrapping! That's a guest-visible regression risk introduced by D1 \u2014 WAIT, no: today the fork raises vector 9 regardless of OVE?? If it raises and the fallback halts \u2014 then ANY userland add-overflow HALTS the machine today. Hmm, does the fork check OVE before raising? NOT STATED in the packet. This is a KEY premise to name: does X(add)'s overflow raise site check `PSR & OVE`? The probe's ovf row (r3=0x7fffffff+1) raised vector 9 with PSR = MODE|IND (OVE presumably clear at reset!) \u2014 the witness output shows ovf \u2192 HALT vector 0x009. If PSR at reset lacks OVE, then the fork raised overflow WITHOUT OVE set \u2192 fork does not implement the OVE mask \u2192 divergence from hardware confirmed by the packet itself (probe ran with reset PSR = MODE|IND; OVE not among them per the premise \"PSR at reset is MODE|IND\"). Unless OVE is... hmm, is OVE maybe SET by default in MODE|IND? OVE is PSR bit... M88K_PSR_OVE \u2014 the reset value MODE|IND includes only MODE and IND bits per the anchor. So at reset OVE=0. The ovf row raised vector 9 anyway. READ: the fork raises integer overflow without checking OVE (or the probe seeded PSR? \"registers seeded with rN=\" \u2014 maybe also cr? If the probe can seed cr1 (PSR), maybe it set OVE... but the brief says PSR at reset MODE|IND and div0 relies on SFD1-clear \u2014 the rows presumably run at reset PSR. The ovf row at reset PSR (OVE clear) raised vector 9 \u2192 fork ignores OVE.) CONCLUSION: the fork's INTEGER_OVERFLOW raise ignores PSR.OVE \u2014 REASONED from packet facts (reset PSR + ovf row raising). This matters for D1: delivering vector 9 on every userland add-overflow that hardware would have wrapped (OVE clear in userland typically \u2014 OpenBSD sets PSR for userland with OVE clear? if OVE clear in user processes, hardware wraps; fork would now deliver T_OVFFLT \u2192 OpenBSD sends SIGFPE \u2192 userland programs that rely on wraparound break (UB-ish but real programs do rely). So D1 + the OVE-less raise = a behavior change vs hardware in the DELIVERED direction. The fix for the round should either (i) gate the INTEGER_OVERFLOW raise on PSR.OVE (matching hardware), or (ii) disclose it. The brief does NOT mention OVE anywhere! That's a genuine gap I found. Let me double-check the manual knowledge: MC88100 PSR bits: IND (disable interrupts), SFD1, SFD2, SFRZ, OVE (overflow enable), MODE, PAR... yes OVE exists: \"integer overflow exception occurs if OVE is set\". And the manual 6.6 (integer overflow, $48): \"If the OVE bit... is clear, the result is stored...\" something like that. So the packet's \u00a74 note \"(6.6 Integer Overflow, offset $48: not re-extracted here...)\" \u2014 and the raise-site census \"(add/sub, any mode)\" \u2014 the brief author may have missed the OVE gate. STRONG finding for Q1/Q4: name it \u2014 premise: \"the fork raises INTEGER_OVERFLOW without checking PSR.OVE\" \u2014 evidenced by the ovf probe row raising at reset PSR (MODE|IND, OVE clear). If instead the probe seeded OVE (not stated), the premise is unsettled \u2014 settle-fact: the raise site's guard (grep anchor `M88K_EXCEPTION_INTEGER_OVERFLOW` with surrounding condition \u2014 does it test `cr[M88K_CR_PSR] & M88K_PSR_OVE`?) and the probe's PSR seeding. This affects D1's correctness directly: delivering an exception hardware wouldn't take.\n\nSimilarly, MISALIGNED: manual says masked by MXM bit (9243-9246) \u2014 does the fork check MXM before raising? The census says MISALIGNED 1 site \"(load/store template, any mode)\". Reset PSR = MODE|IND \u2014 MXM not set (clear) \u2192 misaligned NOT masked \u2192 raises \u2713 consistent with the misalign probe row raising. But does the fork check MXM at all? Not stated. If the fork raises misaligned regardless of MXM, and OpenBSD sets MXM=1 for userland (to let hardware round instead of trap?) \u2014 hmm, does OpenBSD/luna88k set MXM? I don't know. If the guest sets MXM to mask misalignment and the fork ignores MXM, D1 would deliver misaligned traps the hardware would have rounded \u2192 guest-visible divergence (SIGBUS storms). Same class of finding as OVE. NAME BOTH: the MXM gate and the OVE gate at the raise sites are premises the packet doesn't settle; the delivered-exception contract makes the raise-side conditions part of correctness.\n\nAlso PRIVILEGE for trap vectors 0-127 in user mode \u2014 #380's restriction mentioned \u2014 fine.\n\nAlso UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE via prom_call \u2014 the fork's only raise site is prom_call in user mode; but what about genuinely undefined opcodes a guest might execute (e.g., speculative execution scanning, or JIT bugs)? If the decoder's default for unknown opcodes is UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE raise... the census says only 1 raise site (prom_call). Hmm \u2014 what does the fork do with a truly undefined major opcode? If the decoder default-cases to something else (halt? ignore?), then UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE delivery via prom_call is a narrow emulation-liberty. The manual's unimplemented-opcode is for undefined instructions; if the fork never raises it for undefined instructions, delivering vector 5 only for prom_call is fine for now. Not a defect, just note the census implies undefined opcodes don't raise vector 5 \u2014 is that right? Not checkable; name it: what does the decoder's default arm do for undefined major opcodes (grep anchor: the main opcode switch's default).\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 the brief says \"UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE 1 (prom_call in user mode)\". So undefined opcodes presumably DON'T raise it (or prom_call IS an undefined-opcode alias: prom_call might be encoded as an instruction word the real 88100 doesn't define, and the fork's decoder case for it raises UNIMPLEMENTED when in user mode and does PROM stuff in supervisor. That's likely GXemul's design). OK.\n\n19. **Q4 boot-gate risk**: once the five deliver, a guest that deliberately triggers them gets handler execution instead of halt. Gate_ab rows: could a row's behavior change? A row that (deliberately or not) executes a faulting instruction would previously HALT (deterministic) and now run a handler (different). If any existing gate row has an \"expected halt\" for these vectors, it would flip. Not checkable from packet \u2014 settle-fact: grep the gate rows for m88k halt expectations (anchors in gate scripts). Suggest a measuring seat: run gate_ab before/after the fix and diff. Also suggest: boot OpenBSD/luna88k and run a userland `int x=1/0;` program \u2014 expect SIGFPE and the machine to keep running; before the fix it halts the rig. And a misaligned userland access. And an `ldcr` from userland (compile a kernel-module? userland can't execute ldcr without the kernel... a user program with inline asm `ldcr` would trap \u2192 SIGILL). These are the Q4 measurements to name.\n\n20. **Q5 CHANGELOG**: owes: the manual citations resolving the `#if 0` divide doubt (rte-at-SNIP, SNIP=+4, handler adjusts); provenance (retro batch-2, #380, ab9ae13 filing); the split from m88kexcsfu1 (SFU1 needs FP exception registers the fork never writes); the two-site shape of the fix (first-switch implemented flag + tail cases) with the D2 mutant as the reason; the disclosed gaps (scoreboard/SSBR, fcr2/4/5, MXM/OVE gates if not handled, delay-slot arms untested); the level change INFO with gate-6 note; the ERROR re-route latch behavior (double fault \u2192 tail-default \"internal error\") \u2014 and the OVE/MXM raise-gate divergence if confirmed.\n\n21. **The premise \"rte resumes at SNIP, never at SXIP\"** vs the INSTRUCTION_ACCESS tail arm zeroing SNIP \u2014 the fork's rte tries NIP first then XIP; zeroed SNIP (V bit clear \u2192 invalid) falls to SXIP. The manual says hardware resumes at SNIP \u2014 so for INSTRUCTION_ACCESS the fork's zeroing makes rte resume at SXIP = re-execute \u2014 the handler adjusts by... hmm, that's existing behavior for an implemented class, not this round's scope. Fine.\n\n22. **The witness's \"rung 3\" status and the detector's wiring into gate 11 (`gate_m88k_rounding.sh`)**: wiring an exception probe into a gate named \"rounding\" \u2014 naming oddity; also the brief says gate 11 gets \"a new section\" \u2014 fine. The operational note: don't run gates. OK.\n\n23. **Check \"vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START\" with USER_TRAPS_START = 128**: the syscall cases 0x80 (=128) and 0x1c2 are \u2265 128 \u2713 so syscall bypasses the fallback (prints INFO, no halt) \u2713 consistent with the inlined default's ternary (VERBOSITY_ERROR if < START else INFO). MVMEPROM_VECTOR: value? If < 128 and not implemented... MVMEPROM_VECTOR is presumably 0x1fc8 or something high (MVME PROM base) \u2014 likely \u2265128. Not checkable; if MVMEPROM_VECTOR < 128, a PROM vector call would halt via fallback \u2014 worth a one-line check for a file-reader. Actually the case falls to `break` with implemented=false; if MVMEPROM_VECTOR < USER_TRAPS_START \u2192 fallback halt. MVMEPROM_VECTOR in GXemul is probably 0x1fc8? whatever \u2014 flag as a micro-premise. Hmm \u2014 the brief's scope says fallback stays \"for the genuinely unimplemented vectors (BOUNDS_CHECK, ERROR, SFU1 x2) and for unknown vectors below 128\" \u2014 implicitly claiming MVMEPROM and syscall are \u2265128 (else they'd be in the fallback set too). MVMEPROM: the case exists upstream and presumably works (PROM emulation boots things?) so it must be \u2265 START or never taken below. Flag lightly.\n\n24. **The \"nine other switch cases\" vs \"the five\"**: after D1, implemented=true for 4+5=9 classes; the fallback covers BOUNDS, ERROR, SFU1\u00d72 (4 classes) + unknowns <128 + (MVMEPROM if <128). \u2713.\n\n25. **Detector's delivered-row assertion \"SXIP == 0x10004 | V\"**: the raise happens with cpu->pc = 0x10004 (the test instruction). In the fallback (current) the probe printed pc=0x00010004 at HALT \u2713. After delivery SXIP = pc|V \u2014 V bit is bit 0 (M88K_XIP_V = 1?) presumably \u2713. Fine.\n\n26. **The `br .` loop at 0x10008**: after a DELIVERED row, pc=8*vector; the probe breaks there. For DONE rows (live/attrib): pc=0x10008 = the `br .` \u2192 loops at 0x10008. live row shows pc=0x00010008 \u2713 (br . at 0x10008, self-loop: br . encodes -1 from next \u2192 target 0x10008 \u2713 consistent with displacement semantics: (0x10008+4) + 4*(-1) = 0x10008 \u2713). Good internal consistency: `br .` = c3ffffff placed at 0x10008 \u2192 branches to itself. \u2713 (also validates the br encoding interpretation: -1 relative to pc+4 \u2192 self). Wait \u2014 for div0 the test instruction at 0x10004; marker at 0x10000; br at 0x10008 \u2192 self \u2713. live/attrib complete divu \u2192 pc 0x10008 \u2713.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 div0 row: pc=0x00010004 at HALT \u2014 the fallback's `m88k_pc_to_pointers(cpu)` then return; pc remains 0x10004 (the faulting instruction; the fallback does NOT advance pc) \u2713. r2=0xdeadbeef unchanged \u2713 (fallback never wrote r2 \u2014 and importantly the divu's writeback never happened because the raise precedes writeback... presumably; r2 unchanged is consistent).\n\nOne more check: the liveness marker `addu r9,r0,r10` \u2014 proves the planted program executes (the row's DONE/HALT is attributable to the planted instructions, not to a plant failure). But in the HALT rows, what proves the marker RAN before the fault? The fault rows show pc=0x10004 = the test instruction's address \u2014 reaching it means 0x10000 executed. \u2713 attribution covered. And attrib control separates divisor-caused. OK \u2014 the witness design is sound as far as the packet shows.\n\n27. **M_VEC4 mutant**: `+ 4 * vector` instead of 8*vector \u2014 breakpoint not hit, pc mismatch \u2713 caught. \u2713 8 vs 4: the 88100 vector spacing is 8 bytes? Hmm! WAIT. MC88100 exception vectors: the vector offsets are $20, $28, $30... i.e., 8 bytes apart (vectors 4,5,6 \u2192 0x20,0x28,0x30). Yes, MC88100 vectors are 8-byte spaced (each vector slot 8 bytes \u2014 enough for two instructions + branch? Actually the 88100 vector table has 8-byte entries? Hmm \u2014 offset $20 for vector 4 \u2192 8*4=0x20 \u2713. The manual Table 6-1 as cited. \u2713 8*vector is right per the quoted offsets. And 0x80 syscall = vector 128 \u2192 8*128 = 0x400. OpenBSD m88k vector base... fine.)\n\n28. **M_MODE mutant**: \"skip the PSR supervisor switch: PSR row reds\" \u2713 the delivered rows assert PSR bits \u2713 caught. \u2713.\n\n29. **M_NOSHADOW**: \"update_shadow_regs = false for the five: SXIP stale\" \u2014 rows assert SXIP \u2713 caught. \u2713.\n\n30. **M_REVERT5**: drop implemented per class \u2192 fallback halt \u2192 RESULT red \u2713.\n\nSo the kill table's catches are mostly supported EXCEPT M_SNIPX's claimed catch (SNIP not in the row assertion list \u2014 \"the div0 row loops\" doesn't happen without an rte; the row must READ SNIP at the breakpoint; if it does, fine, but \u00a76's list omits it \u2014 inconsistency to flag) and M_LEVEL (disclosed escape).\n\nAlso the delay-slot question: \"should one [row execute a faulting instruction in a delay slot]?\" \u2014 YES: because the five's tail cases are bare breaks, meaning the DELAY-SLOT common-path arm governs (the `else` arm: SNIP = pc+4, SFIP = delay_target \u2014 with author's own \"Perhaps something like this could work\"). For a faulting delay-slot instruction, hardware 88100: SXIP = delay slot inst, SNIP = branch TARGET (so rte resumes at target). The fork's else-arm sets SNIP = pc+4 (the instruction AFTER the delay slot \u2014 which on a taken branch is never executed!) and SFIP = delay_target \u2014 that looks WRONG (swapped semantics vs DATA_ACCESS's arm which sets SNIP = delay_target). So D1's bare-break for the five in delay slots likely produces SNIP = wrong-slot \u2014 guest-visible if a handler rtes. Since the manual quote for these five says SXIP=faulting and rte-at-SNIP, and for delay slots the SNIP should be... hmm, actually careful: what does the 88100 do for an exception in a delay slot of a TAKEN branch? The shadow: SXIP = delay-slot instruction address; SNIP = branch target address. rte resumes at SNIP = target \u2713. The fork's else arm gives SNIP = pc+4 = (delay slot +4)?? wait pc in a delay slot = branch_addr+4? The fork's pc during delay-slot execution = the delay slot instruction's address (branch+4? hmm, in GXemul, when executing the delay slot, cpu->pc = delay slot address = branch+4; so pc+4 = branch+8 = the fall-through, never executed on taken branch). So SNIP = fall-through \u2260 target \u2192 WRONG vs hardware. DATA_ACCESS arm gets it right (SNIP = delay_target). So for the five, D1's bare break leaves the suspicious else-arm. NO ROW tests it (disclosed). Answer to the QUESTION: yes, add a delay-slot row (e.g., `br` to a label with `divu r2,r3,r0` in the slot; assert SNIP == delay_target | V \u2014 and note this may RED the design itself, i.e., the row might catch the DESIGN, not just mutants \u2014 the fix may need the delay-slot arm for the five to set SNIP = delay_target like DATA_ACCESS). Actually hmm \u2014 wait. Which is right per the manual for trap exceptions in delay slots? The manual (not quoted) covers exceptions in delay slots (\"if the exception occurred in a delay slot...\"). The brief's \u00a74 contract (\"SXIP = faulting, SNIP = next instruction\") is for the non-delay-slot case. For delay slots, \"next instruction\" = branch target. The fork's else-arm comment \"Perhaps something like this could work\" shows upstream doubt. So: the brief's claim \"the manual's rte-at-SNIP semantics are the common path's defaults\" is TRUE only for non-delay-slot raises; in a delay slot the common path's else-arm is doubtful and D1 inherits it. This is my strongest substantive finding for Q1/the design: D1's \"no SNIP/SFIP adjustment is correct\" premise holds only outside delay slots; inside delay slots the bare break leaves a suspect arm that no row covers. Either add a row (and likely a tail-arm fix mirroring DATA_ACCESS: SNIP = delay_target) or explicitly scope delay slots out with a comment.\n\nHmm wait, let me re-read the else arm: it's in `if (cpu->delay_slot)` \u2192 DATA_ACCESS \u2192 SNIP=delay_target; INSTRUCTION_ACCESS \u2192 SNIP=delay_target, SFIP=0; else (all other vectors incl. the five after D1) \u2192 SNIP = (pc+4)|V, SFIP = delay_target|V. So for the five in delay slots, SNIP = fall-through, SFIP = target. rte resumes at SNIP = fall-through \u2014 wrong for taken branches (delay_slot is set only for taken branches presumably). So YES the else-arm is wrong-ish vs hardware for the five. But hold on \u2014 is it? What does hardware do for a TRAP exception in a delay slot \u2014 hmm, actually there's a subtlety: the 88100 manual says trap exceptions in delay slots: \"if a trap exception occurs in a delay slot, the SNIP contains the branch target\" \u2014 I believe so. So the else-arm is upstream's guess (\"Perhaps something like this could work\"). Flag it.\n\n31. **Q3 precedent**: can't grep (packet-fed). I can reason from GXemul knowledge: MIPS core delivers all standard exceptions (address error, TLB miss, etc.) even when partially modeled (GXemul's MIPS TLB handling is simplified but delivers). So precedent favors delivering recognized classes. But honestly: I should state \"not checkable from packet; the settle-fact is the other cores' exception functions (anchors: `mips_cpu_exception`, `sh_exception`)\" and give my best-knowledge prior: GXemul's MIPS delivers exceptions it only partially models rather than halting; halting is reserved for emulator-internal errors. That supports D1's shape and Q-D1b (include BOUNDS for consistency = minor; precedent suggests the set of \"recognized per manual but unraisable\" is delivered-with-comment).\n\nAlso Q3 asks \"Is there a member this brief did not name?\" \u2014 the option set: D1 (deliver via common path), D2 (naive flag), D3 (decline/suppress \u2014 rejected). Missing members: (i) **D1' = deliver with raise-side gating fixes** (OVE/MXM gates) \u2014 i.e., delivering the class only when the architectural raise condition holds; without it, D1 delivers exceptions hardware wouldn't take (overflow without OVE). This is a real member: \"deliver the five AS THE HARDWARE WOULD TAKE THEM\", which may include NOT raising overflow when OVE clear \u2014 that's a raise-site change, in scope? The brief's scope is exception DELIVERY (the switch), not raise conditions \u2014 but delivering an ungated raise changes guest semantics. At minimum DISCLOSE. (ii) **delay-slot tail-arm variant** (per above). (iii) A member that routes the five through a common `trap` label \u2014 cosmetic. (iv) Implementing BOUNDS raise (tbnd) \u2014 scope-expanding member. I'll name (i) and (ii).\n\n32. Let me also double-check the claimed arithmetic: \"PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION (vector 6, VBR+0x30)\" \u2192 8*6=0x30 \u2713; ILLEGAL_DIVIDE 8 \u2192 0x40 \u2713; OVERFLOW 9 \u2192 0x48 \u2713; MISALIGNED 4 \u2192 0x20 \u2713; UNIMPL 5 \u2192 0x28 \u2713; BOUNDS 7 \u2192 0x38 \u2713; ERROR 10 \u2192 0x50 \u2713. And the probe breakpoints `breakpoint add <8*vector>`: div0 \u2192 0x40 \u2713 matches \"DELIVER @0x40\". misalign @0x20 \u2713, ovf @0x48 \u2713. \u2713 all consistent.\n\nAnd \"VBR resets to 0, so vector v lands at 8*v in testm88k RAM\" \u2713 premise; probe's pc=8*vector asserts it.\n\n33. **The witness \"repeated\"**: \"[ exception ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE: ] [ exception unimplemented: vector=0x008 ... ] (repeated)\" \u2014 repeated? Why would the div0 row print the exception messages twice? A HALT row: the debugmsg pair printed twice \u2014 maybe the emulator re-executes the instruction (the fallback keeps pc at the faulting instruction; if the debugger `continue` was issued twice, or the main loop retried the same pc before noticing running=false? The fallback sets running=false then returns; emul.c breaks the main loop \u2192 shouldn't re-execute. \"(repeated)\" suggests the pair printed twice \u2014 possibly the probe did breakpoint-continue twice, or the debugmsg is emitted on a second attempt. Hmm \u2014 could be: first `continue` from 0x10000 executes marker + divu \u2192 messages \u2192 halt. Why repeat? Unless the probe's flow (breakpoint add 0x40? no \u2014 0x40 is never reached in the HALT row since delivery never happens!). WAIT. The HALT rows: `breakpoint add <8*vector>` = add breakpoint at 0x40 \u2014 but the machine HALTS at pc=0x10004 (fallback), never reaching 0x40. So how did the probe get its output? The probe: plant, seed, `breakpoint add 0x40`, `continue`, `reg`. `continue` runs until breakpoint OR halt. The HALT rows: machine halts at 0x10004 with the messages; `continue` returns on halt; `reg` shows pc=0x10004, halted=True \u2713. So \"(repeated)\" \u2014 maybe `continue` was called again after halt (the probe loop?) and GXemul re-executes the same instruction after `continue` from a halted state? If emul.c's loop broke, a debugger `continue` might restart the loop, re-executing pc=0x10004 \u2192 same messages \u2192 \"repeated\". Plausible. Or the debugmsg pair is printed once per attempt and the probe attempted twice. It doesn't affect the verdict; but it's an observable that a measuring seat might explain. I'll note it as an unexplained observable in the witness (the pair printing twice is consistent with a debugger re-continue, not with double-delivery \u2014 and in the HALT state pc stayed 0x10004). Minor; maybe mention in passing.\n\n34. **The brief's own premise check on \"SFU1_PRECISE 58\"**: 58 raise sites for SFU1 precise \u2014 plausible (every FP instruction raising when SFD1 set). And the separate row m88kexcsfu1 \"needs FP exception registers the fork never writes\" \u2014 premise, not checkable. Fine.\n\n35. **Q1's \"Is the raise-site census right?\"** \u2014 not checkable from packet; the deriver command is given; the domain question (cpu_m88k*.c only) \u2014 I noted devices might raise; also the census counts are claims. Settle-fact: rerun the deriver grep, or a file-reader eyeballs the anchors. I'll note: the census's DOMAIN premise (all raise sites live in cpu_m88k*.c) is itself unverified \u2014 a raise site in, say, a FPU device file would be missed; but m88k_exception is CPU-internal (takes struct cpu*), so unlikely. Accept with caveat.\n\n36. Also check: \"M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START\" = 128 implied by syscall 0x80 comment \"up to OpenBSD 5.2\" \u2014 OpenBSD m88k syscall trap vector 128/0x80 then 0x1c2 (t vector 450? 0x1c2=450; OpenBSD/luna88k uses trap 450 for syscalls from 5.3). \u2713 consistent with m88k OpenBSD lore (trap #450 = sys_sparc... hmm, m88k syscall trap 450 \u2014 yes, OpenBSD m88k uses vector 453? I recall T_CALLTRAP type 450. Fine, plausible.)\n\n37. **The header claim \"the m88k exception core stops every guest CPU for five trap-like exception classes\"** \u2014 accurate per the fallback loop \u2713.\n\n38. One more: D1 says \"replacing the `#if 0` ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE block\" \u2014 the #if 0 block is in the TAIL switch; deleting the dead block and adding live cases \u2713 fine.\n\n39. The detector section says rows assert \"PSR has SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE\" \u2014 for the PRIV row, the raise happens IN USER MODE (PSR.MODE clear); delivery sets MODE \u2713 the assertion holds post-delivery \u2713. And EPSR should equal the USER PSR (MODE clear, IND clear) \u2014 not asserted \u2192 EPSR escape stands. For the div0 row EPSR = MODE|IND (the reset PSR) \u2014 assertable cheaply.\n\n40. Also \u2014 \"the machine still RUNNING -- no `All machines stopped`\": under D1 delivery, after delivery the CPU continues executing at 0x40 (zeros). The probe must check \"running\" BEFORE the zeros cause a second exception (if word 0 raises one). The probe breaks AT 0x40 before executing \u2192 reads state \u2192 asserts running \u2713. But if the probe then CONTINUES (to finish the row), the zeros at 0x40 execute \u2014 if 0x00000000 is an unimplemented opcode \u2192 vector 5 \u2192 SFRZ set \u2192 (is_trap? if true, deliver vector 5 again with frozen shadows; pc = 0x28; zeros there too \u2192 vector 5 again \u2192 infinite loop of vector-5 deliveries with SFRZ set \u2192 trap-type so no ERROR re-route \u2192 INFINITE LOOP, machine \"running\" forever, probe `continue` never returns \u2192 detector TIMEOUT). Hmm! That's a practical detector concern: after the delivered-row breakpoint, continuing runs into zeros \u2192 potential infinite exception loop (or halt via the ERROR latch path if is_trap=false). The probe should kill at the breakpoint (assert, then stop the row) rather than continue. The packet says rows assert at the breakpoint then presumably end the emulator. Worth one line: \"delivered rows must not `continue` past the breakpoint unless a handler (e.g., an rte loop) is planted at 8*vector; zeros at the vector slot make the post-breakpoint behavior undefined-ish (loop or double-fault-halt) \u2014 plant a minimal handler (e.g., `br .`) at 8*vector to make `continue` deterministic.\" Actually better: plant a handler with rte to test the SNIP contract \u2014 that would catch M_SNIPX naturally (\"the div0 row loops\" \u2014 OH WAIT. That's what the kill table meant by \"the div0 row loops\"! If the probe plants an rte handler at 0x40: delivered \u2192 handler: rte \u2192 resumes at SNIP = 0x10008 (br .) \u2192 row completes (pc=0x10008, RUNNING) \u2713 DELIVER signature. Under M_SNIPX (SNIP = pc = 0x10004): rte \u2192 re-execute divu \u2192 divide by zero again \u2192 SFRZ set \u2192 trap \u2192 deliver again \u2192 handler rte \u2192 loop forever \u2192 \"the div0 row loops\" \u2713 observable as timeout / breakpoint hit repeatedly with SNIP==SXIP. THAT makes sense of the kill table! So the detector DOES plant an rte handler at 8*vector \u2014 but \u00a76's row description doesn't SAY so; it says \"each asserts RESULT and, for delivered rows, pc == 8*vector, SXIP == ..., PSR ..., r2 unchanged, and the machine still RUNNING\". Hmm \u2014 if there's an rte handler, the final state would be pc=0x10008 (back at br .) not pc == 8*vector... unless the assertions are read AT the breakpoint (pc==8*vector at breakpoint \u2713) and then the row continues through rte to prove the SNIP contract (final pc = 0x10008, running). So the intended flow: break at 8*vector, assert pc/SXIP/PSR/r2/running, then continue through the rte handler, assert final pc == 0x10008 (rte-at-SNIP proven) \u2014 but \u00a76 omits the handler/rte/continue and the final-pc assertion. The kill table's M_SNIPX phrasing implies the rte handler exists. INCONSISTENCY between \u00a76's stated row contract and the kill table's assumed probe. Name it: \u00a76 must specify the handler plant (word at 8*vector = rte? rte encoding needed!) and the post-rte assertion (pc == 0x10008, SNIP consumed). Without it, M_SNIPX's claimed catch is unsupported AND the delivered rows' post-breakpoint behavior is nondeterministic (zeros \u2192 loop/halt). Also note: rte at 0x40 requires the rte ENCODING (another encoding premise not in the packet \u2014 the packet gave divu/add/ld/br but not rte; and the probe's existing rows don't use rte). And the handler must also be careful: rte in a machine where SFRZ handling... rte restores PSR from EPSR \u2713. So the detector needs: plant `rte` at 8*vector, break there, assert, continue, assert return to 0x10008. This is a constructive improvement AND resolves the M_SNIPX support gap. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 under the CURRENT design (before fix), the HALT rows: probe plants rte at 0x40 too? The witness output shows the HALT rows stopped at pc=0x10004 with \"All machines stopped\" \u2014 the breakpoint at 0x40 never hit; fine either way.\n\nAlso: does the debugger `continue` after a delivered exception with an rte handler work? Sure.\n\n41. Now Q2's expected answer: \"The main loop expects M_LEVEL to escape (disclosed) -- what else?\" My answers: (i) EPSR save removed \u2014 no row reads EPSR (unless the rte handler makes PSR restoration observable! If the row continues through rte back to 0x10008, then PSR after rte = EPSR \u2014 if EPSR save were removed, EPSR would be stale/garbage \u2192 PSR after rte wrong \u2192 ASSERTABLE. So with the rte-handler flow, EPSR escape closes! Without it, open.) (ii) DMT0/SSBR/fcr \u2014 disclosed-ish (scoreboard/fcr disclosed; DMT0 for the misalign row not named \u2014 add an assert DMT0==0 cheaply). (iii) The delay-slot arm \u2014 disclosed as untested; the escape: a mutant flipping the five's delay-slot else-arm to the DATA_ACCESS-style arm (or vice versa) passes; answer the QUESTION with \"yes, add a delay-slot row\". (iv) The SFRZ re-route latch / is_trap: no row double-faults \u2192 mutants around the re-route escape. (v) SFIP \u2014 no row asserts SFIP (minor). (vi) The message LEVEL \u2014 disclosed. So my Q2 list: EPSR (if no rte-handler flow), DMT0 on misalign, SFIP, delay-slot arm, double-fault latch, plus any raise-side gate (OVE) \u2014 no row sets OVE, so a mutant adding/removing an OVE gate passes (and NOTE: the DESIGN ITSELF may need the OVE gate \u2014 the detector can't see it either way). The single smallest: I'd name \"delete the EPSR save\" as the smallest edit breaking D1 (rte returns with wrong PSR) that passes the stated detector \u2014 with the fix being to add the rte-through-handler final assertion. Actually hmm \u2014 \"breaks D1\" \u2014 does breaking EPSR break D1? D1 is \"deliver the five\"; a guest that rtes from the handler gets a wrong PSR (user code running supervisor? PSR from EPSR... if EPSR save removed, EPSR holds garbage/old value \u2192 rte sets PSR from EPSR \u2192 possibly still supervisor w/ SFRZ \u2192 next user instruction raises privilege violations \u2192 guest chaos). Yes \u2014 breaks the delivered semantics while all \u00a76 assertions stay green (SXIP/pc/PSR-at-breakpoint/r2/running all fine). That's my named smallest edit.\n\n42. Also check D1's claim \"Keep the `unimplemented` fallback's text\" \u2713 fine.\n\n43. **\"gate 11 (`gate_m88k_rounding.sh`) as a new section\"** \u2014 wiring an exception probe into a gate named \"rounding\" \u2014 naming oddity; also the brief says gate 11 gets \"a new section\" \u2014 fine. The operational note: don't run gates. OK.\n\n44. Also \u2014 Q-D1d: \"DMT0 is zeroed by the common path for every vector but DATA_ACCESS -- correct for a PRECISE exception detected before dispatch (manual 9236-9237)?\" \u2014 manual quote 9231-9240 says misaligned is \"detected before the memory access is dispatched to the data unit\" \u2713 so DMT0=0 (no transaction in progress) \u2713 correct per the quote. \u2713.\n\n45. Q-D1e: fcr2/fcr4/fcr5 not written; OpenBSD SIGFPE path doesn't read them (belief). Not checkable from packet (author says \"NOT on disk\" \u2014 OpenBSD source isn't in the tree). Settle-fact: OpenBSD m88k trap code (sys/arch/m88k/m88k/trap.c) \u2014 a file-reading seat with OpenBSD source could check; else condition on it. For the round: follow-up row, agree \u2014 but note the divide exception's handler contract per manual NEEDS fcr2/4/5 only if the handler emulates; OpenBSD's T_ZERODIV path delivers SIGFPE via the process context (registers), not fcr \u2014 plausibly doesn't read them. Condition \u2713.\n\n46. Also Q-D1c's framing: \"the halt and the panic are both 'guest dead' -- but the guest's panic message is the guest's, and the emulator's halt is ours\" \u2014 I agree with OUT OF SCOPE + file; but note the LATCH subtlety makes the ERROR story slightly different post-D1 (double faults from delivered trap classes don't reach ERROR at all if is_trap=true; they re-deliver the same vector with frozen shadows). So the ERROR row's premise (\"ERROR is only the internal re-route\") is incomplete once D1 ships: trap-class double faults bypass ERROR entirely. That's fine per hardware (traps don't re-route), but the brief's Q-D1c discussion should note it.\n\n47. Also the header: \"HAS THIS ROUND ALREADY SHIPPED?\" \u2014 grep -c m88kexcdeliver CHANGELOG.md \u2192 1 (the filing) \u2713 consistent with \"not shipped\". Not checkable, premise. Fine.\n\n48. Also check the claim \"SFU1 is the separate row `m88kexcsfu1` (needs FP exception registers the fork never writes)\" \u2014 premise; fine.\n\n49. The brief's \u00a71 claim: \"Nine other switch cases print a VERBOSITY_ERROR debugmsg and `break`, so `if (vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented)` prints ... sets running=false ... and returns.\" \u2014 the \"so\" is fine for the nine when raised. \u2713. But note: the fallback ALSO fires for `default:` unknown vectors < 128 with implemented=false \u2713 (\"unknown vector\" then \"unimplemented\") \u2014 consistent with D1's fallback retention.\n\n50. One more arithmetic check \u2014 the exemplar calibration: \"or r0,r0,r0 = f4005800\" \u2014 with sub field bits 15-8: f4005800 \u2192 bits 15-8 = 0x58 \u2713 'or' sub-op 0x58 \u2014 in the real 88100, or = 0x58? The 88100 extended opcodes: and = 0x5c? or = 0x58? xor = 0x5ac? Hmm: I recall and=0x5c, or=0x58, xor=0x5a?? Something like that. Plausible \u2713. divu = 0x68: real 88100 divu extended opcode... div = 0x60, divu = 0x64?? Hmm. Actually \u2014 88100: div (0x64?) divu (0x68?)... I have genuine uncertainty. But the internal control (live row: same-encoding divu with r4=0x10 \u2192 r2=6) validates that the FORK decodes f4436800 as divu \u2014 which is what matters for the probe. If the fork's divu sub-op were wrong vs the real ISA, that's a separate fork bug not in scope. The probe is self-consistent. \u2713 The brief's warning \"a wrong encoding would fake the symptom\" is addressed by the live control \u2713 \u2014 good design, confirmed by the packet.\n\nWait, actually, let me double-check the claimed divu sub-op 0x68 against the live row more carefully: live row instruction \u2014 \"divu r2,r3,r4 with r3=0x60, r4=0x10 -> 6\". Its encoding per the brief's list: `divu r2,r3,r0` = f4436800 \u2014 but live uses r4! So live's word = f4436800 | 4 = f4436804. The brief lists the div0 encoding (r0); live is the same with S2=4. OK fine \u2014 0x60/0x10 = 6 \u2713 (0x60=96, 0x10=16, 96/16=6 \u2713 READ-arithmetic \u2713). And attrib r2=1: if attrib = divu r2,r3,rX with r3=rX (e.g., both 0x10) \u2192 1. The mystery stands only if \"SAME divu\" is literal. I'll flag it as a wording premise: if the attrib word is byte-identical to div0's (S2=r0), the row is impossible on a hardwired-r0 core and the fork must lack r0-hardwiring (a finding); if it differs in S2, the \"SAME\" is false-but-harmless. Settle-fact: the attrib row's planted word + the fork's r0 write guard (grep anchor: `r[0]` guard in the decoder or `case 0x3d` writeback; or whether `rN=` seeding of r0 sticks \u2014 probe could `put`... actually simplest: the probe script line for the attrib row).\n\nHmm, one more consideration on attrib: could attrib be `divu r2,r3,r0` with r3 seeded and... no \u2014 divisor r0=0 \u2192 fault. DONE impossible. So the word MUST differ. UNLESS the fork doesn't hardwire r0 AND the probe seeded r0 nonzero for attrib AND zero (default) for div0. Then \"the SAME divu as div0 with a non-zero divisor\" is LITERALLY accurate and the fork lacks r0 hardwiring \u2014 wait, but then in the div0 row r0 must read 0 \u2014 if r0 isn't hardwired, r0 reads whatever was last written; at reset r[] presumably zeroed \u2192 div0 works \u2713; attrib seeds r0=0x10 \u2192 divisor nonzero \u2192 DONE \u2713 r3/r0: r2=1 \u2192 r3 = r0? if r3 seeded = r0's value... e.g., seed r3=0x10, r0=0x10 \u2192 1 \u2713. So BOTH scenarios are internally consistent; they differ in whether the fork hardwires r0. SCENARIO B implies a fork divergence (r0 writable) that the packet would then contain evidence for \u2014 but the packet doesn't state it either way. Either way the control's VALIDITY is intact (a nonzero divisor divu completes) \u2014 but the packet should say which. Also scenario B would mean the fork models r0 as a normal register, which would break tons of things (addu r9,r0,r10 liveness marker reads r0 \u2014 if r0 seeded 0x10 in attrib, the marker still just adds r0 to r10 \u2014 fine). Not decidable. FLAG as the one witness ambiguity. Note it matters for the DETECTOR too (the detector reuses these rows).\n\n51. Also \u2014 \"calibrated on the exemplar's `or r0,r0,r0` = f4005800\" \u2014 where does the exemplar come from? testm88k's default RAM contents? Not stated. Minor premise (that f4005800 is `or r0,r0,r0` per the fork's decoder). The divu validation via live covers the needed encodings anyway.\n\n52. Priority order for the answer. The verdict: the core reading is CORRECT (D1 delivers, D2 halts at tail-default, fallback premises check out, encodings internally consistent with controls, witness supports the symptom), BUT with named defects: (1) \u00a76 row contract vs kill-table inconsistency (SNIP/rte-handler \u2014 M_SNIPX's catch unsupported as stated; delivered rows' post-breakpoint behavior unspecified); (2) the delay-slot arm is inherited by D1's bare breaks and is suspect vs hardware \u2014 uncovered; (3) OVE (and MXM) raise-side gates unaddressed \u2014 delivering overflow without the OVE gate diverges from hardware (evidence: ovf row raises at reset PSR lacking OVE) \u2014 potential guest-visible change; (4) is_trap values at raise sites not in packet (double-fault semantics, ERROR re-route latch \u2014 `implemented` latched pre-re-route); (5) attrib-row \"SAME divu\" ambiguity / r0-hardwiring premise; (6) EPSR unasserted \u2192 smallest escaping edit; (7) uncheckable-from-packet premises named (raise census, tbnd disassembler-only, m88k_stcr mode handling, stcr/ldcr encodings, translation-cache mode tagging, gate-6 rows, other cores' precedent, OpenBSD fcr belief).\n\nVerdict line: something like \"VERDICT: CONFIRM WITH CORRECTIONS \u2014 D1's two-site reading is right and D2 halts as claimed; but the detector as specified in \u00a76 cannot support the M_SNIPX catch it claims, D1 inherits a suspect delay-slot arm for the five, and the overflow raise appears to ignore PSR.OVE (evidence in the packet's own witness), which makes D1 deliver exceptions hardware would not take.\"\n\nLet me also double-check the OVE evidence once more. Reset PSR = M88K_PSR_MODE | M88K_PSR_IND (premise from anchor). Is OVE possibly part of MODE|IND constants? No \u2014 MODE and IND are individual bits (mode, interrupt-disable). OVE is a separate bit (bit 5? whatever). So reset PSR has OVE=0. The ovf row: add r2,r3,r4 with r3=0x7fffffff, r4=1 \u2192 the fork raised vector 0x009 (probe output READ). On real hardware with OVE=0, this add would WRAP (store 0x80000000) with NO exception. So either (a) the fork doesn't implement the OVE gate \u2192 raises regardless \u2192 divergence, or (b) the probe seeded cr1/PSR with OVE before running (not stated anywhere; the seeding described is `rN=` registers only), or (c) M88K_PSR reset includes OVE via some constant (not stated; the anchor says MODE|IND explicitly). So (a) is the REASONED conclusion with (b)/(c) as escape hatches. The settle-fact: the X(add) overflow raise site's guard (does it test `& M88K_PSR_OVE`?) \u2014 grep anchor `M88K_EXCEPTION_INTEGER_OVERFLOW` in cpu_m88k.c showing its `if` condition; plus whether the probe seeds PSR. This is exactly the kind of \"premise only works if you already believe the author\" audit the panel wants: the brief presents INTEGER_OVERFLOW as a trap-like class to deliver, but the packet's own witness shows the raise firing under a PSR where hardware wouldn't trap. Delivering it turns a halt into a SIGFPE-delivery for wraparound arithmetic \u2014 arguably WORSE for guest fidelity than the halt in that one case? No wait \u2014 halting is always worse than anything. But it's a divergence either way; the RIGHT fix gates on OVE. At minimum D1 should disclose or the round should add the gate.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 but actually, hold on: does the real 88100 even take integer overflow only when OVE set? Yes \u2014 PSR.OVE: \"overflow exception enable\". If OVE=0, overflow results are stored (wrapped), no exception. Confirmed by my knowledge of the manual. And OpenBSD m88k kernel: sets OVE in PSR for... userland? OpenBSD/luna88k runs userland with OVE clear I believe (so C int overflow wraps). If the fork delivered overflow unconditionally, userland arithmetic overflow \u2192 T_OVFFLT \u2192 SIGFPE \u2014 chaos. Strong finding. But CONDITION it properly: the manual's 6.6 text is NOT inlined (the brief itself says \"not re-extracted \u2014 condition on it\"), and the OVE bit is my knowledge \u2014 so I present it as: the packet's own facts (reset PSR = MODE|IND; ovf row raised) are in tension with the architectural gate (OVE) that \u00a74 explicitly leaves un-extracted; a file-reader must check manual 6.6 for the OVE condition and the raise site's guard. Name the single settle-facts.\n\nSimilarly MXM for misaligned: reset PSR lacks MXM \u2192 misalign raises \u2713 consistent with hardware (MXM clear \u2192 exception) \u2014 so the misalign row does NOT evidence a missing gate. But whether the fork CHECKS MXM when the guest sets it (OpenBSD might set MXM=1? If OpenBSD/luna88k sets MXM to avoid misaligned traps... I don't know) \u2014 if the guest sets MXM and the fork still raises, divergence. Unverifiable from packet; lower priority than OVE (no packet evidence either way). Name as a sibling premise to check at the same raise site.\n\n53. Also the \"58 SFU1_PRECISE\" \u2014 no effect on scope. Fine.\n\n54. Q4 answer summary: what in a booted system raises the five: userland div-by-zero (divu/div \u2014 also `div` with negative operand per manual 10300-10305 \"either operand is negative in a div\" \u2014 note the census says the fork raises div0 for \"divu/div by zero\" \u2014 does the fork also raise for NEGATIVE operands in `div` (the manual's second condition)? The census says \"divu/div by zero\" only \u2014 if the fork doesn't implement the negative-operand-div condition, that's another raise-side gap (a `div` with negative operands and OVE... hmm, the negative-operand condition raises integer-divide regardless of OVE? The manual says div with negative operand \u2192 exception (because 88100 div is unsigned-ish via FPU?). Actually: \"occurs when the divisor of a div or divu instruction is zero or when either operand is negative in a div instruction\" \u2014 yes, MC88100 `div` raises on ANY negative operand (its signed divide is emulated in software!). OpenBSD/luna88k implements signed division via the kernel exception handler (T_ZERODIV handler emulates signed div using fcr2/fcr4!!) \u2014 OH. That's important: OpenBSD's m88k divide handler DOES use fcr2/fcr4/fcr5 to EMULATE signed division for negative operands! I recall OpenBSD m88k `m88100_trap` T_ZERODIV path reading fpsr/fcr... hmm, actually I recall the OpenBSD m88k code has `M88K_SIGNED_DIV`... hmm, or luna88k's compiler emits a branch-tree for signed div instead of trapping. Do I actually recall OpenBSD m88k reading fcr2/fcr4 in trap.c? I believe there IS such code: the 88100 divu only does unsigned; signed div with negative operands traps, and the OS (or libgcc) emulates. OpenBSD m88k trap.c: case T_ZERODIV: it checks fcr62... I genuinely recall something about `fcr62` (the exception register showing the cause) in OpenBSD m88k. The brief's Q-D1e says \"OpenBSD's SIGFPE path does not read them (belief)\". My knowledge whispers the signed-DIV emulation path DOES read fcr registers (the kernel emulates the division to produce a signed quotient!). Hmm \u2014 if OpenBSD/luna88k's trap handler for divide emulates signed division using fcr2/fcr4/fcr5 (source operands and dest reg), then NOT writing them breaks the guest's signed-division emulation \u2192 wrong results or crash \u2014 a much bigger deal than SIGFPE. But: does OpenBSD/luna88k use trapping signed div, or does its compiler emit inline checks? OpenBSD/luna88k: I believe gcc for m88k emits __divv... calls? Hmm. Honestly uncertain. The right move: FLAG the belief as contested \u2014 my prior contradicts the brief's belief; the settle-fact is OpenBSD's m88k trap.c T_ZERODIV handling (read fcr2/fcr4/fcr5? or just SIGFPE) \u2014 which is NOT on disk per the brief, so a measuring seat would need to boot the rig and test a signed negative divide in userland. ALSO the fork's div raise census (\"divu/div by zero\" only, 4 sites) suggests the fork does NOT raise on negative-operand `div` \u2014 if so, the fork implements signed div directly in X(div) (divergence from hardware: hardware can't do signed div at all!) \u2014 wait, hardware `div` DOES exist but traps on negative operands; the fork's X(div) presumably computes signed division directly (that's why no raise site for negative operands). So the fork diverges: signed div completes instead of trapping. For D1's scope (delivery), this doesn't matter \u2014 no raise. But Q-D1e's belief (\"OpenBSD SIGFPE path doesn't read fcr\") and my counter-recollection matter for the follow-up row. Present it as: premise contested, name the settle-fact.\n\nOK. Also Q4: \"ldcr from userland\" \u2014 userland wouldn't normally; kernel probes? Also `tb1/tb0 tcnd` from userland \u2192 privilege violation (traps 0-127 in user mode \u2014 wait, tb1 r0,r0,imm17 with vector<128 in user mode \u2192 PRIVILEGE per manual; but tcnd/tb1 are ALSO how breakpoints/syscalls... OpenBSD m88k userland uses `tb1 0,r0,453`? no \u2014 0x1c2=450 for syscalls \u2265128 \u2713 allowed in user mode). Fine.\n\n55. \"Is there a boot-gate row (gate_ab) whose behaviour could CHANGE\" \u2014 answer: any row whose guest output contains an expected HALT from these vectors; also rows that RUN OpenBSD userland where a program overflows (unlikely in a boot gate). The real risk: the OVE divergence \u2014 if any gate row's guest code does wraparound arithmetic, post-D1 it now traps \u2192 T_OVFFLT \u2192 OpenBSD delivers SIGFPE \u2192 program dies \u2192 gate row reds. BEFORE the fix it would have halted too (raise \u2192 fallback halt) \u2014 so any such row was already red; net: no previously-green row flips red from DELIVERY itself... unless the row EXPECTED the halt (a \"graceful halt\" expectation). Name the settle-fact: grep gate_ab rows for expected-halt/m88k expectations.\n\n56. What should a measuring seat run (Q4): (1) boot OpenBSD/luna88k on the rig pre/post fix; (2) post-fix userland tests: int div by zero (expect SIGFPE, machine alive); unaligned access (expect SIGBUS or kernel fixup, machine alive); signed negative divide (expect correct signed result \u2014 tests both the fork's non-trapping div AND whether anything reads fcr); add wraparound (expect wrap, NOT SIGFPE \u2014 the OVE test!); (3) a kernel-mode `ldcr` probe. And diff gate_ab outputs pre/post.\n\n57. Now \u2014 Q5 CHANGELOG: list as above.\n\nAlso the brief asks in \u00a77 Q1: \"Is the reading of the two switches right \u2014 does D1 deliver, and does D2 halt?\" \u2014 YES (READ from inlined function, traced). \"Is 'rte resumes at SNIP' the right contract for ALL five\" \u2014 YES for non-delay-slot (manual quotes + \"discard the instruction\" implies resume-after; SXIP-quote covers four of five, overflow conditioned); \"the #if 0 doubt about the divide: should the PC be backed up?\" \u2014 NO: SNIP=+4 is correct; backing up is the handler's choice. \"Are the encodings right?\" \u2014 internally consistent; divu validated by live control; ld/br self-consistent; stcr/ldcr NOT in packet (named); the attrib-row \"SAME divu\" ambiguity flagged. \"Is the raise-site census right?\" \u2014 not checkable; domain caveat; plus the census's own text reveals the negative-operand div gap and says nothing of OVE/MXM gates. \"Is SFD1 really clear at reset\" \u2014 YES, evidenced by the witness (div0 \u2192 vector 0x008, not an SFU1 vector) \u2014 the packet settles it.\n\n58. Also one more internal check \u2014 \u00a72 says \"a liveness marker `addu r9,r0,r10` first\" \u2014 addu sub-op? addu = 0x64? The marker's encoding isn't given; fine, not needed.\n\n59. And the D1 statement \"in the TAIL switch add the five as `case` labels with a bare `break`... replacing the `#if 0` ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE block\" \u2014 note the tail's `default:` prints \"internal error\" and stops the CPU \u2014 with the five added, default still catches BOUNDS/ERROR/SFU1 (which can't reach the tail since implemented=false \u2192 fallback returns first) \u2014 wait, CAN anything else reach the tail default post-D1? Vectors <128 with implemented=true: RESET, INTERRUPT, INST_ACCESS, DATA_ACCESS (have cases), the five (new cases). The ERROR re-route: implemented latched true for any class that passed \u2192 tail switch(vector=ERROR) \u2192 default \u2192 halt \u2713 (as analyzed). Unknown vector <128 \u2192 fallback (implemented=false) \u2192 returns before tail. So post-D1 the tail default is reachable ONLY via the ERROR re-route (from any implemented class double-faulting as non-trap) \u2014 i.e., \"internal error\" becomes the double-fault message. Worth stating in Q5/records and maybe D1 should add a comment there.\n\n60. Also \u2014 is_trap for INTERRUPT: the interrupt tail arm subtracts 4 from SNIP/SFIP (re-execute interrupted instruction) \u2014 fine, existing.\n\nLet me also sanity-check the claim in \u00a73's bold note: \"with `implemented = true` alone, the five vectors pass the fallback, take the common path, and then land in the vector-specific `default:`, which prints `internal error` and sets THIS cpu's `running = false`.\" \u2713 traced. \"On the single-CPU testm88k that is the same halt with a different message.\" \u2713 (message differs: \"internal error\" vs \"unimplemented vector=...\"; also the fallback stops ALL cpus vs this one \u2014 same on 1-CPU \u2713). \"The fix therefore has TWO sites in one function\" \u2713.\n\n61. Now, Q3's precedent \u2014 I must be honest: not checkable. I'll say: packet contains zero lines from other cores; settle-fact = the anchors (`mips_cpu_exception` in cpus/cpu_mips.c etc.). My prior (knowledge, labeled): GXemul's MIPS delivers even partially-modeled exceptions and reserves halting for emulator bugs \u2014 supporting D1. But the panel asked specifically; I'll answer with the caveat and the anchor.\n\n62. Length/format: VERDICT LINE FIRST. Label claims READ/REASONED. Never MEASURED. Keep tight but cover the questions. The instructions say answer ALL questions (\u00a77 Q1-5) and don't pad. I'll structure:\n\n- VERDICT LINE.\n- P1: premise audit of the two switches + fallback (READ) \u2014 confirm, with the enumeration (9 cases counted).\n- Findings list (numbered), each labeled, with settle-facts:\n  F1 delay-slot arm inherited by D1's bare breaks \u2014 suspect vs the manual contract; no row covers; answer \"should one?\" = YES.\n  F2 \u00a76 vs kill-table: SNIP/rte-handler inconsistency; M_SNIPX unsupported as specified; also post-breakpoint behavior at zeroed vector slots (loop via word-0 \u2192 or halt) \u2014 plant an rte handler; also EPSR assertion.\n  F3 OVE gate (packet's own witness evidences ungated raise) \u2014 biggest premise defect; plus MXM sibling; plus negative-operand div condition absent from census.\n  F4 is_trap values at raise sites unknown \u2192 double-fault semantics + implemented-latch interaction (tail-default becomes the double-fault halt post-D1).\n  F5 attrib row \"SAME divu\" ambiguity \u2192 r0-hardwiring premise; settle-fact.\n  F6 uncheckable premises: census counts/domain; tbnd disassembler-only; m88k_stcr mode switch; stcr/ldcr encodings (packet says so); translation-cache mode tagging; gate-6/gate_ab rows; other-core precedent; OpenBSD fcr belief (my counter-prior: signed-div emulation may read fcr \u2014 name the settle test).\n- Q2 answer: EPSR-save deletion (smallest, passes \u00a76 as written; closed if the rte-handler flow + final-pc assertion is added); also DMT0-on-misalign, SFIP, delay-slot arm, double-fault latch.\n- Q3: option-set members missing: D1-gated (raise-side gates), delay-slot tail variant; precedent answer with caveat; Q-D1a", "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 360692960198}
