regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass1\panel_20260905_143211\kimi.txt:461:  Let me also consider: should I actually BUILD and run the probe/mutants in my own temp dir? The operational rule says: "Building and running a single detector or mutant in your own temp directory is fine and encouraged." Hmm — this is PASS-1 (design review), and the detector ships WITH the fix; the fix hasn't been written. A seat COULD verify D2's predicted behavior (implemented=true only → "internal error" halt at pc=8*vector) by patching a scratch copy and rerunning the probe. That would be a strong verification of Q1's "does D2 halt". But building GXemul takes minutes and the probe drives `gxemul` with... the probe uses testm88k and debug commands (put w, breakpoint, continue) — presumably `gxemul -E testm88k -e ...`? with a script file. Doable but heavy. The brief says measurement seats exist; PASS-1 is design review. The witness (probe_head.out) is already inlined and on disk to inspect. I'll verify the witness file, not rerun. Maybe, IF time permits, I could run the existing probe against the existing build to confirm the HALT rows reproduce (the build exists at GXEMUL-SEC/build/gxemul? wait — the brief says "committed `build/gxemul`" — the binary is committed?? "run today against the committed build/gxemul (HEAD, rebuilt 14:17)" — hmm, committed binary? Whatever — check the file exists). Running the probe is "a single detector in my own temp dir" — arguably allowed and encouraged. But it writes to GXEMUL-SEC? The probe writes output where? I'd run it with cwd in my temp dir. Hmm — but the probe drives the harness's testm88k config from the repo... Risk: the probe might share log dirs with gates? The rule bans run.sh/gate_*.sh/battery; a hand-run probe script in _scratchpad is exactly "a single detector". I'll decide after reading the probe script. Actually — for PASS-1, re-running the witness adds little; the witness is already measured and my job is design review + premise audit. I'll prioritize the code audit. If cheap, I'll rerun the probe to confirm reproducibility (it's rung-3 evidence — rerunning strengthens but isn't required; the operator says agy not fired, reserved for measurement). Hmm, the brief says "If you want a measurement that is not inlined, NAME IT" — implying seats shouldn't necessarily run big measurements. The probe rerun: it's inlined already; rerunning just confirms. I'll likely skip unless something's off. Hmm — actually, one measurement IS worth doing if cheap: the D2 mutant (implemented=true only) to confirm "internal error" halt — that validates the kill-table's M_NAIVE prediction. But it requires rebuilding the tree (make in a copied build dir — minutes). PASS-1 design review... The main loop already knows D2 halts (it read the tail default). I'll name it as an optional confirmation instead. Actually wait — is it possible D2 does NOT halt? The tail default: `default:debugmsg_cpu(..., "internal error"); cpu->running = false;`. Yes it stops the CPU. No doubt. Skip.
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass1\panel_20260905_143211\kimi.txt:1009:  - CHANGELOG batch-2 block: filing confirmed (lines 68-73) — "Disposition DELIVER; witness owed at rung 3 before any edit" ✓ consistent with the brief. Also "Astra had scoped it to the tb1 privilege path; Fable widened it to the class and corrected the main loop's 'nine vectors' to 'nine switch cases halt, six reachable via a raise site today'" — SIX reachable? The census: PRIV 10, ILL_DIV 4, OVF 3, MISALIGN 1, UNIMPL 1 → five classes... "six reachable via a raise site" — the six = the five + BOUNDS? No — BOUNDS has no raise site. Six reachable of the nine halting cases: MISALIGNED, UNIMPL, PRIV, BOUNDS, ILL_DIV, OVF, ERROR, SFU1_PRECISE, SFU1_IMPRECISE = nine cases; reachable today: MISALIGNED(1), UNIMPL(1), PRIV(10), ILL_DIV(4), OVF(3), SFU1_PRECISE(58) = SIX classes reachable (including SFU1_PRECISE!). BOUNDS, ERROR, SFU1_IMPRECISE unreachable. ✓ consistent with my grep (58 SFU1_PRECISE sites). Good.
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass1\panel_20260905_143211\kimi.txt:1024:  One more: the M_MODE kill mutant ("skip the PSR supervisor switch: PSR row reds") — detector checks PSR has SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE ✓ (probe reads PSR via `reg 0,0` cr dump — the probe already extracts psr= but pre-fix it's None since the machine halted... the probe DOES dump CRs even after halt; psr=None pre-fix because... the CR dump happens after `reg 0,0` — pre-fix psr=None means the cr() regex didn't match — maybe the dump format differs or PSR line name differs. Post-fix the detector will assert on psr bits — the probe's cr("PSR") must match the dump format. That's an implementation detail for the round; the probe's psr=None on HALT rows is just because... hmm, actually why None? The `reg 0,0` command should dump control registers including PSR regardless of halt. Unless the dump prints "psr" lowercase or "PSR=0x..." with different spacing — the regex `\bPSR=0x([0-9a-f]{8})`. If the dump prints `PSR =0x...` or lowercase, None. The detector section will need the regex fixed to actually read SXIP/PSR — worth flagging: the rung-3 witness shows sxip=None psr=None on all rows INCLUDING the DONE rows where the machine kept running — so the CR extraction NEVER worked, even when it should have. The shipped detector must fix the CR parse (check actual `reg 0,0` output format) or the SXIP/PSR assertions will silently pass on None... wait no — they'd fail: DELIVER requires pc==8*vector; but sxip/psr assertions would compare None == expected → row would read "?"... The detector spec says rows assert SXIP==0x10004|V and PSR bits — with the current cr() extraction returning None, every delivered row would mis-report. So: flag that the probe's CR-dump parse needs verification against the actual `reg 0,0` output format (m88k_cpu_register_dump with coprocs — check the format string: m88k_cpu_register_dump at cpu_m88k.c:267; let me look at how it prints CRs — quick read 300-360).
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass1\panel_20260905_143211\kimi.txt:1044:  Wait — look at the probe: `m3 = send("reg 0,0")` — the debugger `reg` command syntax: `reg 0,0`? In GXemul's debugger, `reg [cpuid] [gprs],coprocs`? Hmm — actually the debugger's reg command syntax is `reg [cpu_nr] [gprs],[coprocs]`? The probe sent `reg 0,0` — if that parses as cpu_nr=0? and "0,0" → gprs=0, coprocs=0 → m88k_cpu_register_dump(cpu, gprs=0, coprocs=0) → dumps NEITHER gprs NOR coprocs! coprocs bit 0..3 — coprocs=0 means NO coproc dump → no CR output at all! That's why psr=None and sxip=None — the command dumped nothing but GPRs (and gprs=0 → nothing?). Actually `reg` with gprs=0, coprocs=0 → prints just pc maybe. The fix for the detector: `reg 0,1` (coprocs bit0 = CPU control registers) or whatever the right argument is. So the rung-3 probe's CR extraction NEVER worked (sxip/psr None on every row) — as I suspected. On the HALT rows this didn't matter (verdict from transcript), but the POST-FIX detector asserts SXIP/PSR — the shipped probe must fix the `reg` invocation (`reg 0,1`? verify debugger syntax: debugger command "reg" in GXemul: `reg [cpu] [g][,c]`? Let me check debugger command. Actually in GXemul debugger: `reg [cpunr] [gprs],[coprocs]`? or `reg [g],[c]`? The m88k register dump signature: m88k_cpu_register_dump(cpu, gprs, coprocs) where coprocs bit 0 = CPU control regs (from the code: `if (coprocs & 1)` around line 296 (the loop at 300-306 is under `coprocs & 1`), `coprocs & 2` = FPU control regs). So the probe needs `reg 0,1`? or `reg ,1`? Debugger syntax check needed: debugger.c "reg" command. Quick grep.
regress\artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass1\panel_20260905_143211\kimi.txt:1486:  Also I could rerun the whole probe: `wsl -e bash -lc 'cd /mnt/c/.../ && python3 _scratchpad/m88kexc/m88k_exc_probe.py ./build/gxemul'` — that reproduces the witness (rung 3 rerun by a second seat = strengthens). The probe writes to stdout only. ~5 rows × a few seconds = under a minute. Worth doing — it directly verifies the witness claim AND shows the reg-dump behavior. Note: probe BIN arg — pass ./build/gxemul (the today's fork build; contains slot_runs). The brief said the witness was against "committed build/gxemul (HEAD, rebuilt 14:17)" — this ./build/gxemul (14:54) is presumably that (or a slightly later rebuild; HEAD hasn't moved: ab9ae13 14:15). Verify quickly that build/gxemul matches HEAD: it contains today's build; GXEMUL-SEC/gxemul (Aug 13) is stale. Fine.
