or\n(b) the witness is fabricated/wrong.\n\nHold on \u2014 actually, wait. Let me look more carefully. In real gxemul, there is indeed a `fatal()` that exits. But hmm, is there also a non-exiting print? There's `debug()`. There's also... Let me recall the actual gxemul code.\n\nFrom gxemul `src/misc/misc.c` (version 0.6.x / 0.7.x / 0.9.x... the 2021 pristine copies mention \"pristine-2021\"):\n\n```c\n/*\n *  fatal():\n *  ...\n */\nvoid fatal(const char *fmt, ...)\n{\n\tva_list ap;\n\n\tva_start(ap, fmt);\n\tvfprintf(stderr, fmt, ap);\n\tva_end(ap);\n\n\texit(1);\n}\n```\n\nYes. I'm quite confident. `fatal()` = print to stderr + exit(1). This is a standard pattern in gxemul. For example, `dev_pcc2.c` or `machine.c` uses fatal for unrecoverable config errors.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 the \"house `(once)` shape\" \u2014 the comment references `pcc2pscaleflood` where \"PSCALEADJ's UNLATCHED print four arms up\". So the house shape is: latch a bit, then call fatal() (once). If fatal() exits, calling it once per offset still kills the host on the first declined write.\n\nActually hold on. Maybe in this fork, they redefined fatal? Hmm, but the packet says \"the house `(once)` shape\" as if there's an established pattern in the fork of latched fatal() calls that don't kill. Hmm, that seems odd because \"fatal\" that doesn't kill isn't fatal.\n\nWait, wait. Let me re-read: \"One latched fatal() per offset per instance, the house `(once)` shape\". Hmm. If the house shape were \"latched debug()\", that would make sense. But \"latched fatal()\"?\n\nHmm, unless... in this fork they've introduced a distinction: `fatal()` for operator-visible messages that don't exit? That would be a huge semantic change that would break every other fatal() call site in the emulator (all the places that rely on fatal exiting).\n\nAlternatively \u2014 maybe I'm wrong about gxemul's fatal(). Let me think about actual gxemul source. I've read gxemul source (Anders Gavare's emulator). Let me recall `src/misc/misc.c`:\n\n```c\n/****************************************************************************/\n\n\n/*\n *  fatal():\n *\n *  A fatal error message. This is usually due to a program error, or a\n *  user configuration error.\n *\n *  The error message is printed to stderr, and the program aborts.\n */\nvoid fatal(const char *fmt, ...)\n{\n\tva_list ap;\n\n\tva_start(ap, fmt);\n\tvfprintf(stderr, fmt, ap);\n\tva_end(ap);\n\n\tfprintf(stderr, \"\\n\");\n\texit(1);\n}\n```\n\nYes \u2014 I'm confident: fatal() prints to stderr and exits with status 1. The docstring literally says \"the program aborts\".\n\nSo the diff as shown calls `fatal()` on a declined write \u2014 which would END THE EMULATOR PROCESS. That is: the \"fix\" replaces `exit(1)` with `fatal(...)`, which itself calls exit(1). The witness's post-fix \"survived\" rows would then be impossible... unless the fork changed fatal() semantics.\n\nHmm, but hold on \u2014 the witness claims: \"ieicr st.b survived\" post-fix, and \"ieicr st.b msg 1\" \u2014 the message printed. If fatal() exits, host dies, contradicts \"survived\". So there's an internal contradiction between the diff and the witness UNLESS fatal() in this fork is non-exiting.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the fork has TWO functions: fatal() that exits, and something else. Or maybe the fork's house convention is that `fatal()` in device access... no.\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 wait. Let me reconsider whether gxemul's fatal() really exits. Alternative memory: some emulators define fatal() as print-and-exit; gxemul... Let me think of a specific usage. In `machine.c`: `fatal(\"unknown machine type\\n\")`? In `cpu_emul...` hmm. In `dev_ram.c`? \n\nI recall from gxemul source, e.g. `src/machines/machine.c`:\n```c\nfatal(\"Unknown machine type '%s'\\n\", machine_name);\n```\nand the emulator then... well, if it didn't exit, execution would continue into nonsense. I'm fairly sure fatal exits.\n\nAlso `src/devices/dev_dec21143.c` or similar: `fatal(\"dev_dec21143: UNIMPLEMENTED...\\n\")` \u2014 hmm, actually in some device code gxemul uses `fatal()` for unimplemented accesses, and running into them kills the emulator with an error. Yes \u2014 that's the classic behavior: gxemul dies with \"fatal\" when a device access hits an unimplemented path.\n\nOK here's the thing though. This is a fictional/fork scenario. The packet says the \"house `(once)` shape\" \u2014 implying the fork has a convention of latched announces. The earlier pcc2widthkills work added `warned` bits with `debug()` presumably. The comment says \"unlike PSCALEADJ's UNLATCHED print four arms up (pcc2pscaleflood)\". So there's a `pcc2pscaleflood` issue where PSCALEADJ printed un-latched (debug flood). The house fix shape is \"(once)\".\n\nHmm, so does the fork's fatal() exit? The packet gives evidence: the witness post-fix shows the host SURVIVES a declined write with the message printed. So within the packet's own facts, fatal() (as used in the diff, on this fork) prints without exiting. But the diff ALSO must be consistent with the committed base where `exit(1)` was the kill mechanism. If fatal() exits, the fix does nothing for writes (still host kill), and the witness post-fix would show host exit1. The witness says survived. So the packet's own facts require fatal() to be non-exiting.\n\nBut is that plausible for gxemul? In stock gxemul, fatal() exits. If the fork didn't change fatal(), the diff is WRONG \u2014 it replaces one exit(1) with another exit(1) (via fatal), and the witness numbers are then fabricated. This is a big deal.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider once more. Maybe I'm misremembering and gxemul's fatal() is just a non-exiting stderr print, with exit done separately. Let me try to recall `main.c`... In gxemul `src/main/main.c` I don't recall fatal. In `src/misc/misc.c`:\n\nActually here's a concrete memory: gxemul's `debug()` writes to stdout only if `verbose >= 1`... and `fatal()` \u2014 hmm.\n\nLet me think about