167:
168:	/*  #508: was just `PFN << pageSizeShift' -- a PAGE BASE.  memory_rw then takes
169:	    `offset = paddr & offset_mask', which was therefore always zero, so every
170:	    walked access FILLED THE TRANSLATION FROM the page's first bytes.  ("every
171:	    walked access resolved to" until 2026-09-07: a pass-2 seat noted that the
172:	    Alpha FAST PATH recomputes `addr & 8191' itself, so a later cache HIT reads
173:	    the right bytes.  The corruption is the SLOW-PATH FILL, which is also why a
174:	    watchpoint -- which keeps a page off the fast store table -- makes it
175:	    reproducible.)  Measured: a cold guest ldq
176:	    of vaddr+8 returned the vaddr+0 value.  A debugger watchpoint on a walked page
177:	    landed at the base for the same reason.
178:	
179:	    The mask is derived from pageSizeShift rather than written as 0x1fff: this
180:	    function already has TWO names for 13 (ALPHA_PAGESHIFT and this local), and a
181:	    literal beside the shift is how the two drift.  `|' rather than `+' because
182:	    that is what every other CPU's walk in this tree writes; here they are equal,
183:	    the shifted PFN having bits 12:0 clear.  The TODO below STAYS: `flags' is
184:	    still ignored, the protection bits untested, and `return 2' still reports
185:	    every walked page writable.  */
186:	(*return_paddr) = (ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift)
187:	    | (vaddr & ((1ULL << pageSizeShift) - 1));
188:
189:	// TODO.
190:	return 2;
