1:#!/usr/bin/env python3
2:#
3:#  lcairqstubkill -- RUNG-3 WITNESS: ONE guest store to the 8042 command port ends the host.
4:#
5:#  THE DEFECT.  `dev_lca.c' registers the machine's interrupt handlers and they are bare kills:
6:#
7:#      void lca_interrupt_assert(struct interrupt *interrupt)
8:#      {
9:#              fatal("lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\n");
10:#              exit(1);
11:#      }                                        /*  and _deassert, identically  */
12:#
13:#  `DEVINIT(lca)' registers them for "<machine>.lca" and hands that SAME path to `bus_isa_init',
14:#  so every ISA interrupt is routed into a function whose whole body is fatal() + exit(1).
15:#
16:#  THE CHAIN, every link read in the source and then MEASURED by this probe:
17:#
18:#    1.  a guest store to the 8042 COMMAND port queues a reply; `dev_pckbc_access' ends by
19:#        calling `pckbc_reassert_interrupts'                            -- SYNCHRONOUS, in the
20:#                                                                          guest store itself
21:#    2.  that asserts ISA line 1; `isa_interrupt_assert' sets the PIC IRR bit
22:#    3.  `isa_interrupt_common': new = pic1->irr & ~pic1->ier.  `dev_8259.c' memsets the struct
23:#        and `ier' is written ONLY by a guest store, so AT RESET NOTHING IS MASKED and the first
24:#        IRR bit propagates upward on d->irq
25:#    4.  d->irq came from INTERRUPT_CONNECT(interrupt_base_path,...) = "<machine>.lca"
26:#    5.  lca_interrupt_assert(): fatal(); exit(1)
27:#
28:#  **Four guest instructions total.  No disk image, no OS, no host timer, no driver setup, and no
29:#  unusual value -- 0xAA is the standard 8042 self-test command.**
30:#
31:#  ROUTE CHOSEN FROM A codex ISA CENSUS (regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill/codex_reach_20260907.txt,
32:#  denominator: 12 instantiation call sites in bus_isa_init).  Two of its findings decided the
33:#  shape, and both corrected a premise of mine:
34:#    *  **the WDC controllers exist only if an IDE image is attached**, so the IDE route this row
35:#       was originally written around is NOT available to a probe that attaches a memory file;
36:#    *  **PIT and RTC asserts are HOST-TIMER driven** and therefore invisible to a stepped probe,
37:#       while PCKBC and the UARTs are synchronous with the guest access.
38:#
39:#  THE MATCHED CONTROL IS THE LPT PORT, and the census is why it is the right one: `dev_lpt_tick'
40:#  is EMPTY and `dev_lpt_access' never asserts an IRQ.  So a store of the same width, through the
41:#  same K0SEG path, into the same ISA window, at a port that cannot assert, MUST SURVIVE.  That is
42:#  what attributes the death to the DEVICE REACHED rather than to storing into ISA space at all.
43:#
44:#  ONE PROCESS, control first: only the PORT differs between the two stores.
45:#
46:#  *** CONTRACT -- WHAT THIS WITNESSES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. ***
47:#  It establishes that on unmodified `alphabook1' a single ordinary guest store to a documented
48:#  8042 register ends the host process, with the terminating stub's own diagnostic as evidence.
49:#  It does NOT establish that any shipped guest issues that command.
50:#
51:#  *** THE SECOND HALF OF THIS CONTRACT WAS DISCHARGED BY #516 ON 2026-09-09, AND THE SENTENCE IS
52:#  CORRECTED RATHER THAN DELETED so a reader can see what the round owed. ***  It read: "it is NOT
53:#  a detector of a repair: 'host survives' would green for a silent return, for a latched announce,
54:#  and for an actual interrupt implementation alike.  A fix round owes a row that discriminates
55:#  those."  That row is now `irq announce' + `irq honest', and their siblings cover the second stub.
56:#
57:#  WHAT IT NOW COVERS, stated so a later escape is either inside this claim or outside it:
58:#    * BOTH stubs -- assert and deassert.  The deassert edge is driven by [C], the load that drains
59:#      the 8042's reply; before that no witness in this tree reached the second stub AT ALL, and a
60:#      verbatim reinstatement of its exit(1) scored 6/6 on the shipped detector (MEASURED, M2d).
61:#    * the LATCH, by [D] -- a second full assert/deassert cycle, so an announce that repeats reds.
62:#    * *** the ASSERT GUARD AGAINST A SAME-POLARITY REPEAT, by [E]/[F] -- a SECOND ISA line (the
63:#      ns16550 on IRQ 4) raised and dropped while the 8042's line is still pending, so the aggregate
64:#      changes 0x02 -> 0x12 -> 0x02 and the assert stub is entered THREE TIMES before any deassert.
65:#      Added 2026-09-09 after the pass-2 panel's own mutant (swap the latch bit in the ASSERT guard
66:#      only) swept the eleven-row instrument 11/11; this sentence is what the contract lacked until
67:#      the pass-3 review seat noticed the contract still described the eleven-row instrument. ***
68:#    * the HONESTY of each announce, anchored to its OWN site's full text.
69:#  MEASURED against this instrument (killtable_20260909/TABLE_G.out + TABLE_H.out, one instrument,
70:#  byte-identical frozen copies): 2 sites x 8 edits = 16 cells, 14 killed, 2 EQUIVALENT with their
71:#  derivations recorded (`|=' -> `=' on the assert arm; `d != NULL' removed -- the only registration
72:#  of these handlers is dev_lca.c's own interrupt_template, so `extra' is never NULL), plus the null
73:#  medium and two composites.  The two assignment-bit-swap cells were built LAST, after four pass-3
74:#  seats found them unswept in the stated denominator -- "sixteen mutants cover sixteen cells" had
75:#  counted two composites as cells.
76:#  WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT COVER, disclosed under clause (e):
77:#    * a BASE-RUN pin on the second line's assert EDGE.  `uart live' proves the MCR store landed, not
78:#      that the aggregate changed; on the fix the latched announce prints once whether or not [E]
79:#      re-entered the stub, so no row here reds if the UART silently stopped asserting -- and M12
80:#      would then escape again with nothing saying why.  The evidence the edges WERE live at
81:#      measurement time is M12's own `assert once' = 3 (TABLE_G.out).  A green-run pin needs a
82:#      different channel (the PIC1 IRR read back as 0x12 after [E]); filed as `lcairqedgepin'.
83:#    * the readback at [G] is an 8-byte LDQ of a byte-wide register.  It MEASURED 0x8 (memory_rw
84:#      clamps the length to the device's), and the row is green on every base run, but the tidy form
85:#      is a byte load (BWX LDBU, opcode 0x0a); raised by a pass-3 seat, disclosed, not changed --
86:#      changing the instrument would owe another table for a row that already works.
87:#    * interrupt DELIVERY.  Nothing here can observe delivery, because Alpha has none to observe --
88:#      that is the defect the announce reports, not an instrument limitation.
89:#    * the three OTHER routes to the deassert edge (a PIC1 specific EOI to ISA 0x20; an IMR write
90:#      to ISA 0x21 masking IRQ 1 or everything).  *** REPORTED reachable in one guest access each
91:#      by the pass-1 measuring seat; THE WORD HERE WAS "MEASURED" UNTIL 2026-09-09 AND NO ARTIFACT
92:#      ENUMERATING THOSE RUNS WAS PRESERVED -- only the 8042 route has raws.  A score claim names
93:#      the log that produced it, and these have none, so the claim is downgraded to its true
94:#      strength rather than left wearing a measurement's clothes. ***  The no-row conclusion does
95:#      NOT rest on it: it rests on the mutation-indistinguishability argument below, which is
96:#      source-derived and needs no reachability figure at all.  No
97:#      row is owed for them: all four routes land in the same function with the same
98:#      interrupt->extra, so no dev_lca.c mutant can distinguish them and a second row would be
99:#      vacuous by construction.  A PLAIN EOI (0x20 -> 0x20) does NOT reach it -- `d->isr' is never
100:#      set in dev_8259.c, so `irr &= ~isr' is a no-op -- which is worth recording because it is the
101:#      route a reader would reach for first.
102:#
103:#  Usage:  lca_irqstub_probe.py <gxemul-binary> [label]
104:import os, sys, pty, select, time, re
105:
106:BIN   = sys.argv[1]
107:LABEL = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else "?"
108:MACH  = "alphabook1"          #  device_add(machine, "lca") is ST_ALPHABOOK1 ONLY
109:
110:rows = []
111:def row(name, got, want, note=""):
112:    ok = (got == want)
113:    rows.append((name, ok))
114:    print("%-14s %-24s %-24s %-52s %s" % (name, str(got), str(want), note, "ok" if ok else "FAIL"))
115:    return ok
116:
117:# ---------------------------------------------------------------- encoder (memory format only)
118:ZERO, T0, T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6, T7 = 31, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8
119:#  #516/M12: T4..T7 added for the SECOND ISA line.  The shipped set stopped at T3, and a probe
120:#  reaching for an undefined T4 dies with a NameError rather than emitting a bad encoding --
121:#  which is the safe direction, and is how the measuring seat found it.
122:
123:def mem(op, ra, rb, disp):
124:    assert -32768 <= disp <= 32767, "displacement %d out of range" % disp
125:    return (op << 26) | (ra << 21) | (rb << 16) | (disp & 0xffff)
126:
127:def LDA (ra, disp, rb=ZERO): return mem(0x08, ra, rb, disp)
128:def LDAH(ra, disp, rb=ZERO): return mem(0x09, ra, rb, disp)
129:def LDQ (ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x29, ra, rb, disp)
130:def STQ (ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x2d, ra, rb, disp)
131:def STB (ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x0e, ra, rb, disp)   # BWX; ungated in this tree
132:#  The UART registers are BYTE-wide, so the second line needs STB.  NOT a new capability:
133:#  regress/lca_irqstub_uart_probe.py already drives this exact device with STB on this exact
134:#  machine, and cpu_alpha_instr.c decodes opcode 0x0e with no BWX gate.
135:
136:def load_const(reg, val):
137:    #  32-bit only, and deliberately so: this builds the SCRATCH pointer, never a 64-bit operand.
138:    lo = val & 0xffff
139:    hi = (val >> 16) & 0xffff
140:    if lo >= 0x8000:
141:        hi = (hi + 1) & 0xffff
142:    ops = [LDAH(reg, hi if hi < 0x8000 else hi - 0x10000)]
143:    if lo:
144:        ops.append(LDA(reg, lo if lo < 0x8000 else lo - 0x10000, reg))
145:    return ops
146:
147:# ---------------------------------------------------------------- addresses (ALL RAM < 64 MB)
148:K0SEG        = 0xfffffc0000000000
149:def k0(p):   return K0SEG | p
150:
151:LCA_IOC_BASE = 0x180000000
152:LCA_IOC_CONF = LCA_IOC_BASE + 0x020      # alpha_lcareg.h
153:LCA_PCI_CONF = 0x1e0000000               # alpha_lcareg.h; DEVINIT maps 0x20000000 bytes here
154:SCRATCH      = 0x01020000
155:CODE         = 0x01040000
156:LEGAL        = 0x1                       # `idata & ~1' == 0 -> ACCEPTED by the CONF guard
157:
158:assert max(SCRATCH, CODE) < 64 * 1024 * 1024, "scratch/code must be inside RAM"
159:
160:DUMMY = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "alpha_dummy.bin")
161:if not os.path.exists(DUMMY):
162:    with open(DUMMY, "wb") as f:
163:        f.write(b"\0" * 8)
164:
165:# ---------------------------------------------------------------- session
166:def session(cmds, settle=1.6, spin=2.2):
167:    pid, fd = pty.fork()
168:    if pid == 0:
169:        os.execv(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-x", "-E", "alpha", "-e", MACH, "-M", "64",
170:                       "0x20000000:" + DUMMY])
171:        os._exit(127)
172:    buf = ""
173:    def rd(t):
174:        nonlocal buf
175:        end = time.time() + t
176:        while time.time() < end:
177:            r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], 0.1)
178:            if not r:
179:                continue
180:            try:
181:                d = os.read(fd, 65536)
182:                if not d:
183:                    return False
184:                buf += d.decode("latin-1", "replace")
185:            except OSError:
186:                return False
187:        return True
188:    alive = rd(settle)
189:    for i, c in enumerate(cmds):
190:        try:
191:            os.write(fd, (c + "\n").encode("latin-1"))
192:        except OSError:
193:            alive = False; break
194:        if not rd(spin if i == len(cmds) - 1 else 0.35):
195:            alive = False; break
196:    try:
197:        os.write(fd, b"quit\n"); rd(0.4)
198:    except OSError:
199:        pass
200:    try:
201:        os.close(fd)
202:    except OSError:
203:        pass
204:    return buf.replace("\r", ""), alive
205:
206:def printed(t, reg):
207:    m = re.search(r"print\s+%s\s*\n\s*(0x[0-9a-f]+)" % reg, t)
208:    return "absent" if not m else "0x%x" % int(m.group(1), 16)
209:
210:print("lca_irqstub_probe -- binary=%s label=%s machine=%s" % (BIN, LABEL, MACH))
211:print("%-14s %-24s %-24s %-52s %s" % ("row", "got", "want", "note", ""))
212:
213:#  ---- ISA port addresses, derived: B = LCA_ISA_BASE = LCA_PCI_SIO + 0x10000000 ----------------
214:ISA_BASE = 0x1d0000000
215:KBC_CMD  = ISA_BASE + 0x64        # 8042 command port; IRQ 1
216:LPT_DATA = ISA_BASE + 0x378       # LPT; dev_lpt_tick is EMPTY -- it never asserts
217:KBC_SELFTEST = 0xAA               # standard 8042 self-test: queues a reply
218:KBC_DATA = ISA_BASE + 0x60        # 8042 DATA port; a load DRAINS the queued reply
219:KBC_REPLY = 0x55                  # what `case 0xaa:' queues (dev_pckbc.c)
220:UART      = ISA_BASE + 0x3f8      # ns16550 tty0 on IRQ 4 -- THE SECOND ISA LINE
221:UART_IER  = UART + 1
222:UART_MCR  = UART + 4
223:IER_ETXRDY, MCR_IENABLE = 0x02, 0x08
224:#  *** THE UART ASSERTS SYNCHRONOUSLY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THESE ROWS CAN EXIST. ***
225:#  DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550) calls dev_ns16550_tick(cpu, d) INLINE at four sites, and the assert
226:#  lives in that tick behind `d->reg[com_mcr] & MCR_IENABLE'.  So it is NOT a host-timer route
227:#  and a STEPPED probe can drive it -- unlike the PIT and the RTC, whose asserts are
228:#  timer-driven and therefore invisible here.  bus_isa_init installs it UNCONDITIONALLY: no
229:#  IDE image, no device_add, no machine modification, which is what keeps this a witness
230:#  rather than a laundered one.

